window.STUDY_CHAPTERS = [{"id": 1, "title": "Financial Statements and Business Decisions", "topics": [{"id": "c1-core", "title": "Core equations & debit and credit rules", "body": "Before the chapters, memorize these relationships:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Assets=Liabilities+Shareholders' Equity}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Net\\ Earnings=Revenues-Expenses}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Ending\\ Retained\\ Earnings\n=Beginning\\ Retained\\ Earnings+Net\\ Earnings-Dividends}\n\\]\n\n### Debit and credit master rule\n\n| Account | Increase | Decrease | Normal balance |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Assets | Debit | Credit | Debit |\n| Liabilities | Credit | Debit | Credit |\n| Shareholders' equity | Credit | Debit | Credit |\n| Revenues/gains | Credit | Debit | Credit |\n| Expenses/losses | Debit | Credit | Debit |\n| Dividends | Debit | Credit | Debit |\n\nA transaction always affects **at least two accounts**, and:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Total\\ Debits=Total\\ Credits}\n\\]", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t1", "title": "Purpose of accounting", "body": "Accounting provides information that helps:\n\n- Investors\n- Creditors/lenders\n- Managers\n- Other financial statement users\n\nmake economic decisions.\n\nAccounting does more than record transactions. It communicates the company's:\n\n- Financial position\n- Financial performance\n- Changes in equity\n- Cash flows", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t2", "title": "Four basic financial statements", "body": "## Statement of financial position\n\nAlso sometimes called the **balance sheet**.\n\nReports financial position at a **specific date**.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Assets=Liabilities+Shareholders' Equity}\n\\]\n\nIt answers:\n\n> What resources does the company control, and who financed those resources?\n\n### Assets\n\nEconomic resources controlled by the entity because of past events.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Cash\n- Accounts receivable\n- Inventory\n- Equipment\n- Investments\n\n### Liabilities\n\nDebts or obligations.\n\nThey represent financing from **creditors**.\n\n### Shareholders' equity\n\nThe residual ownership interest.\n\nTwo major sources:\n\n**Contributed capital**\n- Amounts shareholders invest.\n\n**Retained earnings**\n- Accumulated earnings not distributed as dividends.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t3", "title": "Statement of financial position format", "body": "The heading normally contains:\n\n- Company name\n- Statement name\n- Specific date\n- Monetary unit\n\nCanadian companies commonly list more liquid assets first.\n\n### Liquidity\n\nHow quickly an asset can be converted into cash.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t4", "title": "Statement of earnings", "body": "Reports financial performance **over a period of time**.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Net\\ Earnings=Revenues-Expenses}\n\\]\n\n### Revenues\n\nAmounts earned through sales of goods or services.\n\nRevenue can exist even if the customer has not yet paid.\n\n### Expenses\n\nResources consumed while generating revenues.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Salaries\n- Rent\n- Utilities\n- Cost of sales\n\n### Net earnings\n\nIf:\n\n\\[\nRevenue>Expenses\n\\]\n\nthere are net earnings.\n\nIf:\n\n\\[\nExpenses>Revenue\n\\]\n\nthere is a net loss.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t5", "title": "Statement of changes in equity", "body": "Explains changes in shareholders' equity during the period.\n\nA major relationship is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nBeginning\\ RE+Net\\ Earnings-Dividends=Ending\\ RE\n}\n\\]\n\n### Important\n\n**Dividends are not expenses.**\n\nThey are distributions of accumulated earnings to shareholders.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t6", "title": "Statement of cash flows", "body": "Explains cash inflows and outflows.\n\nThree categories:\n\n### Operating activities — CFO\n\nCash associated with normal operations and generating earnings.\n\n### Investing activities — CFI\n\nCash associated with acquiring or disposing of:\n\n- Property\n- Plant\n- Equipment\n- Investments\n\n### Financing activities — CFF\n\nCash associated with:\n\n- Borrowing\n- Repaying principal\n- Issuing shares\n- Shareholder financing\n\n\\[\nCFO+CFI+CFF=Change\\ in\\ Cash\n\\]\n\nThen:\n\n\\[\nBeginning\\ Cash+Change\\ in\\ Cash=Ending\\ Cash\n\\]", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t7", "title": "Relationships among the statements", "body": "The statements are interconnected.\n\n### Statement of earnings\n\nProduces:\n\n\\[\nNet\\ Earnings\n\\]\n\n↓\n\n### Statement of changes in equity\n\nNet earnings increase retained earnings.\n\n↓\n\n### Statement of financial position\n\nEnding retained earnings becomes part of shareholders' equity.\n\nThe statement of cash flows explains the change in the cash account appearing on the statement of financial position.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t8", "title": "Net earnings is not the same as cash flow", "body": "This is a major concept throughout the course.\n\nA company might:\n\n- Earn revenue before collecting cash.\n- Incur an expense before paying cash.\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Net\\ Earnings\\neq Net\\ Change\\ in\\ Cash}\n\\]\n\nin most situations.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t9", "title": "Notes to the financial statements", "body": "Notes provide information needed to properly understand the statements.\n\nThey may explain:\n\n- Accounting policies\n- Additional details about financial statement accounts\n- Information not separately presented on the statements", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t10", "title": "Canadian accounting standards", "body": "## Accounting Standards Board — AcSB\n\nThe Canadian private-sector accounting standard setter described by the slides.\n\nThe standards are published through the **CPA Canada Handbook**.\n\n## IFRS\n\nCanadian publicly accountable enterprises prepare statements using:\n\n**International Financial Reporting Standards**\n\nIFRS are developed by the:\n\n**International Accounting Standards Board — IASB**", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t11", "title": "Ethics", "body": "Intentional financial statement misreporting is both:\n\n- Unethical\n- Illegal\n\nFive professional ethical principles highlighted in the slides:\n\n1. Integrity\n2. Objectivity\n3. Professional competence and due care\n4. Confidentiality\n5. Professional behaviour", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t12", "title": "Management responsibility and internal control", "body": "Management has primary responsibility for the company's financial information.\n\nCompanies use:\n\n- Internal controls\n- Independent auditors\n- Board/audit committee oversight\n\nto improve the reliability of financial reporting.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t13", "title": "Independent auditors", "body": "Auditors:\n\n- Examine financial reports.\n- Examine accounting methods and underlying transactions.\n- Determine whether the statements fairly represent the company according to applicable accounting standards.\n\nImportant:\n\n> Management prepares and takes responsibility for the statements. Auditors independently examine them.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t14", "title": "Business entities", "body": "| Form | Ownership | Separate legal entity? | Liability |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sole proprietorship | One owner | No | Unlimited |\n| Partnership | Two or more | No | Unlimited |\n| Corporation | Shareholders | Yes | Generally limited to investment |\n\nAll are still treated as separate **accounting entities**.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c1-t15", "title": "Accounting careers", "body": "The slides introduce employment in:\n\n- Public accounting\n- Businesses\n- Government\n- Not-for-profit organizations\n\nServices can include:\n\n- Audit/assurance\n- Tax\n- Management consulting\n- Financial reporting\n- Internal control", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}]}, {"id": 2, "title": "Investing and Financing Decisions and the Accounting System", "topics": [{"id": "c2-t1", "title": "Objective of financial reporting", "body": "Provide financial information useful to:\n\n- Existing investors\n- Potential investors\n- Lenders\n- Other creditors\n\nwhen deciding whether to provide resources to an entity.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t2", "title": "Fundamental qualitative characteristics", "body": "## Relevance\n\nInformation can influence a decision.\n\nIncludes:\n\n- Predictive value\n- Confirmatory/feedback value\n\n## Faithful representation\n\nInformation should be:\n\n- Complete\n- Neutral\n- Free from error or bias", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t3", "title": "Enhancing qualitative characteristics", "body": "### Comparability\n\nAllows comparison between entities or periods.\n\n### Verifiability\n\nIndependent knowledgeable people can reach similar conclusions.\n\n### Timeliness\n\nInformation arrives before losing its usefulness.\n\n### Understandability\n\nReasonably informed users can understand its significance.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t4", "title": "Cost constraint", "body": "Accounting information should generally be provided when:\n\n\\[\nBenefits>Costs\n\\]", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t5", "title": "Accounting assumptions", "body": "## Separate entity\n\nBusiness transactions are kept separate from owners' personal activities.\n\n## Stable monetary unit\n\nCanadian financial information is measured in dollars without continuously adjusting for inflation.\n\n## Going concern / continuity\n\nAssumes the entity will continue operating into the foreseeable future.\n\n## Periodicity\n\nBusiness activities can be divided into reporting periods.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t6", "title": "Historical cost", "body": "Assets are initially recorded based on the cash-equivalent cost given up in the transaction.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t7", "title": "Classified statement of financial position", "body": "Assets and liabilities are separated into:\n\n- Current\n- Non-current\n\n## Current assets\n\nNormally used or converted into cash within approximately one year.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Cash\n- Short-term investments\n- Accounts receivable\n- Inventory\n- Prepaid expenses\n\n## Non-current assets\n\nExamples:\n\n- Property, plant and equipment\n- Long-term financial assets\n- Intangible assets\n- Goodwill", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t8", "title": "Liabilities", "body": "## Current liabilities\n\nExpected to be settled within the coming year.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Accounts payable\n- Short-term borrowings\n- Income taxes payable\n- Accrued liabilities\n- Provisions\n\n## Non-current liabilities\n\nLonger-term obligations.\n\nExample:\n\n- Long-term debt", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t9", "title": "Shareholders' equity", "body": "Two major components:\n\n### Contributed capital\n\nFinancing provided directly by shareholders.\n\n### Retained earnings\n\nAccumulated earnings retained in the company.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t10", "title": "ESG reporting", "body": "The slides introduce environmental, social and governance matters because potential environmental liabilities can affect:\n\n- Future cash flows\n- Financial position\n- Investor decisions", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t11", "title": "Business transactions", "body": "A **transaction** is an event recorded by the accounting system because it has an economic effect.\n\n### External events\n\nTransactions with outside parties.\n\n### Internal events\n\nEvents inside the company that still have a measurable accounting effect.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t12", "title": "Accounts", "body": "An account accumulates the monetary effects of transactions for a particular financial statement item.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Cash\n- Inventory\n- Accounts payable\n- Common shares", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t13", "title": "Chart of accounts", "body": "Organized list of account names and numbers.\n\nGeneral order:\n\n1. Assets\n2. Liabilities\n3. Shareholders' equity\n4. Revenues\n5. Expenses", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t14", "title": "Dual effects", "body": "Every transaction has at least two effects.\n\nThe equation must remain balanced:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{A=L+SE}\n\\]", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t15", "title": "Transaction analysis", "body": "Use three steps.\n\n### Step 1\n\nWhat was received?\n\n### Step 2\n\nWhat was given?\n\n### Step 3\n\nVerify:\n\n\\[\nA=L+SE\n\\]", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t16", "title": "Examples", "body": "## Issue shares for cash\n\nCash:\n\n\\[\n+A\n\\]\n\nContributed capital:\n\n\\[\n+SE\n\\]\n\n## Borrow from bank\n\nCash:\n\n\\[\n+A\n\\]\n\nDebt:\n\n\\[\n+L\n\\]\n\n## Buy equipment using cash and debt\n\nEquipment increases.\n\nCash decreases.\n\nLiability increases for unpaid amount.\n\n## Pay dividend\n\nCash:\n\n\\[\n-A\n\\]\n\nRetained earnings:\n\n\\[\n-SE\n\\]\n\nAgain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Dividend\\neq Expense}\n\\]", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t17", "title": "Journal entries", "body": "Journal entries record debit and credit effects.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Debits=Credits}\n\\]", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t18", "title": "T-accounts", "body": "Left:\n\n\\[\nDebit\n\\]\n\nRight:\n\n\\[\nCredit\n\\]\n\nAssets generally increase with debits.\n\nLiabilities and shareholders' equity generally increase with credits.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t19", "title": "Posting", "body": "Journal entries are transferred to individual ledger accounts.\n\nThis determines account balances.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t20", "title": "Trial balance", "body": "Lists:\n\n- Account names\n- Debit balances\n- Credit balances\n\nUsed to verify:\n\n\\[\nTotal\\ Debits=Total\\ Credits\n\\]\n\nA balanced trial balance does **not** guarantee every accounting entry is correct.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t21", "title": "Current ratio", "body": "Measures short-term liquidity.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nCurrent\\ Ratio=\n\\frac{Current\\ Assets}{Current\\ Liabilities}\n}\n\\]\n\nHigher ratios generally indicate more current resources relative to current obligations, although ratios need appropriate comparison.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t22", "title": "Data analytics", "body": "Accounting increasingly uses large quantities of data to identify:\n\n- Patterns\n- Trends\n- Relationships\n- Unusual transactions", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c2-t23", "title": "Accounting vs bookkeeping", "body": "**Bookkeeping** focuses on routine recording.\n\n**Accounting** includes:\n\n- Designing systems\n- Analyzing transactions\n- Applying judgement\n- Interpreting financial data", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}]}, {"id": 3, "title": "Operating Decisions and the Accounting System", "topics": [{"id": "c3-t1", "title": "Operating cycle", "body": "Also called the **cash-to-cash cycle**.\n\nTime required to:\n\n1. Pay suppliers\n2. Sell goods/services\n3. Collect cash from customers", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t2", "title": "Periodicity assumption", "body": "Financial information is reported for relatively short periods:\n\n- Monthly\n- Quarterly\n- Annually\n\nThis creates two questions:\n\n### Recognition\n\nWhen should something be recorded?\n\n### Measurement\n\nWhat amount should be recorded?", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t3", "title": "Classified statement of earnings", "body": "Basic format:\n\n\\[\nNet\\ Sales\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-Cost\\ of\\ Sales\n\\]\n\n\\[\n=\\boxed{Gross\\ Profit}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-Operating\\ Expenses\n\\]\n\n\\[\n=\\boxed{Earnings\\ from\\ Operations}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n+/-Nonoperating\\ Items\n\\]\n\n\\[\n=Earnings\\ Before\\ Income\\ Taxes\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-Income\\ Tax\\ Expense\n\\]\n\n\\[\n=Earnings\\ from\\ Continuing\\ Operations\n\\]\n\n\\[\n+/-Discontinued\\ Operations\n\\]\n\n\\[\n=\\boxed{Net\\ Earnings}\n\\]", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t4", "title": "Operating expenses", "body": "Examples:\n\n### Cost of sales\n\nCost of products sold.\n\n### Selling and distribution expenses\n\nCosts related to sales activities.\n\n### Administrative expenses\n\nAdministrative salaries, legal, accounting, technology, travel, etc.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t5", "title": "Non-operating items", "body": "Examples:\n\n- Interest income\n- Financing costs\n- Investment gains/losses", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t6", "title": "Income taxes", "body": "Income tax expense follows earnings before income taxes.\n\nCorporations may owe:\n\n- Federal\n- Provincial\n- Foreign\n\nincome taxes depending on operations.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t7", "title": "Discontinued operations", "body": "Major operations or geographic components that have been or will be discontinued are separated because they are less useful for predicting recurring future earnings.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t8", "title": "Earnings per share", "body": "Simplified:\n\n\\[\nEPS=\n\\frac{Net\\ Earnings}{Average\\ Shares\\ Outstanding}\n\\]\n\nLater chapters refine this.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t9", "title": "Cash-basis accounting", "body": "Revenue recorded when cash is received.\n\nExpense recorded when cash is paid.\n\nIt does not properly reflect financial performance for external reporting.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t10", "title": "Accrual accounting", "body": "Revenue recognized when:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{EARNED}\n\\]\n\nExpense recognized when:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{INCURRED}\n\\]\n\nCash timing is secondary.\n\nIFRS financial reporting uses accrual accounting.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t11", "title": "Revenue recognition principle", "body": "Revenue is recognized when promised goods/services are transferred to customers for the amount the business expects to be entitled to receive.\n\n### Five steps\n\n1. Identify the contract.\n2. Identify performance obligations.\n3. Determine transaction price.\n4. Allocate price to performance obligations.\n5. Recognize revenue as obligations are satisfied.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t12", "title": "Revenue and cash timing", "body": "## Cash before earning\n\nReceive cash:\n\n**Dr Cash**\n\n**Cr Deferred revenue**\n\nWhen earned:\n\n**Dr Deferred revenue**\n\n**Cr Revenue**\n\nDeferred revenue is a **liability**.\n\n---\n\n## Cash at same time\n\n**Dr Cash**\n\n**Cr Revenue**\n\n---\n\n## Cash after earning\n\nAt sale:\n\n**Dr Accounts receivable**\n\n**Cr Revenue**\n\nAt collection:\n\n**Dr Cash**\n\n**Cr Accounts receivable**\n\nNo second revenue is recorded.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t13", "title": "Expense recognition", "body": "Resources used to generate revenue should be recognized as expenses in the appropriate period.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Wages\n- Utilities\n- Cost of inventory sold\n- Rent\n- Depreciation", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t14", "title": "Expense and cash timing", "body": "## Cash paid before expense\n\nInitially:\n\n**Dr Asset**\n\n**Cr Cash**\n\nWhen consumed:\n\n**Dr Expense**\n\n**Cr Asset**\n\n---\n\n## Expense and payment together\n\n**Dr Expense**\n\n**Cr Cash**\n\n---\n\n## Expense before cash\n\nWhen incurred:\n\n**Dr Expense**\n\n**Cr Payable**\n\nWhen paid:\n\n**Dr Payable**\n\n**Cr Cash**", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t15", "title": "Revenue and expense effects", "body": "Revenue:\n\n- Increases net earnings\n- Increases retained earnings\n- Increases shareholders' equity\n- Normally has a credit balance\n\nExpense:\n\n- Decreases net earnings\n- Decreases retained earnings\n- Decreases shareholders' equity\n- Normally has a debit balance", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t16", "title": "Total asset turnover", "body": "\\[\n\\boxed{\nTotal\\ Asset\\ Turnover=\n\\frac{Net\\ Sales}{Average\\ Total\\ Assets}\n}\n\\]\n\nwith:\n\n\\[\nAverage\\ Assets=\n\\frac{Beginning+Ending}{2}\n\\]\n\nMeasures sales generated per dollar invested in assets.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t17", "title": "Return on assets — ROA", "body": "\\[\n\\boxed{\nROA=\n\\frac{Net\\ Earnings}{Average\\ Total\\ Assets}\n}\n\\]\n\nMeasures earnings generated by assets.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t18", "title": "Statement of cash flows", "body": "Operating cash flows mainly involve accounts such as:\n\n- Accounts receivable\n- Inventory\n- Prepaids\n- Accounts payable\n- Wages payable\n- Deferred revenue\n\nLong-run survival generally requires positive cash flows from operations.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}, {"id": "c3-t19", "title": "Comprehensive income", "body": "Publicly accountable enterprises may report:\n\n\\[\nComprehensive\\ Income=\nNet\\ Earnings+Other\\ Comprehensive\\ Income\n\\]\n\nOther comprehensive income includes certain unrealized gains/losses not included in regular net earnings.", "format": "markdown", "source": "Create Study Guide Notes · Chapters 1–12 master notes"}]}, {"id": 4, "title": "Adjustments, Financial Statements, and the Closing Process", "topics": [{"id": "c4-t1", "title": "The Accounting Cycle", "body": "The accounting cycle is the process used by entities to analyze and record transactions, adjust the records to provide reliable account balances at the end of the period, prepare financial statements, and prepare the records for the next cycle.\n\nDuring the accounting period, transactions that result in exchanges of benefits and obligations between the company and other external parties are analyzed and recorded in the general journal in chronological order (journal entries), and the related accounts are updated in the general ledger (T-accounts).\n\nThe end-of-period steps focus primarily on adjustments to record revenues and expenses in the proper period and to update the statement of financial position accounts for reporting purposes.\n\n", "blocks": [{"body": "The accounting cycle is the process used by entities to analyze and record transactions, adjust the records to provide reliable account balances at the end of the period, prepare financial statements, and prepare the records for the next cycle.\n\nDuring the accounting period, transactions that result in exchanges of benefits and obligations between the company and other external parties are analyzed and recorded in the general journal in chronological order (journal entries), and the related accounts are updated in the general ledger (T-accounts).\n\nThe end-of-period steps focus primarily on adjustments to record revenues and expenses in the proper period and to update the statement of financial position accounts for reporting purposes.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 3}, {"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch4-image2.png", "alt": "The Accounting Cycle — original slide 4"}], "slide": 4}], "format": "plain", "slides": [3, 4], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t2", "title": "Four Types of Adjustments", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch4-image3.png", "alt": "Four Types of Adjustments — original slide 5"}], "slide": 5}], "format": "plain", "slides": [5], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t3", "title": "Purpose of Adjustments", "body": "Adjusting entries are recorded at the end of every accounting period, so that\n\nRevenues are recorded when earned (the revenue recognition principle).\n\nExpenses are recorded when incurred to generate revenue during the same period (the expense recognition principle).\n\nAssets are reported at amounts that represent the probable future benefits remaining at the end of the period.\n\nLiabilities are reported at amounts that represent the probable future sacrifices of assets or services owed at the end of the period.\n\nCompanies wait until the end of the accounting period to adjust their accounts, because adjusting the records daily would be very costly and time-consuming.\n\nAdjusting entries are required every time a company wants to prepare financial statements for external users.", "blocks": [{"body": "Adjusting entries are recorded at the end of every accounting period, so that\n\nRevenues are recorded when earned (the revenue recognition principle).\n\nExpenses are recorded when incurred to generate revenue during the same period (the expense recognition principle).\n\nAssets are reported at amounts that represent the probable future benefits remaining at the end of the period.\n\nLiabilities are reported at amounts that represent the probable future sacrifices of assets or services owed at the end of the period.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 6}, {"body": "Companies wait until the end of the accounting period to adjust their accounts, because adjusting the records daily would be very costly and time-consuming.\n\nAdjusting entries are required every time a company wants to prepare financial statements for external users.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 7}], "format": "plain", "slides": [6, 7], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t4", "title": "Adjustment Process", "body": "Step 1: Ask: Was revenue earned that is not yet recorded?If the answer is YES, increase the revenue account —credit the revenue account in the adjusting entry.\n\nStep 2: Ask: Was the related cash received in the past or will it be received in the future?\n\nIf cash was received in the past, a deferred revenue (liability) account was recorded in the past ⟶ now, reduce (debit) the liability account (usually deferred revenue) that was recorded when cash was received, because the company has fulfilled its obligations to the customer and decreased all or part of its liability.\n\nIf cash will be received in the future ⟶ increase (debit) the receivable account (such as interest receivable or rent receivable) to record what is owed by others to the company (creates accrued revenue).\n\nStep 3: Compute the amount of revenue earned and record the adjusting entry.\n\nSometimes the amount is given or known. At other times it must be computed, or sometimes estimated.\n\nStep 1: Ask: Was an expense incurred that is not yet recorded?\n\nIf the answer is YES, increase the expense account—debit the expense account in the adjusting entry.\n\nStep 2: Ask: Was the related cash paid in the past or will it be paid in the future?\n\nIf cash was paid in the past, a deferred expense account (asset) was recorded in the past ⟶ now, reduce (credit) the asset account (such as supplies or prepaid expenses) that was recorded in the past because the entire asset or part of it has been used since then. (A variation of this concept will be illustrated for the use of buildings, equipment, and some intangible assets.)\n\nIf cash will be paid in the future ⟶ increase (credit) the payable account (such as interest payable or wages payable) to record what is owed by the company to others (creates an accrued expense).\n\nStep 3: Compute the amount of expense incurred and record the adjusting entry.\n\nSometimes the amount is given or known. At other times it must be computed, or sometimes estimated.", "blocks": [{"body": "Step 1: Ask: Was revenue earned that is not yet recorded?If the answer is YES, increase the revenue account —credit the revenue account in the adjusting entry.\n\nStep 2: Ask: Was the related cash received in the past or will it be received in the future?\n\nIf cash was received in the past, a deferred revenue (liability) account was recorded in the past ⟶ now, reduce (debit) the liability account (usually deferred revenue) that was recorded when cash was received, because the company has fulfilled its obligations to the customer and decreased all or part of its liability.\n\nIf cash will be received in the future ⟶ increase (debit) the receivable account (such as interest receivable or rent receivable) to record what is owed by others to the company (creates accrued revenue).\n\nStep 3: Compute the amount of revenue earned and record the adjusting entry.\n\nSometimes the amount is given or known. At other times it must be computed, or sometimes estimated.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 8}, {"body": "Step 1: Ask: Was an expense incurred that is not yet recorded?\n\nIf the answer is YES, increase the expense account—debit the expense account in the adjusting entry.\n\nStep 2: Ask: Was the related cash paid in the past or will it be paid in the future?\n\nIf cash was paid in the past, a deferred expense account (asset) was recorded in the past ⟶ now, reduce (credit) the asset account (such as supplies or prepaid expenses) that was recorded in the past because the entire asset or part of it has been used since then. (A variation of this concept will be illustrated for the use of buildings, equipment, and some intangible assets.)\n\nIf cash will be paid in the future ⟶ increase (credit) the payable account (such as interest payable or wages payable) to record what is owed by the company to others (creates an accrued expense).\n\nStep 3: Compute the amount of expense incurred and record the adjusting entry.\n\nSometimes the amount is given or known. At other times it must be computed, or sometimes estimated.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 9}], "format": "plain", "slides": [8, 9], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t5", "title": "Adjusting Entries Summary", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["When revenue is earned", " ", " ", " ", "When expense is incurred", " ", " "], ["DEFERRED REVENUE", " ", " ", " ", "DEFERRED EXPENSE", " ", " "], ["If cash was received and previously recorded", " ", " ", " ", "If cash was paid and previously recorded", " ", " "], ["Cash (+A)", "xx", " ", " ", "Prepaid expense (+A)", "xx", " "], ["     Deferred revenue (+L)", " ", "xx", " ", "     Cash (–A)", " ", "xx"], ["the adjusting entry is", " ", " ", " ", "the adjusting entry is", " ", " "], ["Deferred revenue (–L)", "xx", " ", " ", "Expense (+E, –SE)    ", "xx", " "], ["     Revenue (+R, +SE)", " ", "xx", " ", "     Prepaid expense (–A)", " ", "xx"], ["OR", "", "", " ", "OR", "", ""], ["ACCRUED REVENUE", " ", " ", " ", "ACCRUED EXPENSE", " ", " "], ["If cash will be received, the adjusting entry is", " ", " ", " ", "If cash will be paid, the adjusting entry is", " ", " "], ["Accounts receivable (+A)", "xx", " ", " ", "Expense (+E, –SE)", "xx", " "], ["     Revenue (+R, +SE)", " ", "xx", " ", "     Payable (+L)", " ", "xx"]]], "images": [], "slide": 10}], "format": "plain", "slides": [10], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t6", "title": "Deferred Revenues", "body": "When a customer pays for goods or services before the company delivers them, the company records the amount of cash received in a deferred revenue account.\n\nDeferred revenue is a liability representing the company’s promise to perform or deliver the goods or services in the future.\n\nRecognition of (recording) the revenue is deferred (postponed) until the company meets its obligation.\n\nExamples include rent received in advance, magazine subscriptions, season tickets to sporting events, plays, and concerts, and airline tickets sold in advance.\n\nAdjusting Entry: Decrease Liability and Increase Revenue", "blocks": [{"body": "When a customer pays for goods or services before the company delivers them, the company records the amount of cash received in a deferred revenue account.\n\nDeferred revenue is a liability representing the company’s promise to perform or deliver the goods or services in the future.\n\nRecognition of (recording) the revenue is deferred (postponed) until the company meets its obligation.\n\nExamples include rent received in advance, magazine subscriptions, season tickets to sporting events, plays, and concerts, and airline tickets sold in advance.\n\nAdjusting Entry: Decrease Liability and Increase Revenue", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 11}], "format": "plain", "slides": [11], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t7", "title": "Accrued Revenues", "body": "Sometimes, companies perform services or provide goods (i.e., earn revenue) before customers pay.\n\nBecause the cash that is owed for these goods has not yet been received, the revenue that was earned has not been recorded.\n\nRevenues that have been recognized (earned) but have not yet been realized in cash and recorded at the end of the accounting period are called accrued revenues.\n\nExamples include interest on notes receivable and rent of facilities that have not been received in cash by the end of the accounting period.\n\nAdjusting Entry: Increase Asset and Increase Revenue", "blocks": [{"body": "Sometimes, companies perform services or provide goods (i.e., earn revenue) before customers pay.\n\nBecause the cash that is owed for these goods has not yet been received, the revenue that was earned has not been recorded.\n\nRevenues that have been recognized (earned) but have not yet been realized in cash and recorded at the end of the accounting period are called accrued revenues.\n\nExamples include interest on notes receivable and rent of facilities that have not been received in cash by the end of the accounting period.\n\nAdjusting Entry: Increase Asset and Increase Revenue", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 12}], "format": "plain", "slides": [12], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t8", "title": "Deferred Expenses", "body": "Assets represent resources with probable future benefits to the company. Many assets are used over time to generate revenues, including supplies, prepaid rent, prepaid insurance, buildings, equipment, and intangible assets, such as patents and copyrights. These assets are deferred expenses.\n\nAt the end of every period, an adjustment must be made to record the amount of the asset that was used during the period.\n\nExamples include supplies, prepaid expenses (e.g., rent, insurance) and buildings and equipment.\n\nAdjusting Entry: Increase Expense and Decrease Asset", "blocks": [{"body": "Assets represent resources with probable future benefits to the company. Many assets are used over time to generate revenues, including supplies, prepaid rent, prepaid insurance, buildings, equipment, and intangible assets, such as patents and copyrights. These assets are deferred expenses.\n\nAt the end of every period, an adjustment must be made to record the amount of the asset that was used during the period.\n\nExamples include supplies, prepaid expenses (e.g., rent, insurance) and buildings and equipment.\n\nAdjusting Entry: Increase Expense and Decrease Asset", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 13}], "format": "plain", "slides": [13], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t9", "title": "Property, Plant, and Equipment", "body": "Property, plant, and equipment increases when assets are acquired and decreases when they are sold or disposed of.\n\nHowever, these assets are also used over time to generate revenue. Thus, a part of their cost should be expensed in the same period (following the expense recognition principle).\n\nThese assets depreciate over time as they are used. In accounting, depreciation is an allocation of an asset’s cost over its estimated useful life to the organization.\n\nTo keep track of the asset’s historical cost, the amount that has been used is not subtracted directly from the asset account. Instead, it is accumulated in a new kind of account called a contra account.\n\nA contra account is an account that is an offset to, or reduction of, the primary account. It is directly related to another account, but has a balance on the opposite side of the T-account.\n\nAs a contra account increases, the net amount (the account balance less the contra-account balance) decreases. For property, plant, and equipment, the contra account is called accumulated depreciation.\n\nSince assets have debit balances, accumulated depreciation has a credit balance.\n\nOn the statement of financial position, the amount that is reported for property, plant, and equipment is its carrying amount (book value or net book value), which equals the ending balance in the property, plant, and equipment account minus the ending balance in the accumulated depreciation account.", "blocks": [{"body": "Property, plant, and equipment increases when assets are acquired and decreases when they are sold or disposed of.\n\nHowever, these assets are also used over time to generate revenue. Thus, a part of their cost should be expensed in the same period (following the expense recognition principle).\n\nThese assets depreciate over time as they are used. In accounting, depreciation is an allocation of an asset’s cost over its estimated useful life to the organization.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 14}, {"body": "To keep track of the asset’s historical cost, the amount that has been used is not subtracted directly from the asset account. Instead, it is accumulated in a new kind of account called a contra account.\n\nA contra account is an account that is an offset to, or reduction of, the primary account. It is directly related to another account, but has a balance on the opposite side of the T-account.\n\nAs a contra account increases, the net amount (the account balance less the contra-account balance) decreases. For property, plant, and equipment, the contra account is called accumulated depreciation.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 15}, {"body": "Since assets have debit balances, accumulated depreciation has a credit balance.\n\nOn the statement of financial position, the amount that is reported for property, plant, and equipment is its carrying amount (book value or net book value), which equals the ending balance in the property, plant, and equipment account minus the ending balance in the accumulated depreciation account.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 16}], "format": "plain", "slides": [14, 15, 16], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t10", "title": "Accrued Expenses", "body": "Numerous expenses are incurred in the current period without being paid for until the next period.\n\nCommon examples include interest expense incurred on debt, wages expense for the wages owed to employees, and utilities expense for water, gas, and electricity used during the period for which the company has not yet received a bill.\n\nThese accrued expenses accumulate (accrue) over time but are not recognized until the end of the period in an adjusting entry.\n\nExamples include interest payable, wages payable, and property taxes payable.\n\nAdjusting Entry: Increase Expense and Increase Liability", "blocks": [{"body": "Numerous expenses are incurred in the current period without being paid for until the next period.\n\nCommon examples include interest expense incurred on debt, wages expense for the wages owed to employees, and utilities expense for water, gas, and electricity used during the period for which the company has not yet received a bill.\n\nThese accrued expenses accumulate (accrue) over time but are not recognized until the end of the period in an adjusting entry.\n\nExamples include interest payable, wages payable, and property taxes payable.\n\nAdjusting Entry: Increase Expense and Increase Liability", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 17}], "format": "plain", "slides": [17], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t11", "title": "Materiality and Adjusting Entries", "body": "The term “materiality” describes the relative significance of financial statement information in influencing economic decisions made by financial statement users.\n\nMateriality suggests that minor items that would not influence the decisions of financial statement users are to be treated in the most cost-beneficial way in compliance with the accounting standards.\n\nMateriality is a qualitative factor in the conceptual framework providing scope for accountants to apply the pervasive constraint that benefit to users must outweigh the cost of reporting accounting information when they record the effects of transactions and prepare financial disclosure.", "blocks": [{"body": "The term “materiality” describes the relative significance of financial statement information in influencing economic decisions made by financial statement users.\n\nMateriality suggests that minor items that would not influence the decisions of financial statement users are to be treated in the most cost-beneficial way in compliance with the accounting standards.\n\nMateriality is a qualitative factor in the conceptual framework providing scope for accountants to apply the pervasive constraint that benefit to users must outweigh the cost of reporting accounting information when they record the effects of transactions and prepare financial disclosure.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 18}], "format": "plain", "slides": [18], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t12", "title": "Preparing Financial Statements", "body": "Financial statements are interrelated; that is, the numbers from one statement flow into the next statement.\n\nThe adjusted balances in the trial balance is what is used to prepare the statement of earnings, the statement of changes in equity (which includes a column for retained earnings), and then the statement of financial position.\n\nExhibit 4.4 highlights the interconnections among the statements using the fundamental accounting equation, followed by a summary structure of each statement highlighting relationships.  This is shown on the next slide.", "blocks": [{"body": "Financial statements are interrelated; that is, the numbers from one statement flow into the next statement.\n\nThe adjusted balances in the trial balance is what is used to prepare the statement of earnings, the statement of changes in equity (which includes a column for retained earnings), and then the statement of financial position.\n\nExhibit 4.4 highlights the interconnections among the statements using the fundamental accounting equation, followed by a summary structure of each statement highlighting relationships.  This is shown on the next slide.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 19}], "format": "plain", "slides": [19], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t13", "title": "Interrelationships of Financial Statements", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch4-image4.png", "alt": "Interrelationships of Financial Statements — original slide 20"}], "slide": 20}], "format": "plain", "slides": [20], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t14", "title": "Statement of Earnings", "body": "The statement of earnings is prepared first, because net earnings is a component of retained earnings, which is reported on the statement of changes in equity.\n\nThe earnings per share (EPS) ratio is reported on the statement of earnings. It is widely used in evaluating the operating performance and profitability of a company and is the only ratio required to be disclosed on the statement or in the notes to the financial statements.", "blocks": [{"body": "The statement of earnings is prepared first, because net earnings is a component of retained earnings, which is reported on the statement of changes in equity.\n\nThe earnings per share (EPS) ratio is reported on the statement of earnings. It is widely used in evaluating the operating performance and profitability of a company and is the only ratio required to be disclosed on the statement or in the notes to the financial statements.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 21}], "format": "plain", "slides": [21], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t15", "title": "Earnings Per Share (EPS)", "body": "The actual calculation for EPS is quite complex and appropriate for intermediate accounting courses; however, in this text we simplify the earnings per share calculation to be:\n\nEarnings per share  =\n\nNet earnings\n\nAverage number of common shares\n\nOutstanding during the period", "blocks": [{"body": "The actual calculation for EPS is quite complex and appropriate for intermediate accounting courses; however, in this text we simplify the earnings per share calculation to be:\n\nEarnings per share  =\n\nNet earnings\n\nAverage number of common shares\n\nOutstanding during the period", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 22}], "format": "plain", "slides": [22], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t16", "title": "Statement of Changes in Equity", "body": "The final amount from the statement of earnings, net earnings, is carried forward to the retained earnings column of the statement of changes in equity.\n\nNet earnings is an increase in Retained Earnings.\n\nDividends declared during the period are deducted to arrive at the ending balance. The issuance of additional shares is added to the beginning balance of contributed capital.", "blocks": [{"body": "The final amount from the statement of earnings, net earnings, is carried forward to the retained earnings column of the statement of changes in equity.\n\nNet earnings is an increase in Retained Earnings.\n\nDividends declared during the period are deducted to arrive at the ending balance. The issuance of additional shares is added to the beginning balance of contributed capital.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 23}], "format": "plain", "slides": [23], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t17", "title": "Statement of Financial Position", "body": "The ending balances under the headings contributed capital, retained earnings, and other components on the statement of changes in equity are included on the statement of financial position.\n\nAssets are listed in order of liquidity, while liabilities are listed in order of time to maturity.\n\nCurrent assets are those used or turned into cash within one year. Current liabilities are obligations to be settled within one year.", "blocks": [{"body": "The ending balances under the headings contributed capital, retained earnings, and other components on the statement of changes in equity are included on the statement of financial position.\n\nAssets are listed in order of liquidity, while liabilities are listed in order of time to maturity.\n\nCurrent assets are those used or turned into cash within one year. Current liabilities are obligations to be settled within one year.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 24}], "format": "plain", "slides": [24], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t18", "title": "Net Profit Margin Ratio", "body": "The net profit margin ratio compares net earnings to the revenues generated during the period.\n\nANALYTICAL QUESTION → How effective is management at controlling revenues and expenses to generate more earnings?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The net profit margin ratio is useful in answering this question. It is computed as follows:\n\n*Net sales is sales revenue less any returns from customers, and other reductions. For companies in the service industry, total operating revenues equals net sales.\n\nNet EarningsNet Sales (or Operating revenues)*\n\nNet Profit\n\nMargin Ratio\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "The net profit margin ratio compares net earnings to the revenues generated during the period.\n\nANALYTICAL QUESTION → How effective is management at controlling revenues and expenses to generate more earnings?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The net profit margin ratio is useful in answering this question. It is computed as follows:\n\n*Net sales is sales revenue less any returns from customers, and other reductions. For companies in the service industry, total operating revenues equals net sales.\n\nNet EarningsNet Sales (or Operating revenues)*\n\nNet Profit\n\nMargin Ratio\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 25}], "format": "plain", "slides": [25], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t19", "title": "Net Profit Margin Ratio Interpretations", "body": "The net profit ratio margin measures how much profit is earned as a percentage of revenues generated during the period.\n\nA rising net profit margin ratio signals more efficient management of sales and expenses.\n\nFinancial analysts expect well-run businesses to maintain or improve their net profit margin ratio over time.", "blocks": [{"body": "The net profit ratio margin measures how much profit is earned as a percentage of revenues generated during the period.\n\nA rising net profit margin ratio signals more efficient management of sales and expenses.\n\nFinancial analysts expect well-run businesses to maintain or improve their net profit margin ratio over time.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 26}], "format": "plain", "slides": [26], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t20", "title": "Return on Equity (ROE)", "body": "The return on equity relates net earnings to shareholders’ investment in the business.\n\nANALYTICAL QUESTION → How well has management used shareholder investment to generate net earnings during the period?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The return on equity (ROE) helps in answering this question. It is computed as follows:\n\n​​\n\n*Average shareholders’ equity = (Beginning shareholders’ equity + Ending shareholders’ equity) ÷ 2\n\nReturn onEquity\n\n=\n\nNet Earnings\n\nAverage Shareholders’ Equity*", "blocks": [{"body": "The return on equity relates net earnings to shareholders’ investment in the business.\n\nANALYTICAL QUESTION → How well has management used shareholder investment to generate net earnings during the period?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The return on equity (ROE) helps in answering this question. It is computed as follows:\n\n​​\n\n*Average shareholders’ equity = (Beginning shareholders’ equity + Ending shareholders’ equity) ÷ 2\n\nReturn onEquity\n\n=\n\nNet Earnings\n\nAverage Shareholders’ Equity*", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 27}], "format": "plain", "slides": [27], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t21", "title": "Return on Equity Interpretations", "body": "ROE measures how much the firm earned, on average, for every dollar invested by shareholders.\n\nIn the long run, firms with higher ROE are expected to have higher share prices than firms with lower ROE, all other things being equal.\n\nManagers, analysts, and creditors use this ratio to assess the effectiveness of the company’s overall business strategy (its operating, investing, and financing strategies).", "blocks": [{"body": "ROE measures how much the firm earned, on average, for every dollar invested by shareholders.\n\nIn the long run, firms with higher ROE are expected to have higher share prices than firms with lower ROE, all other things being equal.\n\nManagers, analysts, and creditors use this ratio to assess the effectiveness of the company’s overall business strategy (its operating, investing, and financing strategies).", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 28}], "format": "plain", "slides": [28], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t22", "title": "How Transactions Affect Ratios", "body": "Investors and creditors follow companies key financial ratios closely, therefore, company managers closely follow the effects of their own actual and planned transactions on these same key financial ratios.\n\nThe following three-step process helps compute the effects of transactions on ratios:\n\nJournalize the transaction to determine its effects on various accounts.\n\nDetermine which accounts belong to the financial statement subtotals or totals in the numerator (top) and denominator (bottom) of the ratio and the direction of their effects.\n\nEvaluate the combined effects from step 2 on the ratio.", "blocks": [{"body": "Investors and creditors follow companies key financial ratios closely, therefore, company managers closely follow the effects of their own actual and planned transactions on these same key financial ratios.\n\nThe following three-step process helps compute the effects of transactions on ratios:\n\nJournalize the transaction to determine its effects on various accounts.\n\nDetermine which accounts belong to the financial statement subtotals or totals in the numerator (top) and denominator (bottom) of the ratio and the direction of their effects.\n\nEvaluate the combined effects from step 2 on the ratio.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 29}], "format": "plain", "slides": [29], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t23", "title": "Closing the Books", "body": "All adjusting entries are recorded and posted to update the accounts and then the financial statements are prepared.\n\nA closing process is needed as the last step in the accounting cycle to mark the end of the current period and the beginning of the next.", "blocks": [{"body": "All adjusting entries are recorded and posted to update the accounts and then the financial statements are prepared.\n\nA closing process is needed as the last step in the accounting cycle to mark the end of the current period and the beginning of the next.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 30}], "format": "plain", "slides": [30], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t24", "title": "End of the Accounting Cycle", "body": "Statement of financial position accounts are permanent (real) accounts and are not reduced to zero at the end of the accounting period. The ending balance for the current accounting period becomes the beginning account balance for the next accounting period.\n\nHowever, revenue, expense, gain, loss, and dividend accounts are used to accumulate transaction effects for the current accounting period only; they are called temporary (nominal) accounts.\n\nAt the end of each period, the balances in the temporary accounts are transferred, or closed, to the retained earnings account by recording closing entries.", "blocks": [{"body": "Statement of financial position accounts are permanent (real) accounts and are not reduced to zero at the end of the accounting period. The ending balance for the current accounting period becomes the beginning account balance for the next accounting period.\n\nHowever, revenue, expense, gain, loss, and dividend accounts are used to accumulate transaction effects for the current accounting period only; they are called temporary (nominal) accounts.\n\nAt the end of each period, the balances in the temporary accounts are transferred, or closed, to the retained earnings account by recording closing entries.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 31}], "format": "plain", "slides": [31], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t25", "title": "Closing Entries", "body": "The closing entries have two purposes:\n\nTo transfer the balances in the temporary accounts to retained earnings.\n\nTo establish a zero balance in each of the temporary accounts to start the accumulation in the next accounting period.", "blocks": [{"body": "The closing entries have two purposes:\n\nTo transfer the balances in the temporary accounts to retained earnings.\n\nTo establish a zero balance in each of the temporary accounts to start the accumulation in the next accounting period.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 32}], "format": "plain", "slides": [32], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t26", "title": "Steps for Closing Entries", "body": "The closing process requires three journal entries:\n\none entry to close the revenue and gain accounts,\n\nanother entry to close the expense and loss accounts, and\n\na final entry to close the dividends declared account.\n\nIncome Summary, is a temporary account used only during the closing process to facilitate closing temporary accounts.\n\nRevenue and gain accounts, which have credit balances, are closed by debiting each account for the account balance and crediting the total amount to income summary.\n\nExpense and loss accounts, which have debit balances, are closed by crediting each account for the account balance and debiting the total amount to income summary.\n\nThe balance of the income summary account reflects the net earnings (loss) and is then closed to the retained earnings account.\n\nThe statement of earnings accounts are again ready for their temporary accumulation function for the next period.\n\nClosing entries are dated the last day of the accounting period, entered in the usual format in the journal, and immediately posted to the ledger (or T-accounts).", "blocks": [{"body": "The closing process requires three journal entries:\n\none entry to close the revenue and gain accounts,\n\nanother entry to close the expense and loss accounts, and\n\na final entry to close the dividends declared account.\n\nIncome Summary, is a temporary account used only during the closing process to facilitate closing temporary accounts.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 33}, {"body": "Revenue and gain accounts, which have credit balances, are closed by debiting each account for the account balance and crediting the total amount to income summary.\n\nExpense and loss accounts, which have debit balances, are closed by crediting each account for the account balance and debiting the total amount to income summary.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 34}, {"body": "The balance of the income summary account reflects the net earnings (loss) and is then closed to the retained earnings account.\n\nThe statement of earnings accounts are again ready for their temporary accumulation function for the next period.\n\nClosing entries are dated the last day of the accounting period, entered in the usual format in the journal, and immediately posted to the ledger (or T-accounts).", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 35}], "format": "plain", "slides": [33, 34, 35], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}, {"id": "c4-t27", "title": "Post-Closing Trial Balance", "body": "After the closing process is complete, all of the statement of earnings accounts have a zero balance. These accounts are ready for recording the effects of transactions that generate revenues and incur expenses in the new accounting period.\n\nThe ending balance in retained earnings is now up to date and is carried forward as the beginning balance for the next period.\n\nA post-closing trial balance should be prepared as the last step of the accounting cycle to check that debits equal credits and that all temporary accounts have been closed.", "blocks": [{"body": "After the closing process is complete, all of the statement of earnings accounts have a zero balance. These accounts are ready for recording the effects of transactions that generate revenues and incur expenses in the new accounting period.\n\nThe ending balance in retained earnings is now up to date and is carried forward as the beginning balance for the next period.\n\nA post-closing trial balance should be prepared as the last step of the accounting cycle to check that debits equal credits and that all temporary accounts have been closed.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 36}], "format": "plain", "slides": [36], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 4 slides"}], "deck": "sources/chapter-4.pptx"}, {"id": 5, "title": "Reporting and Interpreting Sales Revenue, Receivables, and Cash", "topics": [{"id": "c5-t1", "title": "Accounting for Net Sales Revenue", "body": "The core principle of revenue recognition requires that revenues be recorded when the company transfers goods and services to customers, in the amount it expects to be entitled to receive.\n\nThere are straightforward ways to identify when the core principle is fulfilled and the risks and benefits of ownership have transferred from the seller to the customer.\n\nA contract, written, oral, or based on conventions and norms, will specify when the risks and benefits of ownership are transferred.\n\nIt is customary to use the shipping terms in a sales contract to determine the point at which title (ownership) changes hands:\n\nWhen goods are shipped FOB (free on board) shipping point, title changes hands at shipment, and the buyer normally pays for shipping.\n\nWhen they are shipped FOB destination point, title changes hands on delivery, and the seller normally pays for shipping.\n\nRevenues from goods sold FOB shipping point are normally recognized at shipment. Revenues from goods sold FOB destination point are normally recognized at delivery.\n\nService companies most often record sales revenue when they have fulfilled their obligations to the buyer under the contract to which both have agreed.\n\nCompanies disclose the specific revenue recognition policies they use in the note to their financial statements titled “Significant Accounting Policies.”", "blocks": [{"body": "The core principle of revenue recognition requires that revenues be recorded when the company transfers goods and services to customers, in the amount it expects to be entitled to receive.\n\nThere are straightforward ways to identify when the core principle is fulfilled and the risks and benefits of ownership have transferred from the seller to the customer.\n\nA contract, written, oral, or based on conventions and norms, will specify when the risks and benefits of ownership are transferred.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 4}, {"body": "It is customary to use the shipping terms in a sales contract to determine the point at which title (ownership) changes hands:\n\nWhen goods are shipped FOB (free on board) shipping point, title changes hands at shipment, and the buyer normally pays for shipping.\n\nWhen they are shipped FOB destination point, title changes hands on delivery, and the seller normally pays for shipping.\n\nRevenues from goods sold FOB shipping point are normally recognized at shipment. Revenues from goods sold FOB destination point are normally recognized at delivery.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 5}, {"body": "Service companies most often record sales revenue when they have fulfilled their obligations to the buyer under the contract to which both have agreed.\n\nCompanies disclose the specific revenue recognition policies they use in the note to their financial statements titled “Significant Accounting Policies.”", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 6}], "format": "plain", "slides": [4, 5, 6], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t2", "title": "Motivating Sales and Collections", "body": "There are a variety of methods that can be used to motivate its customers, both business and consumers, to buy products and make payments for their purchases.\n\nThe principal methods are:\n\n(1) allowing all customers to use credit cards to pay for purchases\n\n(2) providing business customers direct credit and discounts for early payment and\n\n(3) allowing returns from all customers under specific circumstances.\n\nThese methods, in turn, affect the way we compute net sales revenue.", "blocks": [{"body": "There are a variety of methods that can be used to motivate its customers, both business and consumers, to buy products and make payments for their purchases.\n\nThe principal methods are:\n\n(1) allowing all customers to use credit cards to pay for purchases\n\n(2) providing business customers direct credit and discounts for early payment and\n\n(3) allowing returns from all customers under specific circumstances.\n\nThese methods, in turn, affect the way we compute net sales revenue.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 7}], "format": "plain", "slides": [7], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t3", "title": "Credit Card Sales to Customers", "body": "Companies accept credit cards for several reasons:\n\nTo increase sales.\n\nTo avoid providing credit directly to customers.\n\nTo avoid losses due to bad cheques.\n\nTo avoid losses due to fraudulent credit card sales.\n\nTo receive payment quicker.\n\nThe credit card company charges a fee for the service it provides.\n\nThe fee charged by the credit card company for its services is the credit card discount.", "blocks": [{"body": "Companies accept credit cards for several reasons:\n\nTo increase sales.\n\nTo avoid providing credit directly to customers.\n\nTo avoid losses due to bad cheques.\n\nTo avoid losses due to fraudulent credit card sales.\n\nTo receive payment quicker.\n\nThe credit card company charges a fee for the service it provides.\n\nThe fee charged by the credit card company for its services is the credit card discount.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 8}], "format": "plain", "slides": [8], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t4", "title": "Credit Card Sales to Customers - Example", "body": "If daily credit card sales were $3,000, and the credit card company charges a 3% service fee, the following would be reported:\n\nJournal Entry\n\nCash\t\t\t\t\t2,910\n\nCredit card discount\t\t      90\n\nSales (or Revenue)\t\t\t\t\t3,000", "blocks": [{"body": "If daily credit card sales were $3,000, and the credit card company charges a 3% service fee, the following would be reported:\n\nJournal Entry\n\nCash\t\t\t\t\t2,910\n\nCredit card discount\t\t      90\n\nSales (or Revenue)\t\t\t\t\t3,000", "tables": [[["Gross sales", " ", "$3,000"], ["Less: Credit card discount (0.03 × $3,000)", " ", "90"], ["Net sales (reported on the statement of earnings)", " ", "$2,910"]]], "images": [], "slide": 9}], "format": "plain", "slides": [9], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t5", "title": "Sales Discounts to Businesses", "body": "A sales (or cash) discount is offered to the business customer to encourage prompt payment of a trade account receivable.", "blocks": [{"body": "A sales (or cash) discount is offered to the business customer to encourage prompt payment of a trade account receivable.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch5-image2.png", "alt": "Sales Discounts to Businesses — original slide 10"}], "slide": 10}], "format": "plain", "slides": [10], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t6", "title": "Sales Discounts to Businesses - Example", "body": "If credit sales are recorded with terms 2/10, n/30 and payment of $980 (= $1,000 × 0.98) is made within the discount period, net sales of the following amount would be reported:\n\nAt time of sale\n\nAccounts receivable\t\t\t980\n\nRevenue\t\t\t\t\t980\n\nCollection\n\nCash\t\t\t\t\t     980\n\nAccounts receivable\t\t\t980", "blocks": [{"body": "If credit sales are recorded with terms 2/10, n/30 and payment of $980 (= $1,000 × 0.98) is made within the discount period, net sales of the following amount would be reported:\n\nAt time of sale\n\nAccounts receivable\t\t\t980\n\nRevenue\t\t\t\t\t980\n\nCollection\n\nCash\t\t\t\t\t     980\n\nAccounts receivable\t\t\t980", "tables": [[["Gross sales", " ", "$1,000"], ["Less: Sales discounts (0.02 × $1,000)", " ", "20"], ["Net sales (reported on the statement of earnings)", " ", "$   980"]]], "images": [], "slide": 11}], "format": "plain", "slides": [11], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t7", "title": "Sales Returns", "body": "The amount of expected product returns can be estimated based on past experience, subsequent to the sale transaction.\n\nThe estimated amount of sales returns is then subtracted from the gross sales to determine the net sales revenue.\n\nFor example, a customer bought 40 dozen T-shirts from Gildan for $2,000 on account.  If the company expects 5% of the T-shirts to be returned for credit, then Gildan would compute net sales as follows:", "blocks": [{"body": "The amount of expected product returns can be estimated based on past experience, subsequent to the sale transaction.\n\nThe estimated amount of sales returns is then subtracted from the gross sales to determine the net sales revenue.\n\nFor example, a customer bought 40 dozen T-shirts from Gildan for $2,000 on account.  If the company expects 5% of the T-shirts to be returned for credit, then Gildan would compute net sales as follows:", "tables": [[["Gross sales", " ", "$2,000"], ["Less: Estimated sales returns (0.05 × $2,000)", " ", "100"], ["Net sales (reported on the statement of earnings)", " ", "$1,900"]]], "images": [], "slide": 12}], "format": "plain", "slides": [12], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t8", "title": "Reporting Net Sales", "body": "The amount of sales revenue to record is the amount the company expects to be entitled to receive.\n\nConsequently, credit card fees, sales discounts, and estimated sales returns must be subtracted to compute net sales.", "blocks": [{"body": "The amount of sales revenue to record is the amount the company expects to be entitled to receive.\n\nConsequently, credit card fees, sales discounts, and estimated sales returns must be subtracted to compute net sales.", "tables": [[["Gross sales"], ["Less:  Credit card discounts"], ["      Sales discounts "], ["      Estimated sales returns "], ["Net sales (reported on the first line of the statement of earnings)"]]], "images": [], "slide": 13}], "format": "plain", "slides": [13], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t9", "title": "Revenue Recognition for Bundled Goods & Services", "body": "As customers, we make frequent purchases and we know that some purchases are paid for completely at the point of sale, some have warranties, and return opportunities, and others have benefits attached to the purchase that are provided over time.\n\nAccountants refer to this type of sale as a bundle of goods and services in a sales contract, which is an agreement between two or more parties that creates enforceable rights and obligations.\n\nBundling of goods and services within one sales contract is common in a variety of industries.\n\nWhen a seller promises to provide more than one good or service in a single sales contract, International Financial Reporting Standards specify a five-step process to determine the amount to be recognized as revenue.\n\nRevenue Recognition for Bundled Goods & Services Part 2", "blocks": [{"body": "As customers, we make frequent purchases and we know that some purchases are paid for completely at the point of sale, some have warranties, and return opportunities, and others have benefits attached to the purchase that are provided over time.\n\nAccountants refer to this type of sale as a bundle of goods and services in a sales contract, which is an agreement between two or more parties that creates enforceable rights and obligations.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 14}, {"body": "Bundling of goods and services within one sales contract is common in a variety of industries.\n\nWhen a seller promises to provide more than one good or service in a single sales contract, International Financial Reporting Standards specify a five-step process to determine the amount to be recognized as revenue.\n\nRevenue Recognition for Bundled Goods & Services Part 2", "tables": [[["Step 1:  Identify the contract between the company and customer."], ["Step 2:  Identify the performance obligations (promised goods and services)."], ["Step 3:  Determine the transaction price."], ["Step 4:  Allocate the transaction price to the performance obligations."], ["Step 5:  Recognize revenue when each performance obligation is satisfied (or over time if a service is provided over time)."]]], "images": [], "slide": 15}], "format": "plain", "slides": [14, 15], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t10", "title": "Key Ratio Analysis – Gross Profit Percentage", "body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How effective is management at selling goods and services for more than the costs to purchase or produce them?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The gross profit percentage is helpful in answering this question. It is computed as follows:\n\nInterpretation in General → The gross profit percentage measures how much gross profit is generated from every sales dollar. It reflects the ability to charge premium prices and produce goods and services at low cost. All other things being equal, a higher gross profit results in higher net earnings.\n\nGross ProfitPercentage\n\nGross ProfitNet Sales\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How effective is management at selling goods and services for more than the costs to purchase or produce them?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The gross profit percentage is helpful in answering this question. It is computed as follows:\n\nInterpretation in General → The gross profit percentage measures how much gross profit is generated from every sales dollar. It reflects the ability to charge premium prices and produce goods and services at low cost. All other things being equal, a higher gross profit results in higher net earnings.\n\nGross ProfitPercentage\n\nGross ProfitNet Sales\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 16}], "format": "plain", "slides": [16], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t11", "title": "Measuring and Reporting Receivables", "body": "Receivables may be classified in three common ways:\n\nFirst, in a statement of financial position, receivables are classified as either current or non-current assets (short-term or long-term), depending on whether the cash is expected to be collected within one year of the date of the statement.\n\nSecond, a receivable may be either an account receivable or a note receivable.\n\nThird, receivables may be classified as trade or non-trade receivables.\n\nAccounts receivable are created when companies have sales to customers on open accounts.\n\nNotes receivable are written promises from another party to pay with specified terms.\n\nTrade receivables are open accounts owed to the business by trade customers and are created in the normal course of business when there is a sale of merchandise or services on credit.\n\nNon-trade receivables are amounts owed to the business for other than normal business transactions.", "blocks": [{"body": "Receivables may be classified in three common ways:\n\nFirst, in a statement of financial position, receivables are classified as either current or non-current assets (short-term or long-term), depending on whether the cash is expected to be collected within one year of the date of the statement.\n\nSecond, a receivable may be either an account receivable or a note receivable.\n\nThird, receivables may be classified as trade or non-trade receivables.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 17}, {"body": "Accounts receivable are created when companies have sales to customers on open accounts.\n\nNotes receivable are written promises from another party to pay with specified terms.\n\nTrade receivables are open accounts owed to the business by trade customers and are created in the normal course of business when there is a sale of merchandise or services on credit.\n\nNon-trade receivables are amounts owed to the business for other than normal business transactions.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 18}], "format": "plain", "slides": [17, 18], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t12", "title": "Accounting for Bad Debts", "body": "Bad debts result from credit customers who will not pay the amount they owe, regardless of collection efforts.\n\nThe expense recognition principle requires the recording of bad debt expense in the same accounting period as the one in which the related sales are made.\n\nThis presents an important accounting problem, companies may not know which particular customers will not pay until the next accounting period, therefore not knowing which customers’ accounts receivable are bad debts.", "blocks": [{"body": "Bad debts result from credit customers who will not pay the amount they owe, regardless of collection efforts.\n\nThe expense recognition principle requires the recording of bad debt expense in the same accounting period as the one in which the related sales are made.\n\nThis presents an important accounting problem, companies may not know which particular customers will not pay until the next accounting period, therefore not knowing which customers’ accounts receivable are bad debts.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 19}], "format": "plain", "slides": [19], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t13", "title": "Allowance Method", "body": "The allowance method is used to measure bad debt expense.\n\nThe allowance method is based on an estimate of the expected amount of uncollectible accounts.\n\nThere are two primary steps in applying the allowance method:\n\nEstimating bad debt expense and recording the end-of-period adjusting entry\n\nWriting off specific accounts determined to be uncollectible during the period\n\nThe estimation of bad debts is an example of prudence, whereby the company deducts the estimated amount of bad debts from the ending balance of accounts receivable and reports the net realizable value of accounts receivable on the statement of financial position.", "blocks": [{"body": "The allowance method is used to measure bad debt expense.\n\nThe allowance method is based on an estimate of the expected amount of uncollectible accounts.\n\nThere are two primary steps in applying the allowance method:\n\nEstimating bad debt expense and recording the end-of-period adjusting entry\n\nWriting off specific accounts determined to be uncollectible during the period", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 20}, {"body": "The estimation of bad debts is an example of prudence, whereby the company deducts the estimated amount of bad debts from the ending balance of accounts receivable and reports the net realizable value of accounts receivable on the statement of financial position.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 21}], "format": "plain", "slides": [20, 21], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t14", "title": "Recording Bad Debt Expense Estimates", "body": "Bad debt expense (doubtful accounts expense) is the expense associated with estimated uncollectible accounts receivable.\n\nBad debt expense is recorded through an adjusting entry at the end of the accounting period.\n\nBad debt expense decreases net earnings and shareholders’ equity.\n\nAccounts receivable is not credited in the journal entry, because there is no way to know which customers’ accounts receivable are involved.\n\nThe credit is made, instead, to the account called allowance for doubtful accounts (allowance for doubtful receivables, allowance for bad debts), a contra-asset account containing the estimated uncollectible accounts receivable.\n\nAs a contra asset, the balance in allowance for doubtful accounts is always subtracted from the balance of the asset accounts receivable.\n\nThus, the entry decreases the net realizable value of accounts receivable and total assets.", "blocks": [{"body": "Bad debt expense (doubtful accounts expense) is the expense associated with estimated uncollectible accounts receivable.\n\nBad debt expense is recorded through an adjusting entry at the end of the accounting period.\n\nBad debt expense decreases net earnings and shareholders’ equity.\n\nAccounts receivable is not credited in the journal entry, because there is no way to know which customers’ accounts receivable are involved.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 22}, {"body": "The credit is made, instead, to the account called allowance for doubtful accounts (allowance for doubtful receivables, allowance for bad debts), a contra-asset account containing the estimated uncollectible accounts receivable.\n\nAs a contra asset, the balance in allowance for doubtful accounts is always subtracted from the balance of the asset accounts receivable.\n\nThus, the entry decreases the net realizable value of accounts receivable and total assets.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 23}], "format": "plain", "slides": [22, 23], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t15", "title": "Writing Off Specific Doubtful Accounts", "body": "When it is determined that a customer will not pay its debt (e.g., due to bankruptcy), the write-off of that individual receivable is recorded through a journal entry.\n\nWhen a specific accounts receivable has been identified as uncollectible, it must be removed from the appropriate accounts receivable account.\n\nAt the same time, the previously established allowance for such a doubtful receivable is no longer needed and should be removed from the allowance for doubtful accounts.", "blocks": [{"body": "When it is determined that a customer will not pay its debt (e.g., due to bankruptcy), the write-off of that individual receivable is recorded through a journal entry.\n\nWhen a specific accounts receivable has been identified as uncollectible, it must be removed from the appropriate accounts receivable account.\n\nAt the same time, the previously established allowance for such a doubtful receivable is no longer needed and should be removed from the allowance for doubtful accounts.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 24}], "format": "plain", "slides": [24], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t16", "title": "Recovery of Bad Debts", "body": "When a customer makes a payment on an account previously written off, the initial journal entry to write off the account is reversed for the amount that is collected, and another journal entry is made to record the collection of cash.\n\nThe net effect of the recovered amount on accounts receivable is zero.\n\nThe recovered amount is first recorded in accounts receivable to reinstate the amount in the customers’ accounts and show that the customers have honoured their previous commitments.", "blocks": [{"body": "When a customer makes a payment on an account previously written off, the initial journal entry to write off the account is reversed for the amount that is collected, and another journal entry is made to record the collection of cash.\n\nThe net effect of the recovered amount on accounts receivable is zero.\n\nThe recovered amount is first recorded in accounts receivable to reinstate the amount in the customers’ accounts and show that the customers have honoured their previous commitments.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 25}], "format": "plain", "slides": [25], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t17", "title": "Summary of the Accounting Process", "body": "1. Record            estimated     bad debts     adjustment\n\n2. Identify and     write off     actual bad     debts\n\n3. Record recovery\n\nof bad debts that\n\nwere written off\n\npreviously\n\nEnd of period in which sales are made\n\nThroughoutperiod as baddebts become known\n\nThroughout period as bad debts are recovered\n\nBad Debt Expense (E)\n\nAllowance for Doubtful Accounts (XA)\n\nAccounts Receivable (A)\n\nAllowance for Doubtful Accounts (XA)\n\nAccounts Receivable (A)\n\nAllowance for doubtful accounts (XA)\n\nCash (A)\n\nNet Earnings\n\nAssets (AccountsReceivable, Net)\n\nNet Earnings\n\nAssets (AccountsReceivable, Net)\n\nNet earnings\n\nAssets (A/R, Net)\n\nCash\n\nStep\n\nTiming\n\nAccounts Affected\n\nFinancialStatement Effects\n\nNo\n\neffect\n\nNo\n\neffect\n\nAccounting for bad debts is a three-step process:", "blocks": [{"body": "1. Record            estimated     bad debts     adjustment\n\n2. Identify and     write off     actual bad     debts\n\n3. Record recovery\n\nof bad debts that\n\nwere written off\n\npreviously\n\nEnd of period in which sales are made\n\nThroughoutperiod as baddebts become known\n\nThroughout period as bad debts are recovered\n\nBad Debt Expense (E)\n\nAllowance for Doubtful Accounts (XA)\n\nAccounts Receivable (A)\n\nAllowance for Doubtful Accounts (XA)\n\nAccounts Receivable (A)\n\nAllowance for doubtful accounts (XA)\n\nCash (A)\n\nNet Earnings\n\nAssets (AccountsReceivable, Net)\n\nNet Earnings\n\nAssets (AccountsReceivable, Net)\n\nNet earnings\n\nAssets (A/R, Net)\n\nCash\n\nStep\n\nTiming\n\nAccounts Affected\n\nFinancialStatement Effects\n\nNo\n\neffect\n\nNo\n\neffect\n\nAccounting for bad debts is a three-step process:", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 26}], "format": "plain", "slides": [26], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t18", "title": "Estimating Bad Debts", "body": "The bad debt expense recorded in the end-of-period adjusting entry is often estimated based on either:\n\nA percentage of total credit sales for the period or\n\nAn aging of accounts receivable.\n\nBoth methods are acceptable under IFRS.\n\nThe percentage of credit sales method is simpler to apply, but the aging method is generally more accurate.", "blocks": [{"body": "The bad debt expense recorded in the end-of-period adjusting entry is often estimated based on either:\n\nA percentage of total credit sales for the period or\n\nAn aging of accounts receivable.\n\nBoth methods are acceptable under IFRS.\n\nThe percentage of credit sales method is simpler to apply, but the aging method is generally more accurate.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 27}], "format": "plain", "slides": [27], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t19", "title": "Percentage of Credit Sales Method", "body": "The percentage of credit sales method bases bad debt expense on the historical percentage of credit sales that result in bad debts.\n\nThe average percentage of credit sales that result in bad debts can be computed by dividing total bad debts by total credit sales.\n\nA company that has been operating for some years has sufficient experience to estimate probable future bad debts.", "blocks": [{"body": "The percentage of credit sales method bases bad debt expense on the historical percentage of credit sales that result in bad debts.\n\nThe average percentage of credit sales that result in bad debts can be computed by dividing total bad debts by total credit sales.\n\nA company that has been operating for some years has sufficient experience to estimate probable future bad debts.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 28}], "format": "plain", "slides": [28], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t20", "title": "Aging of Accounts Receivable Method", "body": "The aging of accounts receivable method estimates uncollectible accounts based on the age of each account receivable.\n\nAs accounts receivable become older and overdue they are usually less likely to be collectible.\n\nFor example, a receivable that is due in 30 days but has not been paid after 60 days is more likely to be collected, on average, than a similar receivable that still remains unpaid after 120 days.", "blocks": [{"body": "The aging of accounts receivable method estimates uncollectible accounts based on the age of each account receivable.\n\nAs accounts receivable become older and overdue they are usually less likely to be collectible.\n\nFor example, a receivable that is due in 30 days but has not been paid after 60 days is more likely to be collected, on average, than a similar receivable that still remains unpaid after 120 days.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 29}], "format": "plain", "slides": [29], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t21", "title": "Aging of Accounts Receivable Method - Example", "body": "This illustration of the aging schedule results in an estimate of total uncollectible amounts of $1,532, the estimated ending balance that should be in the allowance for doubtful accounts.\n\nFrom this, the adjustment to record bad debt expense (and an increase in the allowance for doubtful accounts) for 2023 would be calculated.", "blocks": [{"body": "This illustration of the aging schedule results in an estimate of total uncollectible amounts of $1,532, the estimated ending balance that should be in the allowance for doubtful accounts.\n\nFrom this, the adjustment to record bad debt expense (and an increase in the allowance for doubtful accounts) for 2023 would be calculated.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch5-image3.png", "alt": "Aging of Accounts Receivable Method - Example — original slide 30"}], "slide": 30}], "format": "plain", "slides": [30], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t22", "title": "Comparison of the Two Methods", "body": "It is important to recognize that the approach to recording bad debt expense using the percentage of credit sales method is different from that for the aging of accounts receivable method:\n\nPercentage of credit sales. Directly compute the amount to be recorded in the adjusting entry as bad debt expense for the period.\n\nAging of accounts receivable. Compute the estimated ending balance of the allowance for doubtful accounts. The difference between the current balance in the account and the estimated balance is recorded as the adjusting entry for bad debt expense for the period.", "blocks": [{"body": "It is important to recognize that the approach to recording bad debt expense using the percentage of credit sales method is different from that for the aging of accounts receivable method:\n\nPercentage of credit sales. Directly compute the amount to be recorded in the adjusting entry as bad debt expense for the period.\n\nAging of accounts receivable. Compute the estimated ending balance of the allowance for doubtful accounts. The difference between the current balance in the account and the estimated balance is recorded as the adjusting entry for bad debt expense for the period.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 31}], "format": "plain", "slides": [31], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t23", "title": "Actual Write-offs Compared with Estimates", "body": "The amount of uncollectible accounts actually written off seldom equals the estimated amount previously recorded.\n\nThis error in estimating bad debts is taken into consideration in determining the bad debt expense at the end of the next accounting period.\n\nWhen estimates are found to be incorrect, financial statement values for prior annual accounting periods are not corrected.", "blocks": [{"body": "The amount of uncollectible accounts actually written off seldom equals the estimated amount previously recorded.\n\nThis error in estimating bad debts is taken into consideration in determining the bad debt expense at the end of the next accounting period.\n\nWhen estimates are found to be incorrect, financial statement values for prior annual accounting periods are not corrected.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 32}], "format": "plain", "slides": [32], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t24", "title": "Prudence in the Valuation of Accounts Receivable", "body": "Prudence is the exercise of caution when making judgments under conditions of uncertainty.\n\nPotential investors, creditors, and other stakeholders prefer that managers avoid overstating both revenue and assets such as receivables, and understating both liabilities and expenses such as bad debts.\n\nManagers fulfill their duty of care when they choose prudent credit policies, which align with the preferences of stakeholders.\n\nAccountants are also required to exercise a professional duty of care, professional skepticism, and judgment to fulfill the requirements of the Professional Code of Conduct.\n\nThey too must be prudent and are held to a higher level of responsibility because their professional education and experience combine to provide accountants with exceptional skills to assess how well managers have safeguarded assets.", "blocks": [{"body": "Prudence is the exercise of caution when making judgments under conditions of uncertainty.\n\nPotential investors, creditors, and other stakeholders prefer that managers avoid overstating both revenue and assets such as receivables, and understating both liabilities and expenses such as bad debts.\n\nManagers fulfill their duty of care when they choose prudent credit policies, which align with the preferences of stakeholders.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 33}, {"body": "Accountants are also required to exercise a professional duty of care, professional skepticism, and judgment to fulfill the requirements of the Professional Code of Conduct.\n\nThey too must be prudent and are held to a higher level of responsibility because their professional education and experience combine to provide accountants with exceptional skills to assess how well managers have safeguarded assets.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 34}], "format": "plain", "slides": [33, 34], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t25", "title": "Key Ratio Analysis – Receivables Turnover Ratio", "body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How effective are credit granting and collection activities?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → An answer to this question is provided by the receivables turnover ratio, which is computed as follows:\n\nInterpretations in General → The receivables turnover ratio reflects how many times average accounts receivable are recorded and collected during the period.\n\nThe higher the ratio, the faster the collection of receivables.\n\nNet Credit Sales\n\nAverage Net Accounts Receivable\n\nReceivables Turnover Ratio\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How effective are credit granting and collection activities?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → An answer to this question is provided by the receivables turnover ratio, which is computed as follows:\n\nInterpretations in General → The receivables turnover ratio reflects how many times average accounts receivable are recorded and collected during the period.\n\nThe higher the ratio, the faster the collection of receivables.\n\nNet Credit Sales\n\nAverage Net Accounts Receivable\n\nReceivables Turnover Ratio\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 35}], "format": "plain", "slides": [35], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t26", "title": "Internal Control and Management Responsibility", "body": "The term internal control refers to the process by which a company’s board of directors, audit committee, management, and other personnel provide reasonable assurance that the accounting system minimizes the risk of both types of fraud, either misappropriation of assets or material misstatement of reported financial information.\n\nThe internal control system must assure both the effectiveness and efficiency of the company’s operations, and its compliance with all laws and regulations.\n\nAccountants assess how well managers have developed a company’s system of internal control, which prevents and detects both fraud and inadvertent material misstatement of the company’s financial position.", "blocks": [{"body": "The term internal control refers to the process by which a company’s board of directors, audit committee, management, and other personnel provide reasonable assurance that the accounting system minimizes the risk of both types of fraud, either misappropriation of assets or material misstatement of reported financial information.\n\nThe internal control system must assure both the effectiveness and efficiency of the company’s operations, and its compliance with all laws and regulations.\n\nAccountants assess how well managers have developed a company’s system of internal control, which prevents and detects both fraud and inadvertent material misstatement of the company’s financial position.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 36}], "format": "plain", "slides": [36], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t27", "title": "Control over Accounts Receivable", "body": "To guard against the extending of credit to non-worthy customers, the following practices can help minimize bad debts:\n\nRequire approval of customers’ credit history by a person independent of the sales and collection functions\n\nMonitor the age of accounts receivable periodically, and contact customers with overdue payments\n\nReward both sales and collection personnel for speedy collections so that they work as a team.", "blocks": [{"body": "To guard against the extending of credit to non-worthy customers, the following practices can help minimize bad debts:\n\nRequire approval of customers’ credit history by a person independent of the sales and collection functions\n\nMonitor the age of accounts receivable periodically, and contact customers with overdue payments\n\nReward both sales and collection personnel for speedy collections so that they work as a team.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 37}], "format": "plain", "slides": [37], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t28", "title": "Cash and Cash Equivalents", "body": "Cash is defined as money or any instrument that banks will accept for deposit and immediate credit to the company’s account, such as a cheque, money order, or bank draft.\n\nCash is usually divided into three categories: cash on hand, cash deposited in banks, and other instruments that meet the definition of cash.\n\nCash equivalents are as short-term, highly liquid investments that are readily convertible to known amounts of cash and that are subject to an insignificant risk of changes in value.\n\nTypical instruments included as cash equivalents are bank certificates of deposit, and Treasury bills issued by the government to finance its activities.", "blocks": [{"body": "Cash is defined as money or any instrument that banks will accept for deposit and immediate credit to the company’s account, such as a cheque, money order, or bank draft.\n\nCash is usually divided into three categories: cash on hand, cash deposited in banks, and other instruments that meet the definition of cash.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 38}, {"body": "Cash equivalents are as short-term, highly liquid investments that are readily convertible to known amounts of cash and that are subject to an insignificant risk of changes in value.\n\nTypical instruments included as cash equivalents are bank certificates of deposit, and Treasury bills issued by the government to finance its activities.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 39}], "format": "plain", "slides": [38, 39], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t29", "title": "Cash Management", "body": "Cash Management Procedures include:\n\nAccurate accounting so that reports of cash flows and balances may be prepared.\n\nControls to ensure that enough cash is on hand to meet (a) current operating needs, (b) maturing liabilities, and (c) unexpected emergencies.\n\nPrevention of the accumulation of excess amounts of idle cash. Idle cash earns no revenue; therefore, it is often invested in securities to earn revenue (return) until it is needed for operations.", "blocks": [{"body": "Cash Management Procedures include:\n\nAccurate accounting so that reports of cash flows and balances may be prepared.\n\nControls to ensure that enough cash is on hand to meet (a) current operating needs, (b) maturing liabilities, and (c) unexpected emergencies.\n\nPrevention of the accumulation of excess amounts of idle cash. Idle cash earns no revenue; therefore, it is often invested in securities to earn revenue (return) until it is needed for operations.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 40}], "format": "plain", "slides": [40], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t30", "title": "Internal Control of Cash", "body": "Because cash is the asset most vulnerable to theft and fraud, a significant number of internal control procedures should focus on cash.\n\nEffective internal control of cash should include the following:\n\nSeparation of duties related to cash handling and recordkeeping.\n\nPrescribed policies and procedures.", "blocks": [{"body": "Because cash is the asset most vulnerable to theft and fraud, a significant number of internal control procedures should focus on cash.\n\nEffective internal control of cash should include the following:\n\nSeparation of duties related to cash handling and recordkeeping.\n\nPrescribed policies and procedures.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 41}], "format": "plain", "slides": [41], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t31", "title": "Separation of Duties", "body": "Separation of duties related to cash handling and recordkeeping:\n\nComplete separation of the tasks of receiving cash and disbursing cash ensures that the individual responsible for depositing cash has no authority to sign cheques.\n\nComplete separation of the procedures of accounting for cash receipts and cash disbursements ensures, for example, that those handling sales returns do not create fictitious returns to conceal cash shortages.\n\nComplete separation of the physical handling of cash and all phases of the accounting function ensures that those either receiving or paying cash have no authority to make accounting entries.", "blocks": [{"body": "Separation of duties related to cash handling and recordkeeping:\n\nComplete separation of the tasks of receiving cash and disbursing cash ensures that the individual responsible for depositing cash has no authority to sign cheques.\n\nComplete separation of the procedures of accounting for cash receipts and cash disbursements ensures, for example, that those handling sales returns do not create fictitious returns to conceal cash shortages.\n\nComplete separation of the physical handling of cash and all phases of the accounting function ensures that those either receiving or paying cash have no authority to make accounting entries.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 42}], "format": "plain", "slides": [42], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t32", "title": "Separation of Duties Contributes to Strong Internal Control", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch5-image4.png", "alt": "Separation of Duties Contributes to Strong Internal Control — original slide 43"}], "slide": 43}], "format": "plain", "slides": [43], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t33", "title": "Typical Internal Controls for Cash", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["Internal Control Component", " ", "Prescribed Policies or Procedures"], ["Cash budget", " ", "Prepare a monthly forecast of cash receipts, disbursements, and balances for the year, and require that managers document and justify any deviations from the budget each month. This is important in order to detect differences quickly and remedy them as soon as possible."], ["Cash receipts", " ", "Prepare a listing of cash receipts on a daily basis. In practice, this often takes the form of cash register receipts or a descriptive list of incoming cheques. Require that all cash receipts be deposited in a bank daily. Keep any cash on hand under strict control. In so doing, the amount of actual cash accessible is kept to a minimum each day and the loss from theft is minimized."], ["Cash payments", " ", "Require separate approval of the purchases and other expenditures and separate approval of the actual cash payments. Assign the cash-payment approval and the actual cheque-signing responsibilities to different individuals. Use pre-numbered cheques and pay special attention to payments by electronic funds transfers, since the bank does not process controlled documents (cheques). Once in place, this procedure will require at least four employees to collude to misappropriate funds (steal cash) from the company."], ["Independent internal verification", " ", "Require comparison, by an independent supervisor, of cash receipts to bank deposits and of cheques issued to invoices. Require monthly reconciliation of bank accounts with the cash accounts on the company’s books (discussed in detail in the next section). This is important in order to detect differences quickly and remedy them as soon as possible."], ["Rotation of duties", " ", "Require that employees take vacations, and rotate their duties. Any economic crime already under way will be interrupted by an extended absence of a perpetrator; this provides an opportunity for others to detect the crime."]]], "images": [], "slide": 44}], "format": "plain", "slides": [44], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t34", "title": "Reconciliation of the Cash Accounts and the Bank Statements", "body": "Proper use of the bank accounts of a business can be an important internal control procedure for cash.\n\nEvery month, the bank provides the company (the depositor) with a bank statement.\n\nThe bank statement and the online banking site list:\n\nEach paper or electronic deposit recorded by the bank during the period,\n\nEach paper or electronic cheque cleared by the bank during the period,\n\nThe bank charges or deductions (such as service charges) made directly to the company’s account by the bank, and\n\nThe balance in the company’s account.", "blocks": [{"body": "Proper use of the bank accounts of a business can be an important internal control procedure for cash.\n\nEvery month, the bank provides the company (the depositor) with a bank statement.\n\nThe bank statement and the online banking site list:\n\nEach paper or electronic deposit recorded by the bank during the period,\n\nEach paper or electronic cheque cleared by the bank during the period,\n\nThe bank charges or deductions (such as service charges) made directly to the company’s account by the bank, and\n\nThe balance in the company’s account.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 45}], "format": "plain", "slides": [45], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t35", "title": "Need for Reconciliation", "body": "A bank reconciliation is a schedule that explains the difference between the ending cash balance in the company’s records and the ending cash balance reported by the bank on the monthly bank statement (or on the online banking site).\n\nA bank reconciliation should be completed for each separate chequing account at the end of each month.\n\nUsually, the ending cash balance as shown on the bank statement does not agree with the ending cash balance shown by the related cash ledger account on the books of the company.", "blocks": [{"body": "A bank reconciliation is a schedule that explains the difference between the ending cash balance in the company’s records and the ending cash balance reported by the bank on the monthly bank statement (or on the online banking site).\n\nA bank reconciliation should be completed for each separate chequing account at the end of each month.\n\nUsually, the ending cash balance as shown on the bank statement does not agree with the ending cash balance shown by the related cash ledger account on the books of the company.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 46}], "format": "plain", "slides": [46], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t36", "title": "Bank Reconciliation", "body": "The flow of documents that have not reached either the company or the bank by the end of the accounting period is illustrated below:\n\nMost common causes of differences between the ending bank balance and the ending book balance of cash are as follows:\n\nOutstanding cheques\n\nDeposits in transit\n\nNSF cheques\n\nBank service charges\n\nInterest\n\nErrors", "blocks": [{"body": "The flow of documents that have not reached either the company or the bank by the end of the accounting period is illustrated below:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch5-image5.png", "alt": "Bank Reconciliation — original slide 47"}], "slide": 47}, {"body": "Most common causes of differences between the ending bank balance and the ending book balance of cash are as follows:\n\nOutstanding cheques\n\nDeposits in transit\n\nNSF cheques\n\nBank service charges\n\nInterest\n\nErrors", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 48}], "format": "plain", "slides": [47, 48], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t37", "title": "Bank Reconciliation Illustrated", "body": "The general format for the bank reconciliation is:", "blocks": [{"body": "The general format for the bank reconciliation is:", "tables": [[["Ending cash balance per books", " ", "$xxx", " ", "Ending cash balance per bank statement", " ", "$xxx"], ["+ Interest paid by bank", " ", "xx", " ", "+ Deposits in transit", " ", "xx"], ["− NSF cheques/Service charges", " ", "xx", " ", "− Outstanding cheques", " ", "xx"], ["± Company errors                       ", " ", "    xx", " ", "± Bank errors                                              ", " ", "    xx"], ["Ending correct cash balance", " ", "$xxx", " ", "Ending correct cash balance", " ", "$xxx"]]], "images": [], "slide": 49}], "format": "plain", "slides": [49], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t38", "title": "Bank Reconciliation", "body": "Steps to follow in preparing the bank reconciliation:\n\nIdentify the outstanding cheques\n\nIdentify the deposits in transit.\n\nIdentify bank charges and credits not recorded on the company’s books.\n\nDetermine the impact of errors.\n\nNote: all of the additions and deductions on the company’s-books side of the reconciliation need journal entries to update the cash account. The additions and deductions on the bank-statement side do not need journal entries because they will work out automatically when they clear the bank.", "blocks": [{"body": "Steps to follow in preparing the bank reconciliation:\n\nIdentify the outstanding cheques\n\nIdentify the deposits in transit.\n\nIdentify bank charges and credits not recorded on the company’s books.\n\nDetermine the impact of errors.\n\nNote: all of the additions and deductions on the company’s-books side of the reconciliation need journal entries to update the cash account. The additions and deductions on the bank-statement side do not need journal entries because they will work out automatically when they clear the bank.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 50}], "format": "plain", "slides": [50], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}, {"id": "c5-t39", "title": "Appendix 5A: Recording Discounts and Returns", "body": "The five-step model of revenue recognition illustrated in the chapter consists of five steps:\n\nIdentify the contract between the company and customer.\n\nIdentify the performance obligations (promised goods and services).\n\nDetermine the transaction price.\n\nAllocate the transaction price to the performance obligations.\n\nRecognize revenue when each performance obligation is satisfied (or over time if a service is provided over time).\n\nAll five steps must be satisfied before revenue can be recognized.\n\nThis appendix considers three separate sales transactions involving:\n\nCredit card sales to customers\n\nCredit sales to businesses\n\nSales returns\n\nThe appendix goes through these three separate sales transactions and shows the journal entries to record them.", "blocks": [{"body": "The five-step model of revenue recognition illustrated in the chapter consists of five steps:\n\nIdentify the contract between the company and customer.\n\nIdentify the performance obligations (promised goods and services).\n\nDetermine the transaction price.\n\nAllocate the transaction price to the performance obligations.\n\nRecognize revenue when each performance obligation is satisfied (or over time if a service is provided over time).\n\nAll five steps must be satisfied before revenue can be recognized.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 51}, {"body": "This appendix considers three separate sales transactions involving:\n\nCredit card sales to customers\n\nCredit sales to businesses\n\nSales returns\n\nThe appendix goes through these three separate sales transactions and shows the journal entries to record them.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 52}], "format": "plain", "slides": [51, 52], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 5 slides"}], "deck": "sources/chapter-5.pptx"}, {"id": 6, "title": "Reporting and Interpreting Cost of Sales and Inventory", "topics": [{"id": "c6-t1", "title": "Understanding the Business", "body": "Primary Goals of Inventory Management:\n\nProvides sufficient quantities of high-quality inventory.\n\nMinimizes the costs of carrying inventory.\n\nRoles of the Accounting System:\n\nProvides accurate information\n\nProvides up-to-date information\n\nProvides information to help protect and control assets", "blocks": [{"body": "Primary Goals of Inventory Management:\n\nProvides sufficient quantities of high-quality inventory.\n\nMinimizes the costs of carrying inventory.\n\nRoles of the Accounting System:\n\nProvides accurate information\n\nProvides up-to-date information\n\nProvides information to help protect and control assets", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 4}], "format": "plain", "slides": [4], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t2", "title": "Inventory", "body": "Inventory is a tangible asset that is either:\n\nHeld for sale in the ordinary course of business or\n\nUsed in the process to produce goods for sale or render services.\n\nInventory is reported on the statement of financial position as a current asset, because it is normally used or converted into cash within one year or within the next operating cycle of the business, whichever is longer.\n\nThe types of inventory normally held depend on the characteristics of the business.", "blocks": [{"body": "Inventory is a tangible asset that is either:\n\nHeld for sale in the ordinary course of business or\n\nUsed in the process to produce goods for sale or render services.\n\nInventory is reported on the statement of financial position as a current asset, because it is normally used or converted into cash within one year or within the next operating cycle of the business, whichever is longer.\n\nThe types of inventory normally held depend on the characteristics of the business.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 5}], "format": "plain", "slides": [5], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t3", "title": "Items Included in Inventory", "body": "Merchandisers hold Merchandise inventory.\n\nThese are finished goods (or merchandise) held for resale in the ordinary course of business. The goods are usually acquired by the merchandiser in a finished condition and are ready for sale without further processing.\n\nManufacturing businesses hold three types of inventory:\n\nRaw materials inventory\n\nWork-in-process inventory\n\nFinished goods inventory", "blocks": [{"body": "Merchandisers hold Merchandise inventory.\n\nThese are finished goods (or merchandise) held for resale in the ordinary course of business. The goods are usually acquired by the merchandiser in a finished condition and are ready for sale without further processing.\n\nManufacturing businesses hold three types of inventory:\n\nRaw materials inventory\n\nWork-in-process inventory\n\nFinished goods inventory", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 6}], "format": "plain", "slides": [6], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t4", "title": "Manufacturing Types of Inventory", "body": "Raw materials inventory: This consists of items acquired by purchase, growth (such as food products), or extraction (natural resources) for processing into finished goods. Such items are included in raw materials inventory until used, at which point they become part of work-in-process inventory.\n\nWork-in-process inventory: This includes goods in the process of being manufactured but not yet complete. When complete, work-in-process inventory becomes finished goods inventory.\n\nFinished goods inventory: This consists of manufactured goods that are complete and available for sale.", "blocks": [{"body": "Raw materials inventory: This consists of items acquired by purchase, growth (such as food products), or extraction (natural resources) for processing into finished goods. Such items are included in raw materials inventory until used, at which point they become part of work-in-process inventory.\n\nWork-in-process inventory: This includes goods in the process of being manufactured but not yet complete. When complete, work-in-process inventory becomes finished goods inventory.\n\nFinished goods inventory: This consists of manufactured goods that are complete and available for sale.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 7}], "format": "plain", "slides": [7], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t5", "title": "Environmental, Social, and Governance Reporting", "body": "IFRS detail the type of depiction of sustainability-related information as a neutral and faithful representation of its sustainability-related financial information.\n\nThe information must be a sufficient basis on which users can assess the implications of sustainability-related risks and opportunities on the entity’s enterprise value.", "blocks": [{"body": "IFRS detail the type of depiction of sustainability-related information as a neutral and faithful representation of its sustainability-related financial information.\n\nThe information must be a sufficient basis on which users can assess the implications of sustainability-related risks and opportunities on the entity’s enterprise value.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 8}], "format": "plain", "slides": [8], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t6", "title": "Costs Included in Inventory Purchases", "body": "Goods in inventory are initially recorded at cost.\n\nThe primary basis of accounting for inventory is cash-equivalent cost, which is either the price paid or consideration given to acquire an asset.\n\nInventory cost includes the sum of the costs incurred in bringing an article to a usable or saleable condition and location.\n\nAny purchase returns and allowances or purchase discounts taken are subtracted.", "blocks": [{"body": "Goods in inventory are initially recorded at cost.\n\nThe primary basis of accounting for inventory is cash-equivalent cost, which is either the price paid or consideration given to acquire an asset.\n\nInventory cost includes the sum of the costs incurred in bringing an article to a usable or saleable condition and location.\n\nAny purchase returns and allowances or purchase discounts taken are subtracted.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 9}], "format": "plain", "slides": [9], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t7", "title": "Flow of Inventory Costs", "body": "Direct labour cost represents the earnings of employees who work directly on the products being manufactured.\n\nFactory overhead costs include all other manufacturing costs that are not raw material or direct labour costs.\n\nThere are three stages to inventory cost flows for both merchandisers and manufacturers.\n\nThe first involves purchasing and/or production activities.\n\nThe second stage, these activities result in additions to inventory accounts on the statement of financial position.\n\nThe third stage, the inventory items are sold and the amounts become cost of sales on the statement of earnings.", "blocks": [{"body": "Direct labour cost represents the earnings of employees who work directly on the products being manufactured.\n\nFactory overhead costs include all other manufacturing costs that are not raw material or direct labour costs.\n\nThere are three stages to inventory cost flows for both merchandisers and manufacturers.\n\nThe first involves purchasing and/or production activities.\n\nThe second stage, these activities result in additions to inventory accounts on the statement of financial position.\n\nThe third stage, the inventory items are sold and the amounts become cost of sales on the statement of earnings.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 10}], "format": "plain", "slides": [10], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t8", "title": "Flow of Inventory Costs – Exhibit 6.2", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch6-image2.png", "alt": "Flow of Inventory Costs – Exhibit 6.2 — original slide 11"}], "slide": 11}], "format": "plain", "slides": [11], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t9", "title": "Cost of Sales Equation", "body": "Cost of sales (COS) is directly related to sales revenue.\n\nSales revenue during an accounting period is the number of units sold multiplied by the sales price.\n\nCost of sales is the same number of units, multiplied by their unit costs.\n\nIt includes all costs of the merchandise or the finished goods sold during the period.\n\nEach accounting period is started with a stock of merchandise inventory called beginning inventory (BI).\n\nDuring the accounting period, new purchases (P) are added to inventory.\n\nThe sum of the cost of beginning inventory and the cost of purchases (or additions to its merchandise inventory) is the cost of goods available for sale during that period.\n\nWhat remains unsold at the end of the period becomes ending inventory (EI) on the statement of financial position.\n\nThe portion of the cost of goods available for sale that are actually sold becomes cost of sales on the statement of earnings.\n\nThe ending inventory for one accounting period then becomes the beginning inventory for the next period.\n\nThe relationships between these various amounts are brought together in the cost of sales equation:\n\nBI + P − EI = COS\n\nBeginning inventory\n\n+ Purchases of merchandise during the year\n\nCost of goods available for sale\n\n-\tEnding inventory\n\nCost of sales", "blocks": [{"body": "Cost of sales (COS) is directly related to sales revenue.\n\nSales revenue during an accounting period is the number of units sold multiplied by the sales price.\n\nCost of sales is the same number of units, multiplied by their unit costs.\n\nIt includes all costs of the merchandise or the finished goods sold during the period.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 12}, {"body": "Each accounting period is started with a stock of merchandise inventory called beginning inventory (BI).\n\nDuring the accounting period, new purchases (P) are added to inventory.\n\nThe sum of the cost of beginning inventory and the cost of purchases (or additions to its merchandise inventory) is the cost of goods available for sale during that period.\n\nWhat remains unsold at the end of the period becomes ending inventory (EI) on the statement of financial position.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 13}, {"body": "The portion of the cost of goods available for sale that are actually sold becomes cost of sales on the statement of earnings.\n\nThe ending inventory for one accounting period then becomes the beginning inventory for the next period.\n\nThe relationships between these various amounts are brought together in the cost of sales equation:\n\nBI + P − EI = COS", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 14}, {"body": "Beginning inventory\n\n+ Purchases of merchandise during the year\n\nCost of goods available for sale\n\n-\tEnding inventory\n\nCost of sales", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 15}], "format": "plain", "slides": [12, 13, 14, 15], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t10", "title": "Nature of Cost of Sales for Merchandise Inventory", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch6-image3.png", "alt": "Nature of Cost of Sales for Merchandise Inventory — original slide 16"}], "slide": 16}], "format": "plain", "slides": [16], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t11", "title": "Perpetual and Periodic Inventory Systems", "body": "To calculate cost of sales, three amounts must be known:\n\nBeginning inventory;\n\nPurchases of merchandise, or additions to finished goods, during the period; and\n\nEnding inventory.\n\nThe amount of purchases for the period is always accumulated in the accounting system.\n\nThe cost of sales and cost of ending inventory can be determined by using one of two different inventory systems: perpetual and periodic.", "blocks": [{"body": "To calculate cost of sales, three amounts must be known:\n\nBeginning inventory;\n\nPurchases of merchandise, or additions to finished goods, during the period; and\n\nEnding inventory.\n\nThe amount of purchases for the period is always accumulated in the accounting system.\n\nThe cost of sales and cost of ending inventory can be determined by using one of two different inventory systems: perpetual and periodic.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 17}], "format": "plain", "slides": [17], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t12", "title": "Perpetual Inventory System", "body": "In a perpetual inventory system, purchase transactions are directly recorded in an inventory account.\n\nSimultaneously, when each sale is recorded, a companion cost of sales entry is made, decreasing inventory and recording cost of sales.\n\nAs a result, information on cost of sales and ending inventory is available on a continuous (perpetual) basis.\n\nA detailed record is maintained for each type of merchandise stocked, showing:\n\nUnits and cost of the beginning inventory,\n\nUnits and cost of each purchase,\n\nUnits and cost of the goods for each sale, and\n\nUnits and cost of the goods on hand at any point in time.\n\nThis up-to-date record is maintained on a transaction-by-transaction basis and is usually necessary for purchasing, manufacturing, and distribution decisions.", "blocks": [{"body": "In a perpetual inventory system, purchase transactions are directly recorded in an inventory account.\n\nSimultaneously, when each sale is recorded, a companion cost of sales entry is made, decreasing inventory and recording cost of sales.\n\nAs a result, information on cost of sales and ending inventory is available on a continuous (perpetual) basis.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 18}, {"body": "A detailed record is maintained for each type of merchandise stocked, showing:\n\nUnits and cost of the beginning inventory,\n\nUnits and cost of each purchase,\n\nUnits and cost of the goods for each sale, and\n\nUnits and cost of the goods on hand at any point in time.\n\nThis up-to-date record is maintained on a transaction-by-transaction basis and is usually necessary for purchasing, manufacturing, and distribution decisions.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 19}], "format": "plain", "slides": [18, 19], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t13", "title": "Periodic Inventory System", "body": "Under the periodic inventory system, companies do not maintain an ongoing record of inventory during the year; rather, an actual physical count of the goods remaining on hand is required at the end of each period.\n\nThe number of units of each type of merchandise on hand is multiplied by their unit cost to compute the total cost of the ending inventory.\n\nCost of sales is calculated by using the cost of sales equation.\n\nBecause the amount of inventory is not known until the end of the period when the physical inventory count is taken, the cost of sales cannot be determined reliably until the inventory count is completed.\n\nInventory purchases are debited to a temporary account called purchases.\n\nRevenues are recorded at the time of each sale.\n\nHowever, the cost of sales is not recorded until after the inventory count is completed.\n\nAt all other times, companies using a periodic system must estimate the value of inventory on hand.", "blocks": [{"body": "Under the periodic inventory system, companies do not maintain an ongoing record of inventory during the year; rather, an actual physical count of the goods remaining on hand is required at the end of each period.\n\nThe number of units of each type of merchandise on hand is multiplied by their unit cost to compute the total cost of the ending inventory.\n\nCost of sales is calculated by using the cost of sales equation.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 20}, {"body": "Because the amount of inventory is not known until the end of the period when the physical inventory count is taken, the cost of sales cannot be determined reliably until the inventory count is completed.\n\nInventory purchases are debited to a temporary account called purchases.\n\nRevenues are recorded at the time of each sale.\n\nHowever, the cost of sales is not recorded until after the inventory count is completed.\n\nAt all other times, companies using a periodic system must estimate the value of inventory on hand.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 21}], "format": "plain", "slides": [20, 21], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t14", "title": "Recording Inventory Transactions in a Perpetual System", "body": "Sellers of merchandise or raw materials use a variety of methods to motivate their customers to buy the products they offer.\n\nThe principal methods include:\n\n(1) allowing customers to make purchases on account and pay later,\n\n(2) offering business customers discounts for early payment, and\n\n(3) allowing returns from all customers under specific circumstances.\n\nThe amount recorded for inventory purchases should include the invoice price to be paid plus other expenditures related to the purchase, such as import duties, transportation charges (freight-in), as well as inspection and preparation costs.\n\nAny purchase returns and allowances or purchase discounts taken are subtracted.", "blocks": [{"body": "Sellers of merchandise or raw materials use a variety of methods to motivate their customers to buy the products they offer.\n\nThe principal methods include:\n\n(1) allowing customers to make purchases on account and pay later,\n\n(2) offering business customers discounts for early payment, and\n\n(3) allowing returns from all customers under specific circumstances.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 22}, {"body": "The amount recorded for inventory purchases should include the invoice price to be paid plus other expenditures related to the purchase, such as import duties, transportation charges (freight-in), as well as inspection and preparation costs.\n\nAny purchase returns and allowances or purchase discounts taken are subtracted.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 23}], "format": "plain", "slides": [22, 23], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t15", "title": "Inventory Purchases", "body": "The point at which title (ownership) changes hands is determined by the shipping terms in the purchase contract, as explained in Chapter 5.\n\nWhen goods are shipped FOB (free on board) destination point, title changes hands on delivery at the buyer’s location, and the seller pays the shipping costs.\n\nWhen they are shipped FOB shipping point, title changes hands on delivery at the seller’s location, and the buyer pays for shipping.", "blocks": [{"body": "The point at which title (ownership) changes hands is determined by the shipping terms in the purchase contract, as explained in Chapter 5.\n\nWhen goods are shipped FOB (free on board) destination point, title changes hands on delivery at the buyer’s location, and the seller pays the shipping costs.\n\nWhen they are shipped FOB shipping point, title changes hands on delivery at the seller’s location, and the buyer pays for shipping.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 24}], "format": "plain", "slides": [24], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t16", "title": "Purchase Returns and Allowances", "body": "Goods purchased may be returned to the supplier if they do not meet specifications, arrive in damaged condition, or are otherwise unsatisfactory.\n\nAlternatively, the buyer may keep the unsatisfactory goods and benefit from a cost reduction or allowance.\n\nThe effect of purchases returns or allowances on the cost of purchases must be measured.\n\nThe buyer normally receives a cash refund or a reduction in the liability owed to the supplier.\n\nPurchase returns and allowances are treated as a reduction in the cost of inventory purchases associated with unsatisfactory goods.", "blocks": [{"body": "Goods purchased may be returned to the supplier if they do not meet specifications, arrive in damaged condition, or are otherwise unsatisfactory.\n\nAlternatively, the buyer may keep the unsatisfactory goods and benefit from a cost reduction or allowance.\n\nThe effect of purchases returns or allowances on the cost of purchases must be measured.\n\nThe buyer normally receives a cash refund or a reduction in the liability owed to the supplier.\n\nPurchase returns and allowances are treated as a reduction in the cost of inventory purchases associated with unsatisfactory goods.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 25}], "format": "plain", "slides": [25], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t17", "title": "Purchase Discounts", "body": "Cash discounts must be accounted for by both the buyer and the seller (seller was discussed in Chapter 5).\n\nWhen inventory is purchased on credit, terms such as 2/10, n/30 are sometimes specified. These terms mean that if payment is made within 10 days from the date of purchase, the buyer can deduct a 2 percent purchase discount for prompt payment.\n\nIf payment is not made within the discount period, the amount owed (net of purchases returns and allowances) is due 30 days after the date of purchase.", "blocks": [{"body": "Cash discounts must be accounted for by both the buyer and the seller (seller was discussed in Chapter 5).\n\nWhen inventory is purchased on credit, terms such as 2/10, n/30 are sometimes specified. These terms mean that if payment is made within 10 days from the date of purchase, the buyer can deduct a 2 percent purchase discount for prompt payment.\n\nIf payment is not made within the discount period, the amount owed (net of purchases returns and allowances) is due 30 days after the date of purchase.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 26}], "format": "plain", "slides": [26], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t18", "title": "Inventory Costing Methods", "body": "There are three generally accepted inventory costing methods available to determine the cost of sales:\n\nSpecific identification\n\nFirst-in, first-out (FIFO)\n\nWeighted average\n\nThese methods are alternative ways to assign the total cost of goods available for sale between (1) ending inventory and (2) cost of sales.\n\nInternational Accounting Standard 2 requires only that the inventory costing method used be rational and systematic; it should be the one that provides a faithful representation of expenses to correspond to the revenues earned during the period.", "blocks": [{"body": "There are three generally accepted inventory costing methods available to determine the cost of sales:\n\nSpecific identification\n\nFirst-in, first-out (FIFO)\n\nWeighted average\n\nThese methods are alternative ways to assign the total cost of goods available for sale between (1) ending inventory and (2) cost of sales.\n\nInternational Accounting Standard 2 requires only that the inventory costing method used be rational and systematic; it should be the one that provides a faithful representation of expenses to correspond to the revenues earned during the period.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 27}], "format": "plain", "slides": [27], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t19", "title": "Specific Identification Method", "body": "The specific identification method requires that the cost of each item sold be individually identified and recorded as cost of sales.\n\nIt cannot be used when units are identical because the method may be manipulated without electronic tracking of the units sold.\n\nIt is easy to affect the cost of sales and the ending inventory accounts by picking and choosing from among the several available unit costs. For this reason, IFRS prohibit the use of specific identification when there are large numbers of inventory items that are interchangeable, such as leather jackets of the same size and style.\n\nThe specific identification method is impractical when large quantities of similar items are kept in stock.\n\nIt is appropriate, however, when dealing with very expensive items, such as broadband telecommunications systems, aircraft, yachts, fine art objects, buildings, luxury cars, and fine jewellery, because each item tends to differ from the other items.\n\nTechnology assists in keeping track of the purchase cost of each item. This is done by either (1) coding the purchase cost on each unit before placing it in stock or (2) keeping a separate record of the unit and identifying it with a serial number.", "blocks": [{"body": "The specific identification method requires that the cost of each item sold be individually identified and recorded as cost of sales.\n\nIt cannot be used when units are identical because the method may be manipulated without electronic tracking of the units sold.\n\nIt is easy to affect the cost of sales and the ending inventory accounts by picking and choosing from among the several available unit costs. For this reason, IFRS prohibit the use of specific identification when there are large numbers of inventory items that are interchangeable, such as leather jackets of the same size and style.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 28}, {"body": "The specific identification method is impractical when large quantities of similar items are kept in stock.\n\nIt is appropriate, however, when dealing with very expensive items, such as broadband telecommunications systems, aircraft, yachts, fine art objects, buildings, luxury cars, and fine jewellery, because each item tends to differ from the other items.\n\nTechnology assists in keeping track of the purchase cost of each item. This is done by either (1) coding the purchase cost on each unit before placing it in stock or (2) keeping a separate record of the unit and identifying it with a serial number.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 29}], "format": "plain", "slides": [28, 29], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t20", "title": "Cost Flow Assumptions", "body": "Most inventory items are accounted for by using one of two cost flow assumptions.\n\nThe choice of an inventory costing method is not based on the physical flow of goods on and off the shelves.\n\nThe first-in, first-out (FIFO) method assumes that the earliest goods purchased (the first ones in) are the first ones sold (the first ones out) and the last ones purchased remain in ending inventory.\n\nFIFO allocates the oldest unit costs to cost of sales and the newest unit costs to ending inventory.", "blocks": [{"body": "Most inventory items are accounted for by using one of two cost flow assumptions.\n\nThe choice of an inventory costing method is not based on the physical flow of goods on and off the shelves.\n\nThe first-in, first-out (FIFO) method assumes that the earliest goods purchased (the first ones in) are the first ones sold (the first ones out) and the last ones purchased remain in ending inventory.\n\nFIFO allocates the oldest unit costs to cost of sales and the newest unit costs to ending inventory.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 30}], "format": "plain", "slides": [30], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t21", "title": "FIFO Inventory Flows – Perpetual Inventory System; Exhibit 6.5", "body": "\n\n", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch6-image4.png", "alt": "FIFO Inventory Flows – Perpetual Inventory System; Exhibit 6.5 — original slide 31"}], "slide": 31}, {"body": "", "tables": [[["Cost of Sales Calculation (FIFO Perpetual)", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""], ["Cost of Jackets Purchased and Sold", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""], ["Date", "", "", "Units", "", "Cost", "", "Total"], ["January 1", "Beginning inventory", "Oldest cost", "800", "×", "$400", "=", "$320,000"], ["January 31", "Cost of sales", "", "(600)", "×", "$400", "=", "(240,000)"], ["", "Cost of remaining inventory", "", "200", "×", "$400", "=", "80,000"], ["February 5", "Purchase", "", "800", "×", "$420", "=", "336,000"], ["", "Cost of goods available for sale", "", "1,000", "", "", "", "416,000"], ["February 28", "Cost of sales (900 units)", "", "(200)", "×", "$400", "=", "(80,000)"], ["", "", "", "(700)", "×", "$420", "=", "(294,000)"], ["", "Cost of remaining inventory", "", "100", "×", "$420", "=", "42,000"], ["March 10", "Purchase", "Newest cost", "900", "×", "$440", "=", "396,000"], ["", "Cost of goods available for sale", "", "1,000", "", "", "", "438,000"], ["March 31", "Cost of sales (200 units)", "", "(100)", "×", "$420", "=", "(42,000)"], ["", "", "", "(100)", "×", "$440", "=", "(44,000)"], ["", "Ending inventory", "", "800", "×", "$440", "=", "352,000"], ["", "Cost of sales*", "", "", "", "", "", "$700,000"], ["*Cost of sales = $240,000 + $80,000 + $294,000 + $42,000 + $44,000 = $700,000", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""]]], "images": [], "slide": 32}], "format": "plain", "slides": [31, 32], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t22", "title": "Weighted-Average Cost Method", "body": "The weighted-average cost method requires calculating the weighted-average unit cost of the goods available for sale.\n\nWhen a unit is sold, the average cost of each unit  in inventory is assigned to cost of sales.\n\nEnding Inventory\n\nUnits in Ending Inventory  ×  Average Cost per Unit\n\nCost of Sales\n\nUnits Sold  ×  Average Cost per Unit\n\nCost of Goods Available for Sale\n\nNumber of Units Available for Sale\n\n÷", "blocks": [{"body": "The weighted-average cost method requires calculating the weighted-average unit cost of the goods available for sale.\n\nWhen a unit is sold, the average cost of each unit  in inventory is assigned to cost of sales.\n\nEnding Inventory\n\nUnits in Ending Inventory  ×  Average Cost per Unit\n\nCost of Sales\n\nUnits Sold  ×  Average Cost per Unit\n\nCost of Goods Available for Sale\n\nNumber of Units Available for Sale\n\n÷", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 33}], "format": "plain", "slides": [33], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t23", "title": "Average Cost Inventory Flows – Perpetual Inventory System;", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["Cost of Sales Calculation (Avg Cost Perpetual) ", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""], ["Cost of jackets purchased and sold", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""], ["Date", " ", " ", "Units", " ", "Cost", " ", "Total"], ["January 1", "Beginning inventory", "", "   800 ", "×", "$400 ", "=", "$320,000  "], ["January 31", "Cost of sales", " ", "   (600)", "×", "$400 ", "=", "(240,000)"], ["", "Cost of remaining inventory", " ", "   200 ", "×", "$400 ", "=", " 80,000"], ["February 5", "Purchase", " ", "    800 ", "×", "$420 ", "=", " 336,000 "], [" ", "Cost of goods available for sale", " ", "1,000 ", " ", "416 ", " ", " 416,000"], ["February 28", "Cost of sales (900 units)", " ", "   (900)", "×", "$416 ", "=", "  (374400)  "], ["", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""], [" ", "Cost of remaining inventory", " ", "   100 ", "×", "$416 ", "=", "     41,600  "], ["March 10", "Purchase", "", "    900  ", "×", "$440 ", "=", " 396,000 "], [" ", "Cost of goods available for sale", " ", "1,000 ", " ", "437.600 ", " ", " 437,600 "], ["March 31 ", "Cost of sales (200 units)", " ", "", "", "", "", ""], [" ", " ", " ", "    (200) ", "×", "$437.60 ", "=", "   (87,520)"], [" ", "Ending inventory", " ", "   800 ", "×", "$218.80 ", "=", "   350,080  "], [" ", "Cost of sales *", " ", " ", " ", " ", " ", "$701,920  "]]], "images": [], "slide": 34}], "format": "plain", "slides": [34], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t24", "title": "Financial Statement Effects of Inventory Costing Methods", "body": "Advantages of Methods:\n\nFirst-In, First-Out  Ending inventory approximates current replacement cost.\n\nWeighted Average  Smooths out price changes.", "blocks": [{"body": "Advantages of Methods:\n\nFirst-In, First-Out  Ending inventory approximates current replacement cost.\n\nWeighted Average  Smooths out price changes.", "tables": [[[" ", "Normal Financial Statement Effects of:", "", "", ""], [" ", "Rising Costs", "", "Declining Costs", ""], [" ", "FIFO", "Weighted Average", "FIFO", "Weighted Average"], ["Cost of sales", "Lower", "Higher", "Higher", "Lower"], ["Gross profit", "Higher", "Lower", "Lower", "Higher"], ["Net earnings", "Higher", "Lower", "Lower", "Higher"], ["Ending inventory", "Higher", "Lower", "Lower", "Higher"]]], "images": [], "slide": 35}], "format": "plain", "slides": [35], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t25", "title": "Consistency in Use of Inventory Costing Methods", "body": "Regardless of the physical flow of goods, a company can use either the weighted-average or the FIFO inventory costing method.\n\nCompanies are expected to select the most representationally faithful inventory costing method.\n\nThe cost of inventory is supposed to reflect most accurately the anticipated benefit that will eventually be realized from either the use or the sale of the inventory.\n\nTherefore, a company is not required to use the same inventory costing method for all inventory items. The justification arises from the pervasive principle of economic substance over form.\n\nTo enhance comparability, accounting rules require companies to apply their accounting methods on a consistent basis.\n\nA company is not permitted to use FIFO one period, weighted average the next, and then FIFO the next.\n\nA change in method is allowed only if the change will improve the measurement of financial results and financial position.\n\nChanging from one inventory costing method to another is a significant event. Such a change requires full disclosure of the reason for the change and the accounting effects.", "blocks": [{"body": "Regardless of the physical flow of goods, a company can use either the weighted-average or the FIFO inventory costing method.\n\nCompanies are expected to select the most representationally faithful inventory costing method.\n\nThe cost of inventory is supposed to reflect most accurately the anticipated benefit that will eventually be realized from either the use or the sale of the inventory.\n\nTherefore, a company is not required to use the same inventory costing method for all inventory items. The justification arises from the pervasive principle of economic substance over form.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 36}, {"body": "To enhance comparability, accounting rules require companies to apply their accounting methods on a consistent basis.\n\nA company is not permitted to use FIFO one period, weighted average the next, and then FIFO the next.\n\nA change in method is allowed only if the change will improve the measurement of financial results and financial position.\n\nChanging from one inventory costing method to another is a significant event. Such a change requires full disclosure of the reason for the change and the accounting effects.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 37}], "format": "plain", "slides": [36, 37], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t26", "title": "Managers’ Choice of Inventory Costing Methods", "body": "In principle, managers should choose the method that best reflects the company’s economic circumstances for financial reporting purposes.\n\nIn practice, most managers choose generally accepted accounting methods based on two factors:\n\nEffect on net earnings (managers prefer to report higher earnings for their companies).\n\nEffect on income taxes (managers prefer to pay the least amount of taxes allowed by law, as late as possible: the least–latest rule).\n\nBecause accounting standards and tax laws differ, management must also choose an inventory costing method to use on the company’s tax return (for tax purposes).\n\nIn general, most companies choose to use the same inventory costing method for both financial reporting and tax reporting purposes.", "blocks": [{"body": "In principle, managers should choose the method that best reflects the company’s economic circumstances for financial reporting purposes.\n\nIn practice, most managers choose generally accepted accounting methods based on two factors:\n\nEffect on net earnings (managers prefer to report higher earnings for their companies).\n\nEffect on income taxes (managers prefer to pay the least amount of taxes allowed by law, as late as possible: the least–latest rule).", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 38}, {"body": "Because accounting standards and tax laws differ, management must also choose an inventory costing method to use on the company’s tax return (for tax purposes).\n\nIn general, most companies choose to use the same inventory costing method for both financial reporting and tax reporting purposes.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 39}], "format": "plain", "slides": [38, 39], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t27", "title": "Valuation at Lower of Cost and Net Realizable Value", "body": "Ending inventory is reported at the lower of cost or net realizable value (LC&NRV).\n\nNet Realizable Value (NRV) is the expected sales price less estimated selling costs (e.g., repair and disposal costs).\n\nThis valuation rule is a departure from the cost principle and is particularly important for two types of companies:\n\n(1) high-technology companies, such as Apple, that manufacture goods for which the cost of production and the selling price are declining and\n\n(2) companies like Canada Goose that sell seasonal goods, the value of which drops dramatically at the end of each selling season.\n\nUnder LC&NRV, companies recognize a loss in the period in which the net realizable value of an item drops rather than in the period in which the item is sold.\n\nThe loss is the difference between the purchase cost and the net realizable value and is added to the cost of sales of the period.\n\nIf the NRV of the inventory items that were written down increases in a subsequent accounting period because of changed economic circumstances, then the amount of the write-down is reversed up to the original cost, so that the new carrying amount of the inventory is the lower of the cost and the revised net realizable value.\n\nNormally, a reversal of a previous write-down occurs when an inventory item that is carried at NRV is still on hand in a subsequent period when its selling price has increased.\n\nUnder IFRS, the LC&NRV rule must be applied to all inventories, item by item, regardless of the inventory costing methods that a company uses.", "blocks": [{"body": "Ending inventory is reported at the lower of cost or net realizable value (LC&NRV).\n\nNet Realizable Value (NRV) is the expected sales price less estimated selling costs (e.g., repair and disposal costs).\n\nThis valuation rule is a departure from the cost principle and is particularly important for two types of companies:\n\n(1) high-technology companies, such as Apple, that manufacture goods for which the cost of production and the selling price are declining and\n\n(2) companies like Canada Goose that sell seasonal goods, the value of which drops dramatically at the end of each selling season.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 40}, {"body": "Under LC&NRV, companies recognize a loss in the period in which the net realizable value of an item drops rather than in the period in which the item is sold.\n\nThe loss is the difference between the purchase cost and the net realizable value and is added to the cost of sales of the period.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 41}, {"body": "If the NRV of the inventory items that were written down increases in a subsequent accounting period because of changed economic circumstances, then the amount of the write-down is reversed up to the original cost, so that the new carrying amount of the inventory is the lower of the cost and the revised net realizable value.\n\nNormally, a reversal of a previous write-down occurs when an inventory item that is carried at NRV is still on hand in a subsequent period when its selling price has increased.\n\nUnder IFRS, the LC&NRV rule must be applied to all inventories, item by item, regardless of the inventory costing methods that a company uses.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 42}], "format": "plain", "slides": [40, 41, 42], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t28", "title": "Internal Control of Inventory", "body": "The following are the most important control features:\n\nSeparation of responsibilities for inventory accounting and physical handling of inventory.\n\nStorage of inventory in a manner that protects it from theft and damage.\n\nLimiting access to inventory to authorized employees.\n\nMaintaining modern and comprehensive accounting information systems that provide timely reports of sales and perpetual inventory records.\n\nComparing perpetual records to periodic physical counts of inventory.\n\nThe first three features protect inventory while the last two ensure the accuracy of reporting inventory costs.", "blocks": [{"body": "The following are the most important control features:\n\nSeparation of responsibilities for inventory accounting and physical handling of inventory.\n\nStorage of inventory in a manner that protects it from theft and damage.\n\nLimiting access to inventory to authorized employees.\n\nMaintaining modern and comprehensive accounting information systems that provide timely reports of sales and perpetual inventory records.\n\nComparing perpetual records to periodic physical counts of inventory.\n\nThe first three features protect inventory while the last two ensure the accuracy of reporting inventory costs.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 43}], "format": "plain", "slides": [43], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t29", "title": "Errors in Measuring Ending Inventory", "body": "As the cost of sales equation indicates, a direct relationship exists between the cost of ending inventory and the cost of sales, because items not in the ending inventory are assumed to have been sold.\n\nThe measurement of ending inventory quantities and costs affects both the statement of financial position (assets) and the statement of earnings (cost of sales, gross profit, and net earnings).\n\nThe measurement of ending inventory affects not only the net earnings for that period but also the net earnings for the next accounting period.\n\nThis two-period effect occurs because the ending inventory for one accounting period is the beginning inventory for the next.\n\nExhibit 6.8 shows how an error that understates the cost of ending inventory affects other elements of financial statements during the year of the error and the following year.\n\nAn error that overstates ending inventory would have exactly the opposite effects on the financial statement items shown in Exhibit 6.8 on the next slide.", "blocks": [{"body": "As the cost of sales equation indicates, a direct relationship exists between the cost of ending inventory and the cost of sales, because items not in the ending inventory are assumed to have been sold.\n\nThe measurement of ending inventory quantities and costs affects both the statement of financial position (assets) and the statement of earnings (cost of sales, gross profit, and net earnings).\n\nThe measurement of ending inventory affects not only the net earnings for that period but also the net earnings for the next accounting period.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 44}, {"body": "This two-period effect occurs because the ending inventory for one accounting period is the beginning inventory for the next.\n\nExhibit 6.8 shows how an error that understates the cost of ending inventory affects other elements of financial statements during the year of the error and the following year.\n\nAn error that overstates ending inventory would have exactly the opposite effects on the financial statement items shown in Exhibit 6.8 on the next slide.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 45}], "format": "plain", "slides": [44, 45], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t30", "title": "Errors in Measuring Ending Inventory – Exhibit 6.8", "body": "Effect of Understatement in Ending Inventory on Selected Financial Statement Items", "blocks": [{"body": "Effect of Understatement in Ending Inventory on Selected Financial Statement Items", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch6-image5.jpeg", "alt": "Errors in Measuring Ending Inventory – Exhibit 6.8 — original slide 46"}], "slide": 46}], "format": "plain", "slides": [46], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t31", "title": "Key Ratio Analysis – Inventory Turnover Ratio", "body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How efficient are inventory management activities?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The answer to this question is facilitated by the computation of the inventory turnover ratio as follows:\n\nThis ratio reflects how many times average inventory was produced and sold during the period.\n\nA higher ratio indicates that inventory moves more quickly thus reducing storage and obsolescence costs.\n\nCost of Sales\n\n=\n\nAverage Inventory\n\nInventory Turnover Ratio", "blocks": [{"body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How efficient are inventory management activities?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The answer to this question is facilitated by the computation of the inventory turnover ratio as follows:\n\nThis ratio reflects how many times average inventory was produced and sold during the period.\n\nA higher ratio indicates that inventory moves more quickly thus reducing storage and obsolescence costs.\n\nCost of Sales\n\n=\n\nAverage Inventory\n\nInventory Turnover Ratio", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 47}], "format": "plain", "slides": [47], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t32", "title": "Key Ratio Analysis – Average Days to Sell Inventory", "body": "This ratio reflects the average time in days it takes a company to produce and deliver inventory to its customers.\n\n365\n\nInventory Turnover\n\nAverage Days to Sell Inventory\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "This ratio reflects the average time in days it takes a company to produce and deliver inventory to its customers.\n\n365\n\nInventory Turnover\n\nAverage Days to Sell Inventory\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 48}], "format": "plain", "slides": [48], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t33", "title": "Inventory and Cash Flows", "body": "When companies expand production to meet increases in demand, this increases the amount of inventory reported on the statement of financial position.\n\nHowever, when companies overestimate demand for a product, they usually produce too many units of the slow-moving item.\n\nThis increases storage costs as well as the interest costs on short-term borrowings that finance the inventory. It may even lead to losses if the excess inventory cannot be sold at normal prices.\n\nThe statement of cash flows often provides the first sign of such problems.", "blocks": [{"body": "When companies expand production to meet increases in demand, this increases the amount of inventory reported on the statement of financial position.\n\nHowever, when companies overestimate demand for a product, they usually produce too many units of the slow-moving item.\n\nThis increases storage costs as well as the interest costs on short-term borrowings that finance the inventory. It may even lead to losses if the excess inventory cannot be sold at normal prices.\n\nThe statement of cash flows often provides the first sign of such problems.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 49}], "format": "plain", "slides": [49], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t34", "title": "Accounting Standards for Private Enterprises", "body": "The accounting standards related to measuring and reporting of inventories by Canadian private enterprises are very similar to those included in IAS 2—Inventories, which Canadian publicly accountable enterprises must use.\n\nAccounting Standards for Private Enterprises (ASPE) do not allow, however, the reversal of a write-down to inventory.", "blocks": [{"body": "The accounting standards related to measuring and reporting of inventories by Canadian private enterprises are very similar to those included in IAS 2—Inventories, which Canadian publicly accountable enterprises must use.\n\nAccounting Standards for Private Enterprises (ASPE) do not allow, however, the reversal of a write-down to inventory.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 50}], "format": "plain", "slides": [50], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t35", "title": "Appendix 6A: Cost Flow Assumptions – Periodic Inventory System", "body": "When a periodic inventory system is used, the cost of merchandise sold is not recorded every time a sale is made.\n\nInstead, the cost of all the goods sold during the period is recorded at year-end.\n\nThe cost of ending inventory is first determined based on an actual physical count of the number of units of each type of merchandise on hand, multiplied by their unit cost.\n\nThe cost of sales equation is then used to calculate the cost of sales.", "blocks": [{"body": "When a periodic inventory system is used, the cost of merchandise sold is not recorded every time a sale is made.\n\nInstead, the cost of all the goods sold during the period is recorded at year-end.\n\nThe cost of ending inventory is first determined based on an actual physical count of the number of units of each type of merchandise on hand, multiplied by their unit cost.\n\nThe cost of sales equation is then used to calculate the cost of sales.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 51}], "format": "plain", "slides": [51], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}, {"id": "c6-t36", "title": "Appendix 6A: Comparison of Perpetual and Periodic Inventory Systems: Exhibit 6A.3", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["Perpetual", "", "Periodic", ""], [" ", "Beginning inventory (carried over from prior period)", " ", "Beginning inventory (carried over from prior period)"], ["+", "Purchases for the period (accumulated in an  inventory  account)", "+", "Purchases for the period (accumulated in a  purchases  account)"], ["=", "Cost of goods available for sale", "=", "Cost of goods available for sale"], ["−", "Cost of sales (measured at every sale, based on perpetual record)", "−", "Ending inventory (measured at end of period, based on physical inventory count)"], ["=", "Ending inventory (perpetual record updated at every sale)", "=", "Cost of sales (computed as a residual amount)"], [" ", "EI = BI + PUR − COS", " ", "COS = BI + PUR − EI"]]], "images": [], "slide": 52}], "format": "plain", "slides": [52], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 6 slides"}], "deck": "sources/chapter-6.pptx"}, {"id": 7, "title": "Reporting and Interpreting Long-Lived Assets", "topics": [{"id": "c7-t1", "title": "Classification of Long-Lived Assets", "body": "The term long-lived assets is used to identify property, plant, equipment, intangible assets, and goodwill held for production, rental to others, or administrative purposes, as well as the development, construction, maintenance, or repair of other assets.\n\nLong-lived assets are listed as non-current assets on the statement of financial position.\n\nLong-lived assets include tangible assets, intangible assets, and goodwill.\n\nTangible assets (or fixed assets) can be touched because they have physical substance. This classification is most often called property, plant, and equipment.\n\nThe three kinds of tangible assets held for use in operations are as follows:\n\nLand\n\nBuildings, fixtures, and equipment\n\nNatural resources\n\nIntangible assets have no physical substance and frequently arise from intellectual effort and are known as intellectual property.  Examples include copyrights, patents, licences, trademarks, software, and subscription lists.\n\nGoodwill has no physical substance. It reflects the favourable reputation that a company has with its customers.\n\nGoodwill arises from factors such as customer confidence, reputation for good service and quality products, and financial standing. It is reported as an asset only when one business purchases another business.", "blocks": [{"body": "The term long-lived assets is used to identify property, plant, equipment, intangible assets, and goodwill held for production, rental to others, or administrative purposes, as well as the development, construction, maintenance, or repair of other assets.\n\nLong-lived assets are listed as non-current assets on the statement of financial position.\n\nLong-lived assets include tangible assets, intangible assets, and goodwill.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 4}, {"body": "Tangible assets (or fixed assets) can be touched because they have physical substance. This classification is most often called property, plant, and equipment.\n\nThe three kinds of tangible assets held for use in operations are as follows:\n\nLand\n\nBuildings, fixtures, and equipment\n\nNatural resources", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 5}, {"body": "Intangible assets have no physical substance and frequently arise from intellectual effort and are known as intellectual property.  Examples include copyrights, patents, licences, trademarks, software, and subscription lists.\n\nGoodwill has no physical substance. It reflects the favourable reputation that a company has with its customers.\n\nGoodwill arises from factors such as customer confidence, reputation for good service and quality products, and financial standing. It is reported as an asset only when one business purchases another business.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 6}], "format": "plain", "slides": [4, 5, 6], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t2", "title": "Key Ratio Analysis – Fixed Asset Turnover Ratio", "body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How effectively is management utilizing its property, plant, and equipment to generate revenues?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The fixed asset turnover ratio is useful in answering this question:\n\nThe fixed asset turnover ratio measures the sales dollars generated by each dollar of fixed assets used.\n\nA high ratio normally suggests effective management. An increasing ratio over time signals more-efficient use of fixed assets.\n\nFixed AssetTurnover Ratio\n\nNet Sales RevenueAverage Net Fixed Assets\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How effectively is management utilizing its property, plant, and equipment to generate revenues?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The fixed asset turnover ratio is useful in answering this question:\n\nThe fixed asset turnover ratio measures the sales dollars generated by each dollar of fixed assets used.\n\nA high ratio normally suggests effective management. An increasing ratio over time signals more-efficient use of fixed assets.\n\nFixed AssetTurnover Ratio\n\nNet Sales RevenueAverage Net Fixed Assets\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 7}], "format": "plain", "slides": [7], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t3", "title": "Measuring and Recording Acquisition Cost", "body": "The cost principle requires that all reasonable and necessary costs incurred in acquiring a long-lived asset, placing it in its operational setting, and preparing it for use should be recorded in a designated asset account.\n\nThese costs, including non-refundable taxes, legal fees, transportation costs, and installation costs, are added to the purchase price of the asset.\n\nThese expenditures are capitalized (recorded as part of the total cost of the asset), not recorded as expenses in the current period.\n\nAny interest charges associated with the purchase are recorded as expenses when incurred.\n\nWhen a company purchases land, all of the incidental costs of the purchase, such as title fees, sales commissions, legal fees, title insurance, delinquent taxes, and surveying fees—should be included in its cost.\n\nBecause land is not subject to depreciation, it must be recorded as a separate asset.\n\nThe amount recorded for the purchase is called the acquisition cost.  It is the net cash amount paid for the asset, or when noncash assets are used as payment, the fair value of the asset given or asset received, whichever can be more clearly determined (called the cash equivalent price).", "blocks": [{"body": "The cost principle requires that all reasonable and necessary costs incurred in acquiring a long-lived asset, placing it in its operational setting, and preparing it for use should be recorded in a designated asset account.\n\nThese costs, including non-refundable taxes, legal fees, transportation costs, and installation costs, are added to the purchase price of the asset.\n\nThese expenditures are capitalized (recorded as part of the total cost of the asset), not recorded as expenses in the current period.\n\nAny interest charges associated with the purchase are recorded as expenses when incurred.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 8}, {"body": "When a company purchases land, all of the incidental costs of the purchase, such as title fees, sales commissions, legal fees, title insurance, delinquent taxes, and surveying fees—should be included in its cost.\n\nBecause land is not subject to depreciation, it must be recorded as a separate asset.\n\nThe amount recorded for the purchase is called the acquisition cost.  It is the net cash amount paid for the asset, or when noncash assets are used as payment, the fair value of the asset given or asset received, whichever can be more clearly determined (called the cash equivalent price).", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 9}], "format": "plain", "slides": [8, 9], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t4", "title": "Acquisition by Construction", "body": "A company may construct an asset for use instead of buying it from a manufacturer.\n\nThe acquisition cost of this self-constructed asset will include all direct and indirect costs of construction, including interest on any loans obtained to construct the asset.\n\nAs soon as the asset is available for use, the accumulated construction costs will be depreciated over its productive life.\n\nThe amount of interest that is included in the cost of the construction is called capitalized interest, which reduces the company’s total interest expense every year until the facility is ready for use.\n\nOnce the construction of the facility is completed, any interest costs on construction loans, however, must be expensed.\n\nThe complex computation of interest capitalization is discussed in other accounting courses.\n\nCapitalizing labour, materials, and a portion of interest expense increases assets, decreases expenses, and increases net earnings.", "blocks": [{"body": "A company may construct an asset for use instead of buying it from a manufacturer.\n\nThe acquisition cost of this self-constructed asset will include all direct and indirect costs of construction, including interest on any loans obtained to construct the asset.\n\nAs soon as the asset is available for use, the accumulated construction costs will be depreciated over its productive life.\n\nThe amount of interest that is included in the cost of the construction is called capitalized interest, which reduces the company’s total interest expense every year until the facility is ready for use.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 10}, {"body": "Once the construction of the facility is completed, any interest costs on construction loans, however, must be expensed.\n\nThe complex computation of interest capitalization is discussed in other accounting courses.\n\nCapitalizing labour, materials, and a portion of interest expense increases assets, decreases expenses, and increases net earnings.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 11}], "format": "plain", "slides": [10, 11], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t5", "title": "Acquisitions as a Basket Purchase of Assets", "body": "When several long-lived assets, such as land, building, and equipment, are acquired in a single transaction and for a single lump sum, known as a basket purchase, the cost of each asset must be measured and recorded separately.\n\nThis is because land is not depreciated but buildings and equipment are, although at different rates.\n\nThe purchase price must be apportioned among the land, the building, and the equipment, on a rational basis.", "blocks": [{"body": "When several long-lived assets, such as land, building, and equipment, are acquired in a single transaction and for a single lump sum, known as a basket purchase, the cost of each asset must be measured and recorded separately.\n\nThis is because land is not depreciated but buildings and equipment are, although at different rates.\n\nThe purchase price must be apportioned among the land, the building, and the equipment, on a rational basis.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 12}], "format": "plain", "slides": [12], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t6", "title": "Repairs, Maintenance, and Improvements", "body": "Most assets require substantial expenditures during their useful lives to maintain or enhance their productive capacity.\n\nThese expenditures include cash outlays for ordinary repairs and maintenance, major repairs, replacements, and additions.\n\nThe terms expenditure and expense are not synonymous.\n\nAn expenditure is the payment of money to acquire goods or services. These goods and services may be recorded as either assets or expenses, depending on whether they benefit future periods or only the current period.\n\nExpenditures that are made after an asset is acquired are classified as follows:\n\nOrdinary repairs and maintenance or\n\nImprovements", "blocks": [{"body": "Most assets require substantial expenditures during their useful lives to maintain or enhance their productive capacity.\n\nThese expenditures include cash outlays for ordinary repairs and maintenance, major repairs, replacements, and additions.\n\nThe terms expenditure and expense are not synonymous.\n\nAn expenditure is the payment of money to acquire goods or services. These goods and services may be recorded as either assets or expenses, depending on whether they benefit future periods or only the current period.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 13}, {"body": "Expenditures that are made after an asset is acquired are classified as follows:\n\nOrdinary repairs and maintenance or\n\nImprovements", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 14}], "format": "plain", "slides": [13, 14], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t7", "title": "Ordinary Repairs and Maintenance", "body": "Ordinary repairs and maintenance are expenditures for the normal maintenance and upkeep of long-lived assets.\n\nThese expenditures maintain the productive capacity of the asset during the current accounting period only.\n\nThese cash outlays are recorded as expenses in the current period.\n\nThese expenditures are recurring in nature, involve relatively small amounts at each occurrence, and do not directly lengthen the useful life of the asset.", "blocks": [{"body": "Ordinary repairs and maintenance are expenditures for the normal maintenance and upkeep of long-lived assets.\n\nThese expenditures maintain the productive capacity of the asset during the current accounting period only.\n\nThese cash outlays are recorded as expenses in the current period.\n\nThese expenditures are recurring in nature, involve relatively small amounts at each occurrence, and do not directly lengthen the useful life of the asset.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 15}], "format": "plain", "slides": [15], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t8", "title": "Improvements", "body": "Improvements are expenditures that increase the productive life, operating efficiency, or capacity of the asset.\n\nThese capital expenditures, which provide benefits to the company over a number of accounting periods, are added to the appropriate asset accounts (i.e., they are capitalized).\n\nThey occur infrequently, involve large amounts of money, and increase an asset’s economic usefulness in the future through either increased efficiency or longer life.\n\nExamples include additions, complete reconditioning, and major overhauls and replacements, such as the complete replacement of an engine on an aircraft.\n\nIn many cases, no clear line distinguishes improvements (assets) from ordinary repairs and maintenance (expenses).\n\nAccountants must exercise professional judgment and make subjective decisions.\n\nBecause the decision to capitalize or expense is subjective, auditors closely review the items reported as capital expenditures and ordinary repairs and maintenance.\n\nTo avoid spending too much time on classifying capital expenditures and repair expenses, some companies develop simple policies that govern the accounting for these expenditures.", "blocks": [{"body": "Improvements are expenditures that increase the productive life, operating efficiency, or capacity of the asset.\n\nThese capital expenditures, which provide benefits to the company over a number of accounting periods, are added to the appropriate asset accounts (i.e., they are capitalized).\n\nThey occur infrequently, involve large amounts of money, and increase an asset’s economic usefulness in the future through either increased efficiency or longer life.\n\nExamples include additions, complete reconditioning, and major overhauls and replacements, such as the complete replacement of an engine on an aircraft.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 16}, {"body": "In many cases, no clear line distinguishes improvements (assets) from ordinary repairs and maintenance (expenses).\n\nAccountants must exercise professional judgment and make subjective decisions.\n\nBecause the decision to capitalize or expense is subjective, auditors closely review the items reported as capital expenditures and ordinary repairs and maintenance.\n\nTo avoid spending too much time on classifying capital expenditures and repair expenses, some companies develop simple policies that govern the accounting for these expenditures.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 17}], "format": "plain", "slides": [16, 17], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t9", "title": "Depreciation Concepts", "body": "All long-lived assets have limited useful lives, except land.\n\nLong-lived assets represent the prepaid cost of a bundle of future services or benefits.\n\nA portion of an asset’s acquisition cost must be allocated as an expense to the periods in which revenue is earned as a result of its use.\n\nThe term used to identify and allocate the acquisition cost of using buildings and equipment that generate revenue over time is depreciation.\n\nDepreciation is the process of allocating the acquisition cost of buildings and equipment over their productive lives by using a systematic and rational method.", "blocks": [{"body": "All long-lived assets have limited useful lives, except land.\n\nLong-lived assets represent the prepaid cost of a bundle of future services or benefits.\n\nA portion of an asset’s acquisition cost must be allocated as an expense to the periods in which revenue is earned as a result of its use.\n\nThe term used to identify and allocate the acquisition cost of using buildings and equipment that generate revenue over time is depreciation.\n\nDepreciation is the process of allocating the acquisition cost of buildings and equipment over their productive lives by using a systematic and rational method.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 18}], "format": "plain", "slides": [18], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t10", "title": "Depreciation Concepts - Example", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch7-image2.png", "alt": "Depreciation Concepts - Example — original slide 19"}], "slide": 19}], "format": "plain", "slides": [19], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t11", "title": "Depreciation Concepts", "body": "The amount of depreciation recorded during each period is reported on the statement of earnings as Depreciation Expense.\n\nThe amount of depreciation expense accumulated since the acquisition date is a contra-account, Accumulated Depreciation, and deducted from the related asset’s cost.\n\nThe acquisition cost minus accumulated depreciation and any write-downs in asset value is called the carrying amount (carrying value or net book value) and is reported on the statement of financial position.\n\nCompanies disclose information about the long-lived assets they own or control, and the related accumulated depreciation, in a note to the financial statements.", "blocks": [{"body": "The amount of depreciation recorded during each period is reported on the statement of earnings as Depreciation Expense.\n\nThe amount of depreciation expense accumulated since the acquisition date is a contra-account, Accumulated Depreciation, and deducted from the related asset’s cost.\n\nThe acquisition cost minus accumulated depreciation and any write-downs in asset value is called the carrying amount (carrying value or net book value) and is reported on the statement of financial position.\n\nCompanies disclose information about the long-lived assets they own or control, and the related accumulated depreciation, in a note to the financial statements.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 20}], "format": "plain", "slides": [20], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t12", "title": "Calculation of Depreciation Expense", "body": "The calculation of depreciation expense requires three amounts for each depreciable asset:\n\nAcquisition cost\n\nEstimated useful life to the company\n\nEstimated residual (or salvage) value at the end of the asset’s useful life to the company\n\nTwo of these three amounts are estimates.\n\nTherefore, depreciation expense is an estimate.\n\nEstimated useful life represents management’s estimate of the asset’s useful economic life to the company, rather than of its total economic life to all potential users.\n\nThe asset’s expected physical life is often longer than the company intends to use the asset.\n\nEstimated residual (or salvage) value represents management’s estimate of the amount the company expects to recover upon disposal of the asset at the end of its estimated useful life, less any estimated costs of dismantling, disposal, and sale.\n\nThe residual value may be the estimated value of the asset as salvage or scrap, or its expected value if sold to another user.", "blocks": [{"body": "The calculation of depreciation expense requires three amounts for each depreciable asset:\n\nAcquisition cost\n\nEstimated useful life to the company\n\nEstimated residual (or salvage) value at the end of the asset’s useful life to the company\n\nTwo of these three amounts are estimates.\n\nTherefore, depreciation expense is an estimate.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 21}, {"body": "Estimated useful life represents management’s estimate of the asset’s useful economic life to the company, rather than of its total economic life to all potential users.\n\nThe asset’s expected physical life is often longer than the company intends to use the asset.\n\nEstimated residual (or salvage) value represents management’s estimate of the amount the company expects to recover upon disposal of the asset at the end of its estimated useful life, less any estimated costs of dismantling, disposal, and sale.\n\nThe residual value may be the estimated value of the asset as salvage or scrap, or its expected value if sold to another user.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 22}], "format": "plain", "slides": [21, 22], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t13", "title": "Alternative Depreciation Methods", "body": "The three most common depreciation methods are:\n\nStraight line\n\nUnits of production\n\nDeclining (or diminishing) balance\n\nThe depreciation method used should reflect the anticipated reduction in future cash flow as a result of the wear and tear from using the asset over time.\n\nOnce a method is selected, the depreciation method should be applied consistently over time to enhance comparability of financial information to users.", "blocks": [{"body": "The three most common depreciation methods are:\n\nStraight line\n\nUnits of production\n\nDeclining (or diminishing) balance\n\nThe depreciation method used should reflect the anticipated reduction in future cash flow as a result of the wear and tear from using the asset over time.\n\nOnce a method is selected, the depreciation method should be applied consistently over time to enhance comparability of financial information to users.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 23}], "format": "plain", "slides": [23], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t14", "title": "Straight-Line Method", "body": "More companies, use straight-line depreciation in their financial statements.\n\nUnder straight-line depreciation, an equal portion of an asset’s depreciable cost is allocated to each accounting period over its estimated useful life.\n\nThe formula to estimate annual depreciation expense follows:\n\nCost  –  Residual ValueUseful Life in Years\n\nDepreciation\n\nExpense per Year\n\n=\n\nUnder the Straight-Line Method:\n\nDepreciation expense is a constant amount for each year.\n\nAccumulated depreciation increases by an equal amount each year.\n\nCarrying amount decreases by the same amount each year until it equals the estimated residual value.", "blocks": [{"body": "More companies, use straight-line depreciation in their financial statements.\n\nUnder straight-line depreciation, an equal portion of an asset’s depreciable cost is allocated to each accounting period over its estimated useful life.\n\nThe formula to estimate annual depreciation expense follows:\n\nCost  –  Residual ValueUseful Life in Years\n\nDepreciation\n\nExpense per Year\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 24}, {"body": "Under the Straight-Line Method:\n\nDepreciation expense is a constant amount for each year.\n\nAccumulated depreciation increases by an equal amount each year.\n\nCarrying amount decreases by the same amount each year until it equals the estimated residual value.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 25}], "format": "plain", "slides": [24, 25], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t15", "title": "Straight-Line Method - Example", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch7-image3.png", "alt": "Straight-Line Method - Example — original slide 26"}, {"src": "sources/images/ch7-image4.png", "alt": "Straight-Line Method - Example — original slide 26"}], "slide": 26}], "format": "plain", "slides": [26], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t16", "title": "Units-of-Production (Activity) Method", "body": "Units-of-production depreciation method allocates the cost of an asset over its useful life based on the relation of its periodic output to its total estimated output.\n\nThe formula to estimate annual depreciation expense under this method follows:\n\nDividing the depreciable amount by the estimated total production yields the depreciation rate per unit of production (or activity), which is then multiplied by the actual annual production (or activity) to determine depreciation expense.\n\nWhen the units-of-production method is used, depreciation expense is said to be a variable expense because it varies directly with production, or use.\n\nThe carrying amount and the amount of accumulated depreciation will also fluctuate from year to year.\n\nIf any difference occurs at the end of the asset’s life, the final adjusting entry to depreciation expense should be for the amount needed to cause the asset’s carrying amount to equal the asset’s estimated residual value.\n\nThe units-of-production method is based on an estimate of an asset’s total productive capacity or output that is difficult to determine.", "blocks": [{"body": "Units-of-production depreciation method allocates the cost of an asset over its useful life based on the relation of its periodic output to its total estimated output.\n\nThe formula to estimate annual depreciation expense under this method follows:\n\nDividing the depreciable amount by the estimated total production yields the depreciation rate per unit of production (or activity), which is then multiplied by the actual annual production (or activity) to determine depreciation expense.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch7-image5.jpeg", "alt": "Units-of-Production (Activity) Method — original slide 27"}], "slide": 27}, {"body": "When the units-of-production method is used, depreciation expense is said to be a variable expense because it varies directly with production, or use.\n\nThe carrying amount and the amount of accumulated depreciation will also fluctuate from year to year.\n\nIf any difference occurs at the end of the asset’s life, the final adjusting entry to depreciation expense should be for the amount needed to cause the asset’s carrying amount to equal the asset’s estimated residual value.\n\nThe units-of-production method is based on an estimate of an asset’s total productive capacity or output that is difficult to determine.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 28}], "format": "plain", "slides": [27, 28], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t17", "title": "Units-of-Production (Activity) Method - Example", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch7-image6.png", "alt": "Units-of-Production (Activity) Method - Example — original slide 29"}, {"src": "sources/images/ch7-image7.png", "alt": "Units-of-Production (Activity) Method - Example — original slide 29"}], "slide": 29}], "format": "plain", "slides": [29], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t18", "title": "Accelerated Depreciation", "body": "If an asset is considered to be more efficient or productive in its earliest years, with a levelling off in later years, managers might choose the declining-balance depreciation method to reflect a sharper reduction in the economic benefits (cash flow) expected to be derived from the asset in the earlier rather than later years.\n\nThis is called accelerated depreciation.\n\nA declining-balance method is used to better reflect the decline of economic benefits over time.", "blocks": [{"body": "If an asset is considered to be more efficient or productive in its earliest years, with a levelling off in later years, managers might choose the declining-balance depreciation method to reflect a sharper reduction in the economic benefits (cash flow) expected to be derived from the asset in the earlier rather than later years.\n\nThis is called accelerated depreciation.\n\nA declining-balance method is used to better reflect the decline of economic benefits over time.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 30}], "format": "plain", "slides": [30], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t19", "title": "Declining-Balance Method", "body": "Declining-balance depreciation is based on multiplying the asset’s carrying amount by a fixed rate that exceeds the straight-line (SL) rate.\n\nThe rate is often double (two times) the SL rate and is called the double declining-balance rate.\n\nFor example, if the estimated useful life of an asset is 10 years and the SL rate is 10 percent (1 ÷ 10), then the declining-balance rate is 20 percent (2 × the SL rate of 10 percent).\n\nThe double declining-balance (DDB) rate is adopted most frequently by companies utilizing the accelerated method.", "blocks": [{"body": "Declining-balance depreciation is based on multiplying the asset’s carrying amount by a fixed rate that exceeds the straight-line (SL) rate.\n\nThe rate is often double (two times) the SL rate and is called the double declining-balance rate.\n\nFor example, if the estimated useful life of an asset is 10 years and the SL rate is 10 percent (1 ÷ 10), then the declining-balance rate is 20 percent (2 × the SL rate of 10 percent).\n\nThe double declining-balance (DDB) rate is adopted most frequently by companies utilizing the accelerated method.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 31}], "format": "plain", "slides": [31], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t20", "title": "Double Declining-Balance Formula", "body": "To calculate depreciation expense under the double declining-balance method the following formula is used:", "blocks": [{"body": "To calculate depreciation expense under the double declining-balance method the following formula is used:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch7-image8.jpeg", "alt": "Double Declining-Balance Formula — original slide 32"}], "slide": 32}], "format": "plain", "slides": [32], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t21", "title": "Double Declining-Balance", "body": "There are two important differences between the double declining-balance method and the others described previously:\n\nAccumulated depreciation, not residual value, is included in the formula.  Since accumulated depreciation increases each year, the carrying amount (cost − accumulated depreciation) decreases. The DDB rate is applied to a lower carrying amount each year, resulting in a decline in depreciation expense over time.\n\nThere are two important differences between the double declining-balance method and the others described previously:\n\nThe carrying amount should not be depreciated below the residual value:\n\nBefore the end of the estimated useful life, if the annual computation reduces the carrying amount below residual value, only the amount of depreciation expense needed to make the carrying amount equal to residual value is recorded, and no additional depreciation expense is computed in subsequent years.\n\nIn the last year of the asset’s estimated useful life, whatever amount is needed to bring the carrying amount to residual value is recorded, regardless of the amount of the computation.\n\n", "blocks": [{"body": "There are two important differences between the double declining-balance method and the others described previously:\n\nAccumulated depreciation, not residual value, is included in the formula.  Since accumulated depreciation increases each year, the carrying amount (cost − accumulated depreciation) decreases. The DDB rate is applied to a lower carrying amount each year, resulting in a decline in depreciation expense over time.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 33}, {"body": "There are two important differences between the double declining-balance method and the others described previously:\n\nThe carrying amount should not be depreciated below the residual value:\n\nBefore the end of the estimated useful life, if the annual computation reduces the carrying amount below residual value, only the amount of depreciation expense needed to make the carrying amount equal to residual value is recorded, and no additional depreciation expense is computed in subsequent years.\n\nIn the last year of the asset’s estimated useful life, whatever amount is needed to bring the carrying amount to residual value is recorded, regardless of the amount of the computation.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 34}, {"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch7-image9.png", "alt": "Double Declining-Balance — original slide 35"}, {"src": "sources/images/ch7-image10.png", "alt": "Double Declining-Balance — original slide 35"}], "slide": 35}], "format": "plain", "slides": [33, 34, 35], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t22", "title": "Depreciation Expense – Summary", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch7-image11.png", "alt": "Depreciation Expense – Summary — original slide 36"}], "slide": 36}], "format": "plain", "slides": [36], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t23", "title": "Changes in Depreciation Estimates", "body": "Depreciation is based on two estimates—useful life and residual value.\n\nThese estimates are made at the time a depreciable asset is acquired.\n\nOne or both of these initial estimates may have to be revised. As well, any improvements may be added to the original acquisition cost at some time during the asset’s use.\n\nWhen it is clear that either estimate should be revised to a material degree or the asset’s cost has been changed, the undepreciated asset balance (less any residual value at that date) should be apportioned over the remaining estimated life from the current year into the future.\n\nTo calculate the new depreciation expense resulting from a change in estimate for any of the depreciation methods described in this chapter you would substitute:\n\nThe carrying amount for the original acquisition cost.\n\nThe new residual value for the original amount.\n\nThe estimated remaining useful life for the original estimated useful life.\n\nCompanies may change depreciation methods; requiring more disclosure because comparability is compromised.\n\nChanges in accounting estimates and depreciation methods should be made only when the new estimate or accounting method “better measures” the periodic net earnings of the business.", "blocks": [{"body": "Depreciation is based on two estimates—useful life and residual value.\n\nThese estimates are made at the time a depreciable asset is acquired.\n\nOne or both of these initial estimates may have to be revised. As well, any improvements may be added to the original acquisition cost at some time during the asset’s use.\n\nWhen it is clear that either estimate should be revised to a material degree or the asset’s cost has been changed, the undepreciated asset balance (less any residual value at that date) should be apportioned over the remaining estimated life from the current year into the future.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 37}, {"body": "To calculate the new depreciation expense resulting from a change in estimate for any of the depreciation methods described in this chapter you would substitute:\n\nThe carrying amount for the original acquisition cost.\n\nThe new residual value for the original amount.\n\nThe estimated remaining useful life for the original estimated useful life.\n\nCompanies may change depreciation methods; requiring more disclosure because comparability is compromised.\n\nChanges in accounting estimates and depreciation methods should be made only when the new estimate or accounting method “better measures” the periodic net earnings of the business.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 38}], "format": "plain", "slides": [37, 38], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t24", "title": "Managers’ Selection among Accounting Alternatives", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["Financial Reporting (IFRS)", " ", "Tax Reporting"], ["The objective of financial reporting is to provide financial information about the reporting entity that is useful to existing and potential investors, lenders, and other creditors in making decisions about providing resources to the entity.", " ", "The objective of the Income Tax Act is to raise sufficient revenues to pay for the expenditures of the federal government, with many provisions designed to encourage certain  behaviours  that are thought to benefit society (e.g., contributions to charities are tax-deductible to encourage people to support worthy programs)."]]], "images": [], "slide": 39}], "format": "plain", "slides": [39], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t25", "title": "Depreciation and CCA", "body": "Corporations that are subject to taxation in Canada must apply capital cost allowance (CCA) to tangible assets, as determined by the schedules provided by the tax authorities.\n\nThe CCA schedules must be used to calculate the maximum annual expense used in computing taxable income according to the tax rules and regulations.\n\nThese schedules classify capital assets into different classes and stipulate the maximum CCA rate (a declining-balance rate) for each class.\n\nCCA does not attempt to match the cost of an asset with the revenue it produces over its useful life.\n\nInstead, CCA provides for accelerated depreciation of an asset over a period that is usually much shorter than the asset’s estimated useful life.\n\nBecause CCA is based on an accelerated method, the expense reported under CCA reduces a corporation’s taxable income more in the early years of a long-lived asset than in later years.", "blocks": [{"body": "Corporations that are subject to taxation in Canada must apply capital cost allowance (CCA) to tangible assets, as determined by the schedules provided by the tax authorities.\n\nThe CCA schedules must be used to calculate the maximum annual expense used in computing taxable income according to the tax rules and regulations.\n\nThese schedules classify capital assets into different classes and stipulate the maximum CCA rate (a declining-balance rate) for each class.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 40}, {"body": "CCA does not attempt to match the cost of an asset with the revenue it produces over its useful life.\n\nInstead, CCA provides for accelerated depreciation of an asset over a period that is usually much shorter than the asset’s estimated useful life.\n\nBecause CCA is based on an accelerated method, the expense reported under CCA reduces a corporation’s taxable income more in the early years of a long-lived asset than in later years.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 41}], "format": "plain", "slides": [40, 41], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t26", "title": "Measuring Asset Impairment", "body": "Canadian corporations are required to review the carrying amount of their tangible and intangible assets for possible impairment.\n\nAssets can have different values that are used for different purposes.\n\nHistorical acquisition costs are essential for the verifiability of past transactions. These costs are then adjusted for systematic depreciation over their useful lives.\n\nHowever, adjusted historical costs are not relevant for investment decisions that rely on expectations of future cash flows.\n\nConsequently, investors and creditors may be interested in the asset’s fair value, which reflects the amount at which an asset can be bought or sold between two knowledgeable, willing parties in an arm’s-length transaction.\n\nCompanies usually acquire long-lived assets for use in their production processes, and not for resale to others.\n\nTherefore, a long-lived asset has value in use, which is measured as the present value (or current cash equivalent) of future cash flows expected to be derived from use of the asset over time.\n\nSubsequent to the acquisition, the economic benefits or future cash flows associated with assets do change for a variety of reasons.\n\nAdverse changes to future benefits may be triggered, for example, by a pandemic, a war, contraction in an economy and decreased disposable income, the reduced market price for the asset, ongoing evidence of operating cash flow losses, and technological obsolescence.\n\nConsequently, assets should then be assessed for possible impairment.", "blocks": [{"body": "Canadian corporations are required to review the carrying amount of their tangible and intangible assets for possible impairment.\n\nAssets can have different values that are used for different purposes.\n\nHistorical acquisition costs are essential for the verifiability of past transactions. These costs are then adjusted for systematic depreciation over their useful lives.\n\nHowever, adjusted historical costs are not relevant for investment decisions that rely on expectations of future cash flows.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 42}, {"body": "Consequently, investors and creditors may be interested in the asset’s fair value, which reflects the amount at which an asset can be bought or sold between two knowledgeable, willing parties in an arm’s-length transaction.\n\nCompanies usually acquire long-lived assets for use in their production processes, and not for resale to others.\n\nTherefore, a long-lived asset has value in use, which is measured as the present value (or current cash equivalent) of future cash flows expected to be derived from use of the asset over time.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 43}, {"body": "Subsequent to the acquisition, the economic benefits or future cash flows associated with assets do change for a variety of reasons.\n\nAdverse changes to future benefits may be triggered, for example, by a pandemic, a war, contraction in an economy and decreased disposable income, the reduced market price for the asset, ongoing evidence of operating cash flow losses, and technological obsolescence.\n\nConsequently, assets should then be assessed for possible impairment.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 44}], "format": "plain", "slides": [42, 43, 44], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t27", "title": "Measuring Asset Impairment – Two Steps", "body": "Step 1: Test for impairment\n\nImpairment occurs when events or changed circumstances cause the carrying amount of these assets to exceed their recoverable amount, which is the higher of its value in use and its fair value less costs to sell.\n\nIf Carrying amount > Recoverable amount, then the asset is impaired\n\nStep 2: Computation of impairment loss\n\nFor any asset considered to be impaired, companies recognize a loss for the difference between the asset’s carrying amount and its recoverable amount.\n\nImpairment loss = Carrying amount − Recoverable amount", "blocks": [{"body": "Step 1: Test for impairment\n\nImpairment occurs when events or changed circumstances cause the carrying amount of these assets to exceed their recoverable amount, which is the higher of its value in use and its fair value less costs to sell.\n\nIf Carrying amount > Recoverable amount, then the asset is impaired\n\nStep 2: Computation of impairment loss\n\nFor any asset considered to be impaired, companies recognize a loss for the difference between the asset’s carrying amount and its recoverable amount.\n\nImpairment loss = Carrying amount − Recoverable amount", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 45}], "format": "plain", "slides": [45], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t28", "title": "Disposal of Property, Plant, and Equipment", "body": "A business may voluntarily decide not to hold a long-lived asset for its entire life.\n\nThese disposals include sale, trade-in, and retirement.\n\nA business may also dispose of an asset involuntarily as a result of a casualty, such as a storm, fire, or accident.\n\nDepreciation must be updated to the date of disposal. The disposal of a depreciable asset usually requires two journal entries:\n\n1. An adjusting entry to update the depreciation \t\texpense and accumulated depreciation accounts.\n\n2. An entry to record the disposal.", "blocks": [{"body": "A business may voluntarily decide not to hold a long-lived asset for its entire life.\n\nThese disposals include sale, trade-in, and retirement.\n\nA business may also dispose of an asset involuntarily as a result of a casualty, such as a storm, fire, or accident.\n\nDepreciation must be updated to the date of disposal. The disposal of a depreciable asset usually requires two journal entries:\n\n1. An adjusting entry to update the depreciation \t\texpense and accumulated depreciation accounts.\n\n2. An entry to record the disposal.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 46}], "format": "plain", "slides": [46], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t29", "title": "Entry to Record the Disposal", "body": "2.\tAn entry to record the disposal:\n\nThe cost of the asset and any accumulated depreciation at the date of disposal must be removed from the accounts.\n\nThe difference between any resources received on disposal of an asset and its carrying amount at the date of disposal is a gain or loss on disposal of the asset.\n\nThis gain (or loss) is reported in operations on the statement of earnings.", "blocks": [{"body": "2.\tAn entry to record the disposal:\n\nThe cost of the asset and any accumulated depreciation at the date of disposal must be removed from the accounts.\n\nThe difference between any resources received on disposal of an asset and its carrying amount at the date of disposal is a gain or loss on disposal of the asset.\n\nThis gain (or loss) is reported in operations on the statement of earnings.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 47}], "format": "plain", "slides": [47], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t30", "title": "Acquisition and Depletion of Natural Resources", "body": "Some large companies develop raw materials and products from natural resources—assets that occur in nature, such as oil, diamonds, gold, and iron ore.\n\nThese resources are often called wasting assets because they are depleted (i.e., physically used up).\n\nIn Canada, natural resource companies do not own the land from which they extract oil or minerals. They acquire mineral rights from the government to explore, develop, and extract minerals from the land, but they do not own the land itself.\n\nWhen the rights to explore and develop natural resources are acquired, they are recorded in conformity with the cost principle.\n\nThrough exploration, the company estimates the value of the resources extracted and sold throughout the productive life of the natural resource reserves.\n\nThe quantity of resources in the reserve is surveyed by using sophisticated geological engineering methods to establish its potential value over its productive life.\n\nAs a natural resource is used up, its acquisition cost must be apportioned among the periods in which revenues are earned in conformity with the expense recognition principle.\n\nThe term depletion describes the process of allocating a natural resource’s cost over the period of its exploitation.\n\nThe units-of-production method often is applied to compute depletion.\n\nOften these companies keep the reserve value separate from other property, plant, and equipment values incurred, because the companies may have to construct special roads and erect housing, buildings, and facilities in remote locations to extract the resources.\n\nWhen the site is put into production, these accumulated costs will be depreciated over the productive life of the site.\n\nTherefore, natural resource companies amortize the acquisition costs of intangible mineral rights, deplete their reserve values, and depreciate exploration and development costs as well as self-constructed extraction facilities.\n\nA depletion rate is calculated by dividing the total acquisition and development cost (less any estimated residual value, which is rare) by the estimated units that can be withdrawn, economically, from the resource.\n\nThe depletion rate is multiplied each period by the actual number of units withdrawn during the accounting period.\n\nThis procedure is the same as the units-of-production method of calculating depreciation.\n\nWhen buildings and similar improvements are acquired for the development and exploitation of a natural resource, they should be recorded in separate asset accounts and depreciated—not depleted.\n\nTheir estimated useful lives cannot be longer than the time needed to exploit the natural resource, unless they have a significant use after the source is depleted.", "blocks": [{"body": "Some large companies develop raw materials and products from natural resources—assets that occur in nature, such as oil, diamonds, gold, and iron ore.\n\nThese resources are often called wasting assets because they are depleted (i.e., physically used up).\n\nIn Canada, natural resource companies do not own the land from which they extract oil or minerals. They acquire mineral rights from the government to explore, develop, and extract minerals from the land, but they do not own the land itself.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 48}, {"body": "When the rights to explore and develop natural resources are acquired, they are recorded in conformity with the cost principle.\n\nThrough exploration, the company estimates the value of the resources extracted and sold throughout the productive life of the natural resource reserves.\n\nThe quantity of resources in the reserve is surveyed by using sophisticated geological engineering methods to establish its potential value over its productive life.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 49}, {"body": "As a natural resource is used up, its acquisition cost must be apportioned among the periods in which revenues are earned in conformity with the expense recognition principle.\n\nThe term depletion describes the process of allocating a natural resource’s cost over the period of its exploitation.\n\nThe units-of-production method often is applied to compute depletion.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 50}, {"body": "Often these companies keep the reserve value separate from other property, plant, and equipment values incurred, because the companies may have to construct special roads and erect housing, buildings, and facilities in remote locations to extract the resources.\n\nWhen the site is put into production, these accumulated costs will be depreciated over the productive life of the site.\n\nTherefore, natural resource companies amortize the acquisition costs of intangible mineral rights, deplete their reserve values, and depreciate exploration and development costs as well as self-constructed extraction facilities.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 51}, {"body": "A depletion rate is calculated by dividing the total acquisition and development cost (less any estimated residual value, which is rare) by the estimated units that can be withdrawn, economically, from the resource.\n\nThe depletion rate is multiplied each period by the actual number of units withdrawn during the accounting period.\n\nThis procedure is the same as the units-of-production method of calculating depreciation.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 52}, {"body": "When buildings and similar improvements are acquired for the development and exploitation of a natural resource, they should be recorded in separate asset accounts and depreciated—not depleted.\n\nTheir estimated useful lives cannot be longer than the time needed to exploit the natural resource, unless they have a significant use after the source is depleted.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 53}], "format": "plain", "slides": [48, 49, 50, 51, 52, 53], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t31", "title": "Acquisition and Amortization of Intangible Assets", "body": "An intangible asset has value because of certain rights and privileges conferred by law on its owner.\n\nAn intangible asset has no material or physical substance.\n\nExamples include mineral rights to explore and develop land, landing rights for time slots at airports, copyrights, customer lists, patents, trademarks, and licences.\n\nLegal documents usually confirm the nature and existence of intangible assets.\n\nIntangible assets are recorded at historical cost only if they are purchased.\n\nIf an intangible asset is developed internally, the cost of development is normally recorded as an expense.\n\nUpon acquisition of intangible assets, managers determine whether the separate intangibles have definite or indefinite lives.", "blocks": [{"body": "An intangible asset has value because of certain rights and privileges conferred by law on its owner.\n\nAn intangible asset has no material or physical substance.\n\nExamples include mineral rights to explore and develop land, landing rights for time slots at airports, copyrights, customer lists, patents, trademarks, and licences.\n\nLegal documents usually confirm the nature and existence of intangible assets.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 54}, {"body": "Intangible assets are recorded at historical cost only if they are purchased.\n\nIf an intangible asset is developed internally, the cost of development is normally recorded as an expense.\n\nUpon acquisition of intangible assets, managers determine whether the separate intangibles have definite or indefinite lives.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 55}], "format": "plain", "slides": [54, 55], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t32", "title": "Acquisition and Amortization of Intangible Assets – Definite Life", "body": "The cost of an intangible with a definite life is allocated on a straight-line basis each period over its useful life in a process called amortization that is similar to depreciation and depletion.\n\nHowever, most companies do not estimate a residual value for their intangible assets.", "blocks": [{"body": "The cost of an intangible with a definite life is allocated on a straight-line basis each period over its useful life in a process called amortization that is similar to depreciation and depletion.\n\nHowever, most companies do not estimate a residual value for their intangible assets.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 56}], "format": "plain", "slides": [56], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t33", "title": "Acquisition and Amortization of Intangible Assets – Indefinite Life", "body": "Intangible assets with indefinite lives are not amortized.\n\nThese assets must be reviewed at least annually for possible impairment.\n\nIf the fair value of the asset less costs to sell is more than its carrying amount, the asset is not impaired.\n\nIf not, the asset’s value in use should be determined.\n\nAn impairment loss must be recognized if the value in use is less than the carrying amount.\n\nIf both the asset’s value in use and its fair value less costs to sell are lower than the carrying amount, then the asset’s recoverable amount (the higher of value in use and fair value less costs to sell) is compared to the carrying amount to determine the impairment loss.\n\nThis process is similar to that used for other long-lived assets, including intangibles with definite lives.", "blocks": [{"body": "Intangible assets with indefinite lives are not amortized.\n\nThese assets must be reviewed at least annually for possible impairment.\n\nIf the fair value of the asset less costs to sell is more than its carrying amount, the asset is not impaired.\n\nIf not, the asset’s value in use should be determined.\n\nAn impairment loss must be recognized if the value in use is less than the carrying amount.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 57}, {"body": "If both the asset’s value in use and its fair value less costs to sell are lower than the carrying amount, then the asset’s recoverable amount (the higher of value in use and fair value less costs to sell) is compared to the carrying amount to determine the impairment loss.\n\nThis process is similar to that used for other long-lived assets, including intangibles with definite lives.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 58}], "format": "plain", "slides": [57, 58], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t34", "title": "Acquisition and Amortization of Intangible Assets – Patents", "body": "A patent is an exclusive right granted by the Canadian Intellectual Property Office of Industry Canada for a period of 20 years.\n\nA patent that is purchased is recorded at cost.\n\nAn internally developed patent is recorded at only its registration and legal costs.\n\nShould acquirers of patent rights incur costs to significantly change the product or process which has been patented, these costs may be capitalized under conditions specified in IAS 38—Intangible Assets. Given its limited life, a patent is typically amortized over the shorter of its economic life and its remaining legal life.", "blocks": [{"body": "A patent is an exclusive right granted by the Canadian Intellectual Property Office of Industry Canada for a period of 20 years.\n\nA patent that is purchased is recorded at cost.\n\nAn internally developed patent is recorded at only its registration and legal costs.\n\nShould acquirers of patent rights incur costs to significantly change the product or process which has been patented, these costs may be capitalized under conditions specified in IAS 38—Intangible Assets. Given its limited life, a patent is typically amortized over the shorter of its economic life and its remaining legal life.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 59}], "format": "plain", "slides": [59], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t35", "title": "Acquisition and Amortization of Intangible Assets – Copyrights", "body": "A copyright that is purchased is recorded at cost. It gives the owner the exclusive right to publish, use, and sell a literary, musical, or artistic piece of work for a period not exceeding 50 years after the author’s death.\n\nThe same principles, guidelines, and procedures used in accounting for the cost of patents are also used for copyrights.", "blocks": [{"body": "A copyright that is purchased is recorded at cost. It gives the owner the exclusive right to publish, use, and sell a literary, musical, or artistic piece of work for a period not exceeding 50 years after the author’s death.\n\nThe same principles, guidelines, and procedures used in accounting for the cost of patents are also used for copyrights.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 60}], "format": "plain", "slides": [60], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t36", "title": "Acquisition and Amortization of Intangible Assets – Technology", "body": "Website development costs related to acquiring a domain name and developing graphics are capitalized as an intangible asset.\n\nComputer software costs incurred during a preliminary concept phase of a project should be expensed.\n\nOnce technological feasibility is reached for the project (the point at which there is a detailed program design or completion of a working model), the direct costs of the actual development, such as coding and testing the software, should be capitalized as an intangible asset.", "blocks": [{"body": "Website development costs related to acquiring a domain name and developing graphics are capitalized as an intangible asset.\n\nComputer software costs incurred during a preliminary concept phase of a project should be expensed.\n\nOnce technological feasibility is reached for the project (the point at which there is a detailed program design or completion of a working model), the direct costs of the actual development, such as coding and testing the software, should be capitalized as an intangible asset.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 61}], "format": "plain", "slides": [61], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t37", "title": "Acquisition and Amortization of Intangible Assets – Research and Development Costs", "body": "If an intangible asset is developed internally, the cost of research is recorded as an expense.\n\nWhen a company can demonstrate that technical and commercial feasibility of the resulting product or service has been established, development costs can be deferred and amortized over the lifetime of the commercialized product.", "blocks": [{"body": "If an intangible asset is developed internally, the cost of research is recorded as an expense.\n\nWhen a company can demonstrate that technical and commercial feasibility of the resulting product or service has been established, development costs can be deferred and amortized over the lifetime of the commercialized product.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 62}], "format": "plain", "slides": [62], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t38", "title": "Acquisition and Amortization of Intangible Assets – Trademarks", "body": "A trademark is an exclusive legal right to use a name, image, or slogan identified with a product or a company.\n\nTrademarks are protected by law when they are registered at the Canadian Intellectual Property Office of Industry Canada.\n\nThe protection of a trademark provides the registered holder with exclusive rights to the trademark and can be renewed every 15 years throughout its life.\n\nMost companies do not amortize their trademarks as their useful lives can be extended indefinitely.\n\nTrademarks are often some of the most valuable assets that a company can own, but they are rarely seen on statements of financial position.\n\nThe reason is simple—intangible assets are not recorded unless they are purchased.\n\nCompanies often spend millions of dollars developing trademarks, but these expenditures are recorded as expenses and not capitalized.", "blocks": [{"body": "A trademark is an exclusive legal right to use a name, image, or slogan identified with a product or a company.\n\nTrademarks are protected by law when they are registered at the Canadian Intellectual Property Office of Industry Canada.\n\nThe protection of a trademark provides the registered holder with exclusive rights to the trademark and can be renewed every 15 years throughout its life.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 63}, {"body": "Most companies do not amortize their trademarks as their useful lives can be extended indefinitely.\n\nTrademarks are often some of the most valuable assets that a company can own, but they are rarely seen on statements of financial position.\n\nThe reason is simple—intangible assets are not recorded unless they are purchased.\n\nCompanies often spend millions of dollars developing trademarks, but these expenditures are recorded as expenses and not capitalized.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 64}], "format": "plain", "slides": [63, 64], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t39", "title": "Acquisition and Amortization of Intangible Assets – Franchises", "body": "A franchise is a contractual right to sell certain products or services, use certain trademarks, or perform activities in a geographical region.\n\nFranchises may be granted by either the government or other businesses for a specified period and purpose.\n\nFranchise agreements are contracts that can have a variety of provisions.\n\nThey usually require an investment by the franchisee; therefore, they should be accounted for as intangible assets.\n\nThe life of the franchise agreement depends on the contract. It may be for a single year or an indefinite period.", "blocks": [{"body": "A franchise is a contractual right to sell certain products or services, use certain trademarks, or perform activities in a geographical region.\n\nFranchises may be granted by either the government or other businesses for a specified period and purpose.\n\nFranchise agreements are contracts that can have a variety of provisions.\n\nThey usually require an investment by the franchisee; therefore, they should be accounted for as intangible assets.\n\nThe life of the franchise agreement depends on the contract. It may be for a single year or an indefinite period.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 65}], "format": "plain", "slides": [65], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t40", "title": "Acquisition and Amortization of Intangible Assets – Licences and Operating Rights", "body": "Licences and operating rights are typically obtained through agreements with governmental units or agencies, and permit the holders to use public property in performing their services.", "blocks": [{"body": "Licences and operating rights are typically obtained through agreements with governmental units or agencies, and permit the holders to use public property in performing their services.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 66}], "format": "plain", "slides": [66], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t41", "title": "Goodwill", "body": "The most frequently reported asset lacking physical substance is goodwill.\n\nThe term goodwill, means the favourable reputation that a company has with its customers.\n\nGoodwill arises from factors such as customer confidence, reputation for good service and quality products, and financial standing.\n\nFrom its first day of operations, a successful business continuously builds its own goodwill through a combination of factors that cannot be sold separately.\n\nGoodwill is said to be internally generated and is not reported as an asset.\n\nThe only way to report goodwill as an asset is to purchase another business.\n\nOften, the purchase price of a business exceeds the fair value of all of the identifiable assets owned by the business minus all of the identifiable liabilities owed to others; this is because of goodwill.\n\nUnlike intangible assets, goodwill is not identifiable and can only be identified with the business as a whole.\n\nGoodwill is therefore reported as a separate item in the statement of financial position.\n\nFor accounting purposes, goodwill is defined as the difference between the purchase price of a company as a whole and the fair value of its net assets.\n\nAccording to IFRS, goodwill has an indefinite life and any subsequent impairment in its value should be written down.\n\nThis leads to the recognition of a loss, which must be reported as a separate item on the statement of earnings in the year the impairment occurs.", "blocks": [{"body": "The most frequently reported asset lacking physical substance is goodwill.\n\nThe term goodwill, means the favourable reputation that a company has with its customers.\n\nGoodwill arises from factors such as customer confidence, reputation for good service and quality products, and financial standing.\n\nFrom its first day of operations, a successful business continuously builds its own goodwill through a combination of factors that cannot be sold separately.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 67}, {"body": "Goodwill is said to be internally generated and is not reported as an asset.\n\nThe only way to report goodwill as an asset is to purchase another business.\n\nOften, the purchase price of a business exceeds the fair value of all of the identifiable assets owned by the business minus all of the identifiable liabilities owed to others; this is because of goodwill.\n\nUnlike intangible assets, goodwill is not identifiable and can only be identified with the business as a whole.\n\nGoodwill is therefore reported as a separate item in the statement of financial position.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 68}, {"body": "For accounting purposes, goodwill is defined as the difference between the purchase price of a company as a whole and the fair value of its net assets.\n\nAccording to IFRS, goodwill has an indefinite life and any subsequent impairment in its value should be written down.\n\nThis leads to the recognition of a loss, which must be reported as a separate item on the statement of earnings in the year the impairment occurs.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 69}], "format": "plain", "slides": [67, 68, 69], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}, {"id": "c7-t42", "title": "Financial Analysis - A Misinterpretation", "body": "Cash from operations can only be provided by selling goods and services.\n\nDepreciation expense does not require a cash outflow. Although depreciation is added in the operating section of the statement of cash flows, depreciation is not a source of cash, when using the indirect method.\n\nBecause tax depreciation (CCA) is tax deductible, it reduces the cash outflow related to taxes (often called a tax shield). CCA can affect cash flows by affecting the amount of taxes that a company pays.", "blocks": [{"body": "Cash from operations can only be provided by selling goods and services.\n\nDepreciation expense does not require a cash outflow. Although depreciation is added in the operating section of the statement of cash flows, depreciation is not a source of cash, when using the indirect method.\n\nBecause tax depreciation (CCA) is tax deductible, it reduces the cash outflow related to taxes (often called a tax shield). CCA can affect cash flows by affecting the amount of taxes that a company pays.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 70}], "format": "plain", "slides": [70], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 7 slides"}], "deck": "sources/chapter-7.pptx"}, {"id": 8, "title": "Reporting and Interpreting Current Liabilities", "topics": [{"id": "c8-t1", "title": "Understanding the Business", "body": "The acquisition of assets is financed from two sources:\n\nDebt → Funds from creditors.\n\nEquity → Funds from shareholders.\n\nThe mix of debt and equity for a company is called the capital structure.\n\nDebt is considered riskier than equity.\n\nDebt payments are legal obligations.\n\nCreditors can force bankruptcy.\n\nCreditors can require sale of assets.", "blocks": [{"body": "The acquisition of assets is financed from two sources:\n\nDebt → Funds from creditors.\n\nEquity → Funds from shareholders.\n\nThe mix of debt and equity for a company is called the capital structure.\n\nDebt is considered riskier than equity.\n\nDebt payments are legal obligations.\n\nCreditors can force bankruptcy.\n\nCreditors can require sale of assets.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 4}], "format": "plain", "slides": [4], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t2", "title": "Liabilities Defined and Classified", "body": "Liabilities are defined as present debts or obligations of the entity that result from past transactions, which will be paid with assets or services.\n\nCurrent liabilities are short-term obligations that will be settled within the coming year by providing cash, goods, other current assets, or services.\n\nNon-current liabilities include all other liabilities.\n\nMost liabilities are recorded as they occur during the accounting period.\n\nHowever, specific liabilities that can be determined with accuracy, such as salaries payable and interest payable, require accrual through adjusting entries at the end of the accounting period, prior to the preparation of financial statements.\n\nFor other types of liabilities, the exact amounts will not be known with certainty until a future event, but they must be estimated and recorded if they relate to transactions that occurred during the accounting period.\n\nSuch liabilities are referred to as provisions on the statement of financial position.\n\nIn particular cases, it may not be possible to provide a reasonable estimate of a potential future liability that is contingent on a future event.\n\nRelevant information about contingent liabilities must, therefore, be disclosed in notes to the financial statements.", "blocks": [{"body": "Liabilities are defined as present debts or obligations of the entity that result from past transactions, which will be paid with assets or services.\n\nCurrent liabilities are short-term obligations that will be settled within the coming year by providing cash, goods, other current assets, or services.\n\nNon-current liabilities include all other liabilities.\n\nMost liabilities are recorded as they occur during the accounting period.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 5}, {"body": "However, specific liabilities that can be determined with accuracy, such as salaries payable and interest payable, require accrual through adjusting entries at the end of the accounting period, prior to the preparation of financial statements.\n\nFor other types of liabilities, the exact amounts will not be known with certainty until a future event, but they must be estimated and recorded if they relate to transactions that occurred during the accounting period.\n\nSuch liabilities are referred to as provisions on the statement of financial position.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 6}, {"body": "In particular cases, it may not be possible to provide a reasonable estimate of a potential future liability that is contingent on a future event.\n\nRelevant information about contingent liabilities must, therefore, be disclosed in notes to the financial statements.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 7}], "format": "plain", "slides": [5, 6, 7], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t3", "title": "Current Liabilities", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch8-image2.png", "alt": "Current Liabilities — original slide 8"}], "slide": 8}], "format": "plain", "slides": [8], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t4", "title": "Accounts Payable", "body": "Most companies purchase goods and services from other businesses in the course of running their day-to-day operations.\n\nTypically, these supplies and services are purchased on credit with cash payments made after the goods and services have been received.\n\nMost companies list such obligations on their statement of financial position as accounts payable, also called trade accounts payable.", "blocks": [{"body": "Most companies purchase goods and services from other businesses in the course of running their day-to-day operations.\n\nTypically, these supplies and services are purchased on credit with cash payments made after the goods and services have been received.\n\nMost companies list such obligations on their statement of financial position as accounts payable, also called trade accounts payable.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 9}], "format": "plain", "slides": [9], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t5", "title": "Key Ratio Analysis – A/P Turnover Ratio", "body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How efficient is management at meeting its obligations to suppliers?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The accounts payable turnover ratio is a measure of how quickly management is paying trade suppliers. Analysts use this ratio as a measure of liquidity. It is computed as follows:\n\nA high turnover ratio normally suggests that a company is paying its suppliers in a timely manner.\n\nA/P Turnover\n\nratio\n\nCost of SalesAverage Accounts Payable\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How efficient is management at meeting its obligations to suppliers?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The accounts payable turnover ratio is a measure of how quickly management is paying trade suppliers. Analysts use this ratio as a measure of liquidity. It is computed as follows:\n\nA high turnover ratio normally suggests that a company is paying its suppliers in a timely manner.\n\nA/P Turnover\n\nratio\n\nCost of SalesAverage Accounts Payable\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 10}], "format": "plain", "slides": [10], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t6", "title": "Accrued Liabilities", "body": "Accrued liabilities are expenses that have been incurred before the end of an accounting period but have not yet been paid.\n\nIncome taxes payable → corporations must pay taxes on income from active business operations, property income and capital gains arising from the sale of assets.\n\nSales Taxes → companies are often required to collect and pay other types of taxes and fees, depending on the specific industry and geographical location in which they operate. These taxes add to the cost of producing and selling goods and services and are eventually passed on to customers through higher sales prices.", "blocks": [{"body": "Accrued liabilities are expenses that have been incurred before the end of an accounting period but have not yet been paid.\n\nIncome taxes payable → corporations must pay taxes on income from active business operations, property income and capital gains arising from the sale of assets.\n\nSales Taxes → companies are often required to collect and pay other types of taxes and fees, depending on the specific industry and geographical location in which they operate. These taxes add to the cost of producing and selling goods and services and are eventually passed on to customers through higher sales prices.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 11}], "format": "plain", "slides": [11], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t7", "title": "Payroll Liabilities", "body": "At the end of each accounting period, employees will usually have earned salaries that have not been paid.\n\nUnpaid salaries may be reported as part of a general accrued liability account on the statement of financial position or as a separate item.\n\nIn addition to reporting salaries that have been earned but are unpaid, companies must also report the cost of unpaid benefits, which include retirement programs, vacation time, employment insurance, and health insurance that employees have earned during the reporting period but have not taken or been paid.\n\nEmployers must also remit income tax and other social benefit contributions on behalf of their employees to the appropriate government agencies.", "blocks": [{"body": "At the end of each accounting period, employees will usually have earned salaries that have not been paid.\n\nUnpaid salaries may be reported as part of a general accrued liability account on the statement of financial position or as a separate item.\n\nIn addition to reporting salaries that have been earned but are unpaid, companies must also report the cost of unpaid benefits, which include retirement programs, vacation time, employment insurance, and health insurance that employees have earned during the reporting period but have not taken or been paid.\n\nEmployers must also remit income tax and other social benefit contributions on behalf of their employees to the appropriate government agencies.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 12}], "format": "plain", "slides": [12], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t8", "title": "Employee Deductions", "body": "Federal and provincial laws require the employer to deduct an appropriate amount of income tax each period from the gross earnings of each employee.\n\nEmployee income tax is usually the largest amount withheld from wages and salaries by the employer.\n\nThe amount of income tax withheld from the employee’s salary is recorded by the employer as a current liability between the date of deduction and the date on which the amount held is remitted to the government.\n\nAdditional amounts are deducted from the gross earnings for Employment Insurance (EI); for contributions to the Canada Pension Plan (CPP), or Quebec Pension Plan (QPP) in the province of Quebec; for future retirement benefits; for health insurance; and for other contributions that you and your employer must remit to the appropriate agencies.", "blocks": [{"body": "Federal and provincial laws require the employer to deduct an appropriate amount of income tax each period from the gross earnings of each employee.\n\nEmployee income tax is usually the largest amount withheld from wages and salaries by the employer.\n\nThe amount of income tax withheld from the employee’s salary is recorded by the employer as a current liability between the date of deduction and the date on which the amount held is remitted to the government.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 13}, {"body": "Additional amounts are deducted from the gross earnings for Employment Insurance (EI); for contributions to the Canada Pension Plan (CPP), or Quebec Pension Plan (QPP) in the province of Quebec; for future retirement benefits; for health insurance; and for other contributions that you and your employer must remit to the appropriate agencies.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 14}], "format": "plain", "slides": [13, 14], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t9", "title": "Deferred Revenue", "body": "Typically, cash is paid when a product or service is delivered, or soon thereafter.  In some cases, however, cash is paid in advance of delivery.\n\nWhen a company collects cash before the related revenue has been earned, the cash is called deferred revenue.\n\nDeferred revenues are reported as a liability because cash has been collected but the related revenue has not been earned by the end of the accounting period.", "blocks": [{"body": "Typically, cash is paid when a product or service is delivered, or soon thereafter.  In some cases, however, cash is paid in advance of delivery.\n\nWhen a company collects cash before the related revenue has been earned, the cash is called deferred revenue.\n\nDeferred revenues are reported as a liability because cash has been collected but the related revenue has not been earned by the end of the accounting period.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 15}], "format": "plain", "slides": [15], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t10", "title": "Notes Payable", "body": "When a company borrows money, it normally signs a formal written contract with a bank and reports the amount borrowed as a note payable.\n\nThe contract specifies the amount borrowed, the date by which it must be repaid, and the interest rate associated with the debt.\n\nNotes payable can vary in amount borrowed, the interest charged, and length of time of the contract.\n\nThe time value of money is interest that is associated with the use of money over time.\n\nTo the borrower, interest reflects the cost of using someone else’s money and is therefore an expense.\n\nTo creditors, interest reflects the benefit of allowing someone else to use their money and is therefore revenue.\n\nYou need three pieces of information to calculate interest:\n\n(1) the principal (i.e., the cash that was borrowed),\n\n(2) the annual interest rate, and\n\n(3) the time period for the loan.\n\nInterest for the period =\n\nPrincipal x Annual interest rate x Number of months\n\n12 months", "blocks": [{"body": "When a company borrows money, it normally signs a formal written contract with a bank and reports the amount borrowed as a note payable.\n\nThe contract specifies the amount borrowed, the date by which it must be repaid, and the interest rate associated with the debt.\n\nNotes payable can vary in amount borrowed, the interest charged, and length of time of the contract.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 16}, {"body": "The time value of money is interest that is associated with the use of money over time.\n\nTo the borrower, interest reflects the cost of using someone else’s money and is therefore an expense.\n\nTo creditors, interest reflects the benefit of allowing someone else to use their money and is therefore revenue.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 17}, {"body": "You need three pieces of information to calculate interest:\n\n(1) the principal (i.e., the cash that was borrowed),\n\n(2) the annual interest rate, and\n\n(3) the time period for the loan.\n\nInterest for the period =\n\nPrincipal x Annual interest rate x Number of months\n\n12 months", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 18}], "format": "plain", "slides": [16, 17, 18], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t11", "title": "Current Portion of Long-Term Debt", "body": "Long-term debt is split between the current portion and the non-current portion.\n\nThe current portion of long-term debt is any portion of a note payable that is due within one year, or the operating cycle, whichever is longer.", "blocks": [{"body": "Long-term debt is split between the current portion and the non-current portion.\n\nThe current portion of long-term debt is any portion of a note payable that is due within one year, or the operating cycle, whichever is longer.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 19}], "format": "plain", "slides": [19], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t12", "title": "Provisions", "body": "When either the amount or the timing of the liability is uncertain, it is referred to as provision.\n\nA provision must be recognized when the following conditions are met:\n\nAn entity has a present obligation as a result of a past event,\n\nIt is probable that cash or other assets will be required to settle the obligation, and\n\nA reliable estimate can be made of the amount of the obligation.\n\nExamples of provisions include:\n\nEstimated liabilities for warranties,\n\nProduct returns and allowances,\n\nLegal and tax disputes that arise in the ordinary course of business,\n\nClosing of stores or specific operations, and\n\nRestructuring of the production, sales, or administrative structures.", "blocks": [{"body": "When either the amount or the timing of the liability is uncertain, it is referred to as provision.\n\nA provision must be recognized when the following conditions are met:\n\nAn entity has a present obligation as a result of a past event,\n\nIt is probable that cash or other assets will be required to settle the obligation, and\n\nA reliable estimate can be made of the amount of the obligation.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 20}, {"body": "Examples of provisions include:\n\nEstimated liabilities for warranties,\n\nProduct returns and allowances,\n\nLegal and tax disputes that arise in the ordinary course of business,\n\nClosing of stores or specific operations, and\n\nRestructuring of the production, sales, or administrative structures.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 21}], "format": "plain", "slides": [20, 21], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t13", "title": "Contingent Liabilities", "body": "A contingent liability is a possible liability that is created as a result of a past event, and which may or may not become a recorded liability, depending on future events.\n\n", "blocks": [{"body": "A contingent liability is a possible liability that is created as a result of a past event, and which may or may not become a recorded liability, depending on future events.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch8-image3.png", "alt": "Contingent Liabilities — original slide 22"}], "slide": 22}, {"body": "", "tables": [[["Level of Certainty of the Present or Possible Obligation", "Should a Liability Be Recognized?", "Disclosure Requirements"], ["There is a present obligation that probably requires an outflow of resources.", "", ""], ["The amount of the liability can be estimated reliably.", "A provision must be recognized.", "Disclosure of the provision is required."], ["The amount of the liability cannot be estimated reliably.", "No provision shall be recognized.", "Disclosure is required for the contingency."], ["There is either a present or a possible obligation that may, but probably will not, require an outflow of resources.", "No provision shall be recognized.", "Disclosure is required for the contingency."], ["There is either a present or a possible obligation where the likelihood of an outflow of resources is remote.", "No provision shall be recognized.", "Disclosure is not required."]]], "images": [], "slide": 23}], "format": "plain", "slides": [22, 23], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t14", "title": "International Perspective – It is a Matter of Degree of Certainty", "body": "The assessment of future probabilities is inherently subjective, but both IFRS and U.S. GAAP provide some guidance.\n\nUnder IFRS, “probable” is defined as more likely than not to occur, which implies more than a 50 percent chance of occurring.\n\nUnder U.S. GAAP, “probable” has been defined as likely to occur, which is commonly interpreted to mean having a greater than 70 percent chance of occurring.\n\nThis difference means that for some contingent liabilities, IFRS would require the reporting of a liability on the statement of financial position whereas U.S. GAAP would simply require note disclosure.\n\nThe more stringent requirement under IFRS reflects a more prudent way of providing relevant information to investors, creditors, and other users, primarily because it advances the timing of recording liabilities.", "blocks": [{"body": "The assessment of future probabilities is inherently subjective, but both IFRS and U.S. GAAP provide some guidance.\n\nUnder IFRS, “probable” is defined as more likely than not to occur, which implies more than a 50 percent chance of occurring.\n\nUnder U.S. GAAP, “probable” has been defined as likely to occur, which is commonly interpreted to mean having a greater than 70 percent chance of occurring.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 24}, {"body": "This difference means that for some contingent liabilities, IFRS would require the reporting of a liability on the statement of financial position whereas U.S. GAAP would simply require note disclosure.\n\nThe more stringent requirement under IFRS reflects a more prudent way of providing relevant information to investors, creditors, and other users, primarily because it advances the timing of recording liabilities.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 25}], "format": "plain", "slides": [24, 25], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t15", "title": "ESG Reporting", "body": "The Canada Energy Regulator (CER) states the aspiration of regulation is to assure the health of people by keeping the environment (soil, air, water) uncontaminated as resource extraction and harvesting proceeds.\n\nThe laws and regulations apply not to the depletion of resources but to the remediation of land altered by extraction of resources.\n\nWhile remediation is not restoration, related costs are significant and can give rise to significant contingent liabilities.\n\nIt is illegal in Canada for companies to abandon sites without decontaminating them.\n\nCanada has enacted 21 sets of laws to protect the environment against contamination.\n\nThe government expects companies to put measures in place to anticipate, prevent, and mitigate contamination, and if it occurs, to manage, contain, and remediate contamination.\n\nRemediation, management, and mitigation must be reported immediately to CER and other regulators as well as all other parties affected.\n\nNon-compliance with environmental laws and regulations can result in significant financial penalties and expenses that must be reported on financial statements according to accounting standards.", "blocks": [{"body": "The Canada Energy Regulator (CER) states the aspiration of regulation is to assure the health of people by keeping the environment (soil, air, water) uncontaminated as resource extraction and harvesting proceeds.\n\nThe laws and regulations apply not to the depletion of resources but to the remediation of land altered by extraction of resources.\n\nWhile remediation is not restoration, related costs are significant and can give rise to significant contingent liabilities.\n\nIt is illegal in Canada for companies to abandon sites without decontaminating them.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 26}, {"body": "Canada has enacted 21 sets of laws to protect the environment against contamination.\n\nThe government expects companies to put measures in place to anticipate, prevent, and mitigate contamination, and if it occurs, to manage, contain, and remediate contamination.\n\nRemediation, management, and mitigation must be reported immediately to CER and other regulators as well as all other parties affected.\n\nNon-compliance with environmental laws and regulations can result in significant financial penalties and expenses that must be reported on financial statements according to accounting standards.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 27}], "format": "plain", "slides": [26, 27], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t16", "title": "Working Capital Management", "body": "Working Capital = Current Assets – Current Liabilities\n\nChanges in working capital accounts are important to managers and analysts because they have a direct impact on cash flows from operating activities reported on the statement of cash flows.\n\nLiquidity is the ability to pay current obligations.", "blocks": [{"body": "Working Capital = Current Assets – Current Liabilities\n\nChanges in working capital accounts are important to managers and analysts because they have a direct impact on cash flows from operating activities reported on the statement of cash flows.\n\nLiquidity is the ability to pay current obligations.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 28}], "format": "plain", "slides": [28], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t17", "title": "Key Ratio Analysis – Quick Ratio", "body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → Does the company currently have the resources to pay its short-term debt?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → Analysts use the quick ratio as an indicator of the amount of quick assets (cash, short-term investments, and net receivables) available to satisfy current liabilities. It is computed as follows:\n\nQuick Ratio = Quick Assets / Current Liabilities\n\nA high ratio normally suggests good liquidity, but too high a ratio suggests inefficient use of resources. Many strong companies use sophisticated management techniques to minimize funds invested in current assets, and as a result, have quick ratios that are very low.", "blocks": [{"body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → Does the company currently have the resources to pay its short-term debt?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → Analysts use the quick ratio as an indicator of the amount of quick assets (cash, short-term investments, and net receivables) available to satisfy current liabilities. It is computed as follows:\n\nQuick Ratio = Quick Assets / Current Liabilities\n\nA high ratio normally suggests good liquidity, but too high a ratio suggests inefficient use of resources. Many strong companies use sophisticated management techniques to minimize funds invested in current assets, and as a result, have quick ratios that are very low.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 29}], "format": "plain", "slides": [29], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t18", "title": "Appendix 8A: Deferred Income Tax Assets and Liabilities", "body": "Permanent differences → arise from specific differences between rules that govern the preparation of financial statements (IFRS) and tax returns (Income Tax Act). Specifically, some types of revenue are exempt from tax while other types of expenses are not deductible in computing taxable income.\n\nTemporary Differences → are timing differences that cause deferred income taxes and will reverse, or turn around, in the future.\n\nTemporary differences of the following types result in complex accounting:\n\nProduct warranty costs that are recognized as a liability and an expense for financial reporting purposes when the related products are sold, but they are deductible for tax purposes only when payments under the warranty are made.\n\nLong-lived assets, including development costs, which are usually depreciated by using the straight-line method for financial reporting purposes, are depreciated on an accelerated basis (capital cost allowance) for tax purposes.\n\nIFRS is the set of rules for preparing financial statements, which results in income tax expense.\n\nThe Income Tax Act is the set of rules for preparing tax returns, which results in income taxes payable.\n\nUsually, income tax expense does not equal income tax payable.\n\nThe difference between income tax expense and income tax payable is recorded in an account called a Deferred Income Tax.", "blocks": [{"body": "Permanent differences → arise from specific differences between rules that govern the preparation of financial statements (IFRS) and tax returns (Income Tax Act). Specifically, some types of revenue are exempt from tax while other types of expenses are not deductible in computing taxable income.\n\nTemporary Differences → are timing differences that cause deferred income taxes and will reverse, or turn around, in the future.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 30}, {"body": "Temporary differences of the following types result in complex accounting:\n\nProduct warranty costs that are recognized as a liability and an expense for financial reporting purposes when the related products are sold, but they are deductible for tax purposes only when payments under the warranty are made.\n\nLong-lived assets, including development costs, which are usually depreciated by using the straight-line method for financial reporting purposes, are depreciated on an accelerated basis (capital cost allowance) for tax purposes.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 31}, {"body": "IFRS is the set of rules for preparing financial statements, which results in income tax expense.\n\nThe Income Tax Act is the set of rules for preparing tax returns, which results in income taxes payable.\n\nUsually, income tax expense does not equal income tax payable.\n\nThe difference between income tax expense and income tax payable is recorded in an account called a Deferred Income Tax.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 32}], "format": "plain", "slides": [30, 31, 32], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t19", "title": "Appendix 8B: Present Value Concepts", "body": "The concept of present value (PV) is based on the time value of money that we introduced earlier in this chapter.\n\nIt provides a foundation for measuring and reporting long-term notes and bonds.\n\nPresent value is the current cash equivalent of an amount to be received in the future, or a future amount discounted for compound interest.\n\nMoney received today is worth more than money to be received one year from today (or at any other future date) because it can be used to earn interest.\n\nWith a present value problem, you know the dollar amount of a cash flow that will occur in the future and need to determine its value now.\n\nThe opposite situation occurs when you know the dollar amount of a cash flow that occurs today and need to determine its value at some point in the future.\n\nThese problems are called future value problems.\n\nFuture value is the sum to which an amount will increase as a result of compound interest.\n\nPresent and future value problems may involve two types of cash flow: a single payment or an annuity (a series of cash payments).\n\nTherefore, four different situations are related to the time value of money:\n\nPresent value of a single payment\n\nFuture value of a single payment\n\nPresent value of an annuity\n\nFuture value of an annuity", "blocks": [{"body": "The concept of present value (PV) is based on the time value of money that we introduced earlier in this chapter.\n\nIt provides a foundation for measuring and reporting long-term notes and bonds.\n\nPresent value is the current cash equivalent of an amount to be received in the future, or a future amount discounted for compound interest.\n\nMoney received today is worth more than money to be received one year from today (or at any other future date) because it can be used to earn interest.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 33}, {"body": "With a present value problem, you know the dollar amount of a cash flow that will occur in the future and need to determine its value now.\n\nThe opposite situation occurs when you know the dollar amount of a cash flow that occurs today and need to determine its value at some point in the future.\n\nThese problems are called future value problems.\n\nFuture value is the sum to which an amount will increase as a result of compound interest.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 34}, {"body": "Present and future value problems may involve two types of cash flow: a single payment or an annuity (a series of cash payments).\n\nTherefore, four different situations are related to the time value of money:\n\nPresent value of a single payment\n\nFuture value of a single payment\n\nPresent value of an annuity\n\nFuture value of an annuity", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 35}], "format": "plain", "slides": [33, 34, 35], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t20", "title": "Appendix 8B: Present Value of a Single Amount", "body": "The present value of a single amount is the amount of cash you are willing to accept today in lieu of a cash receipt at some date in the future.\n\nTo calculate the present value of an amount to be received in the future, we subtract interest that is earned over time from the amount to be received in the future.\n\nYou discount the amount to be received in the future at interest rate i for n periods.\n\nThe formula to calculate the present value of a single amount is:", "blocks": [{"body": "The present value of a single amount is the amount of cash you are willing to accept today in lieu of a cash receipt at some date in the future.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch8-image4.png", "alt": "Appendix 8B: Present Value of a Single Amount — original slide 36"}], "slide": 36}, {"body": "To calculate the present value of an amount to be received in the future, we subtract interest that is earned over time from the amount to be received in the future.\n\nYou discount the amount to be received in the future at interest rate i for n periods.\n\nThe formula to calculate the present value of a single amount is:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch8-image5.jpeg", "alt": "Appendix 8B: Present Value of a Single Amount — original slide 37"}], "slide": 37}], "format": "plain", "slides": [36, 37], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t21", "title": "Appendix 8B: Present Value of an Annuity", "body": "An annuity is a series of consecutive payments characterized by:\n\nAn equal dollar amount each interest period\n\nInterest periods of equal length (year, half-year, quarter, or month)\n\nAn equal interest rate each interest period\n\nExamples of annuities are monthly payments on an automobile or a home loan, annual contributions to a savings account, and monthly retirement benefits.\n\nThe present value of an annuity is the value now of a series of equal amounts to be received (or paid) each period for some specified number of periods in the future.\n\nIt is calculated by discounting each of the equal periodic amounts.\n\nA good example of this type of problem is a retirement program that offers the retiree a monthly income after retirement.\n\nThe present value of an annuity of $1 for three periods at 4 percent may be represented graphically, as follows:", "blocks": [{"body": "An annuity is a series of consecutive payments characterized by:\n\nAn equal dollar amount each interest period\n\nInterest periods of equal length (year, half-year, quarter, or month)\n\nAn equal interest rate each interest period\n\nExamples of annuities are monthly payments on an automobile or a home loan, annual contributions to a savings account, and monthly retirement benefits.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 38}, {"body": "The present value of an annuity is the value now of a series of equal amounts to be received (or paid) each period for some specified number of periods in the future.\n\nIt is calculated by discounting each of the equal periodic amounts.\n\nA good example of this type of problem is a retirement program that offers the retiree a monthly income after retirement.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 39}, {"body": "The present value of an annuity of $1 for three periods at 4 percent may be represented graphically, as follows:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch8-image6.png", "alt": "Appendix 8B: Present Value of an Annuity — original slide 40"}], "slide": 40}], "format": "plain", "slides": [38, 39, 40], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t22", "title": "Appendix 8E: Future Value of a Single Amount", "body": "The future value of a single amount → calculating how much money you will have in the future as a result of investing a certain amount at the present time.\n\nTo solve a future value problem, you need to know three items:\n\nThe amount to be invested.\n\nThe interest rate (i) that the amount will earn.\n\nThe number of periods (n) in which the amount will earn interest.\n\nSince the future value concept is based on compound interest, the amount of interest for each period is calculated by multiplying the interest rate by the principal plus any interest not paid out in prior periods.\n\nGraphically, the calculation of the future value of $1 for three periods and an interest rate of 4 percent may be represented as follows:", "blocks": [{"body": "The future value of a single amount → calculating how much money you will have in the future as a result of investing a certain amount at the present time.\n\nTo solve a future value problem, you need to know three items:\n\nThe amount to be invested.\n\nThe interest rate (i) that the amount will earn.\n\nThe number of periods (n) in which the amount will earn interest.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 41}, {"body": "Since the future value concept is based on compound interest, the amount of interest for each period is calculated by multiplying the interest rate by the principal plus any interest not paid out in prior periods.\n\nGraphically, the calculation of the future value of $1 for three periods and an interest rate of 4 percent may be represented as follows:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch8-image7.png", "alt": "Appendix 8E: Future Value of a Single Amount — original slide 42"}], "slide": 42}], "format": "plain", "slides": [41, 42], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}, {"id": "c8-t23", "title": "Appendix 8E: Future Value of an Annuity", "body": "The future value of an annuity calculation will tell you how much money you will have in the future, if you save a fixed amount of money each month.\n\nThe future value of an annuity includes compound interest on each payment from the date of payment to the end of the term of the annuity.\n\nEach new payment accumulates less interest than prior payments only because the number of periods remaining to accumulate interest decreases.\n\nThe future value of an annuity of $1 for three periods at 4 percent may be represented graphically as follows:", "blocks": [{"body": "The future value of an annuity calculation will tell you how much money you will have in the future, if you save a fixed amount of money each month.\n\nThe future value of an annuity includes compound interest on each payment from the date of payment to the end of the term of the annuity.\n\nEach new payment accumulates less interest than prior payments only because the number of periods remaining to accumulate interest decreases.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 43}, {"body": "The future value of an annuity of $1 for three periods at 4 percent may be represented graphically as follows:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch8-image8.png", "alt": "Appendix 8E: Future Value of an Annuity — original slide 44"}], "slide": 44}], "format": "plain", "slides": [43, 44], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 8 slides"}], "deck": "sources/chapter-8.pptx"}, {"id": 9, "title": "Reporting and Interpreting Non-current Liabilities", "topics": [{"id": "c9-t1", "title": "Understanding the Business", "body": "Non-current liabilities include all of the entity’s obligations that are not classified as current liabilities, such as long-term notes and bonds payable.\n\nTypically, a non-current liability will require payment more than one year in the future. These obligations may be created by borrowing money, or they may result from other activities.\n\nThe mixture of debt and equity used to finance a company’s growth is called the capital structure:\n\nDebt – Funds from creditors\n\nEquity – Funds from shareholders", "blocks": [{"body": "Non-current liabilities include all of the entity’s obligations that are not classified as current liabilities, such as long-term notes and bonds payable.\n\nTypically, a non-current liability will require payment more than one year in the future. These obligations may be created by borrowing money, or they may result from other activities.\n\nThe mixture of debt and equity used to finance a company’s growth is called the capital structure:\n\nDebt – Funds from creditors\n\nEquity – Funds from shareholders", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 4}], "format": "plain", "slides": [4], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t2", "title": "Reasons Why Companies Issue Debt", "body": "Use of long-term debt offers significant advantages:\n\nShareholders maintain control. Issuing bonds allows shareholders to maintain their current level of control. Debtholders do not participate in management decisions, including decisions to distribute dividends to shareholders.\n\nInterest expense is tax deductible. The deductibility of interest for tax purposes reduces the net cost of borrowing. In contrast, dividends are not tax deductible.\n\nIssuing bonds can increase the return to shareholders. If a company can borrow at a low interest rate and invest in projects that earn a high rate of return, it can increase the return to shareholders.", "blocks": [{"body": "Use of long-term debt offers significant advantages:\n\nShareholders maintain control. Issuing bonds allows shareholders to maintain their current level of control. Debtholders do not participate in management decisions, including decisions to distribute dividends to shareholders.\n\nInterest expense is tax deductible. The deductibility of interest for tax purposes reduces the net cost of borrowing. In contrast, dividends are not tax deductible.\n\nIssuing bonds can increase the return to shareholders. If a company can borrow at a low interest rate and invest in projects that earn a high rate of return, it can increase the return to shareholders.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 5}], "format": "plain", "slides": [5], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t3", "title": "Disadvantages to Using Long-Term Debt", "body": "There are two potential disadvantages to using long-term debt:\n\nRisk of bankruptcy. Interest payments to the debtholders must be made each period whether the corporation generates net earnings or incurs a loss.\n\nNegative impact on cash flows. Debt must be repaid at a specific time in the future. Management must be able to generate sufficient cash to repay the debt or have the ability to refinance it.", "blocks": [{"body": "There are two potential disadvantages to using long-term debt:\n\nRisk of bankruptcy. Interest payments to the debtholders must be made each period whether the corporation generates net earnings or incurs a loss.\n\nNegative impact on cash flows. Debt must be repaid at a specific time in the future. Management must be able to generate sufficient cash to repay the debt or have the ability to refinance it.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 6}], "format": "plain", "slides": [6], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t4", "title": "Types of Long-Term Debt", "body": "Long-term debt is available to companies in various forms:\n\nBank loans\n\nNotes\n\nMortgages\n\nBonds and Debentures", "blocks": [{"body": "Long-term debt is available to companies in various forms:\n\nBank loans\n\nNotes\n\nMortgages\n\nBonds and Debentures", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 7}], "format": "plain", "slides": [7], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t5", "title": "Bond Terminology", "body": "A bond usually requires the payment of interest over its life, with the repayment of principal on the maturity date.\n\nThe bond principal is:\n\nThe amount payable at the maturity date and\n\nThe basis for computing periodic cash interest payments.\n\nThe principal also is called the par value, face value, or maturity value.\n\nAll bonds have a par value, which is the amount that will be paid when the bond matures. For most individual bonds, the par value is $1,000, but it can be any amount.\n\nA bond always specifies a coupon rate (also called the stated rate, contract rate, or nominal rate) and the frequency of periodic cash interest payments.\n\nThese interest payments are sometimes called coupon payments.\n\nA bond’s coupon rate is always stated in annual terms.\n\nThe following table reflects how a bond’s cash interest payment is calculated when interest is paid annually, semi-annually, and quarterly. The face value of the bond is $1,000 and the coupon rate is 8 percent.\n\nIf the bond pays interest annually, bondholders will receive one interest payment of $80 during the year. If the bond pays interest semi-annually, bondholders will receive a $40 interest payment every six months, for a total of $80 a year. If the bond pays interest quarterly, bondholders will receive a $20 interest payment every three months, for a total of $80 a year. Note that in all cases, $80 in interest is paid per year.", "blocks": [{"body": "A bond usually requires the payment of interest over its life, with the repayment of principal on the maturity date.\n\nThe bond principal is:\n\nThe amount payable at the maturity date and\n\nThe basis for computing periodic cash interest payments.\n\nThe principal also is called the par value, face value, or maturity value.\n\nAll bonds have a par value, which is the amount that will be paid when the bond matures. For most individual bonds, the par value is $1,000, but it can be any amount.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 8}, {"body": "A bond always specifies a coupon rate (also called the stated rate, contract rate, or nominal rate) and the frequency of periodic cash interest payments.\n\nThese interest payments are sometimes called coupon payments.\n\nA bond’s coupon rate is always stated in annual terms.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 9}, {"body": "The following table reflects how a bond’s cash interest payment is calculated when interest is paid annually, semi-annually, and quarterly. The face value of the bond is $1,000 and the coupon rate is 8 percent.\n\nIf the bond pays interest annually, bondholders will receive one interest payment of $80 during the year. If the bond pays interest semi-annually, bondholders will receive a $40 interest payment every six months, for a total of $80 a year. If the bond pays interest quarterly, bondholders will receive a $20 interest payment every three months, for a total of $80 a year. Note that in all cases, $80 in interest is paid per year.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image2.png", "alt": "Bond Terminology — original slide 10"}], "slide": 10}], "format": "plain", "slides": [8, 9, 10], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t6", "title": "Bond Terminology - Example", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image3.png", "alt": "Bond Terminology - Example — original slide 11"}], "slide": 11}], "format": "plain", "slides": [11], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t7", "title": "Bond Characteristics", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image4.png", "alt": "Bond Characteristics — original slide 12"}], "slide": 12}], "format": "plain", "slides": [12], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t8", "title": "Bond Issuance Process", "body": "An indenture is a bond contract that specifies the legal provisions of a bond issue.\n\nThe bond indenture contains covenants designed to protect the creditors.\n\nThe bond issuer also prepares a prospectus, which describes the company, the bonds, and how the proceeds of the bonds will be used.\n\nWhen a bond is issued, the investor receives a bond certificate.\n\nAll of the bond certificates for the same bond issuance, whether an actual paper certificate or an electronic certificate, contain the same information.\n\nThe certificates show the same maturity date, interest rate, interest dates, and other provisions.\n\nAn independent party, called the trustee, is usually appointed to represent the bondholders.\n\nA trustee’s duties are to ascertain whether the issuing company fulfills all of the provisions of the bond contract.", "blocks": [{"body": "An indenture is a bond contract that specifies the legal provisions of a bond issue.\n\nThe bond indenture contains covenants designed to protect the creditors.\n\nThe bond issuer also prepares a prospectus, which describes the company, the bonds, and how the proceeds of the bonds will be used.\n\nWhen a bond is issued, the investor receives a bond certificate.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 13}, {"body": "All of the bond certificates for the same bond issuance, whether an actual paper certificate or an electronic certificate, contain the same information.\n\nThe certificates show the same maturity date, interest rate, interest dates, and other provisions.\n\nAn independent party, called the trustee, is usually appointed to represent the bondholders.\n\nA trustee’s duties are to ascertain whether the issuing company fulfills all of the provisions of the bond contract.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 14}], "format": "plain", "slides": [13, 14], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t9", "title": "Reporting Note Transactions", "body": "The terms of a debt agreement are negotiated between the lender and the borrower.\n\nThe debt contract specifies the interest rate on the debt and the dates of payment of both interest and principal.\n\nNotes payable → the lender and the borrower may agree that the principal will be paid at a specific date in the future (the maturity date), plus annual or semi-annual interest payments.\n\nAlternatively, they may agree that equal portions of the principal are payable annually with interest on the debt, or that equal payments covering both principal and interest known as blended payments, will be made periodically until the principal is fully paid at maturity.", "blocks": [{"body": "The terms of a debt agreement are negotiated between the lender and the borrower.\n\nThe debt contract specifies the interest rate on the debt and the dates of payment of both interest and principal.\n\nNotes payable → the lender and the borrower may agree that the principal will be paid at a specific date in the future (the maturity date), plus annual or semi-annual interest payments.\n\nAlternatively, they may agree that equal portions of the principal are payable annually with interest on the debt, or that equal payments covering both principal and interest known as blended payments, will be made periodically until the principal is fully paid at maturity.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 15}], "format": "plain", "slides": [15], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t10", "title": "Reporting Bond Transactions", "body": "When bonds (or notes) are issued, two types of cash payments need to be specified in the bond contract:\n\nPrincipal. This is usually a single payment made when the bond matures. It is also called the par value, face value, or maturity value.\n\nCash interest payments. These payments are computed by multiplying the principal amount times the coupon rate. The bond contract specifies whether these payments are made quarterly, semi-annually, or annually.\n\nThe issue price of the bond is determined by the market, based on the time value of money.\n\nThe interest rate used to compute the present value is the market interest rate (also known as the yield, or the effective interest rate).\n\nAs market interest rates rise the price of a bond falls and vice versa.\n\nThis is because of the important role the market interest rate plays in determining the present value of a bond.\n\nWhen a company issues a bond, the market interest rate will be either the same as the coupon rate, greater than the coupon rate, or less than the coupon rate.\n\nThe relationship between the market interest rate and the bond’s coupon rate determines whether the bond is issued at par, at a premium, or at a discount.\n\nIf the coupon rate and the market interest rate are the same, a bond sells at par.\n\nIf a bond pays a coupon rate that is lower than the market rate that creditors demand, they will not buy it unless its price is reduced (i.e., a discount must be provided).\n\nIf a bond pays a coupon rate that is higher than the market rate, then creditors will pay a premium to buy it.\n\n\n\nCorporations and creditors are indifferent to whether a bond is issued at par, at a discount, or at a premium, because bonds are always priced to provide the market rate of interest.", "blocks": [{"body": "When bonds (or notes) are issued, two types of cash payments need to be specified in the bond contract:\n\nPrincipal. This is usually a single payment made when the bond matures. It is also called the par value, face value, or maturity value.\n\nCash interest payments. These payments are computed by multiplying the principal amount times the coupon rate. The bond contract specifies whether these payments are made quarterly, semi-annually, or annually.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 16}, {"body": "The issue price of the bond is determined by the market, based on the time value of money.\n\nThe interest rate used to compute the present value is the market interest rate (also known as the yield, or the effective interest rate).\n\nAs market interest rates rise the price of a bond falls and vice versa.\n\nThis is because of the important role the market interest rate plays in determining the present value of a bond.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 17}, {"body": "When a company issues a bond, the market interest rate will be either the same as the coupon rate, greater than the coupon rate, or less than the coupon rate.\n\nThe relationship between the market interest rate and the bond’s coupon rate determines whether the bond is issued at par, at a premium, or at a discount.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 18}, {"body": "If the coupon rate and the market interest rate are the same, a bond sells at par.\n\nIf a bond pays a coupon rate that is lower than the market rate that creditors demand, they will not buy it unless its price is reduced (i.e., a discount must be provided).\n\nIf a bond pays a coupon rate that is higher than the market rate, then creditors will pay a premium to buy it.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 19}, {"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image5.png", "alt": "Reporting Bond Transactions — original slide 20"}], "slide": 20}, {"body": "Corporations and creditors are indifferent to whether a bond is issued at par, at a discount, or at a premium, because bonds are always priced to provide the market rate of interest.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image6.png", "alt": "Reporting Bond Transactions — original slide 21"}], "slide": 21}], "format": "plain", "slides": [16, 17, 18, 19, 20, 21], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t11", "title": "Reporting Bond Transactions – Data Analytics", "body": "Using Artificial Intelligence (AI) to Invest in Bonds\n\nIndividuals who invest in bonds often spend considerable time and effort gathering and assessing vast amounts of market information as well as company-specific information.\n\nUsing technology to expand and expedite this process would allow investors to make more informed, quicker decisions.\n\nAllianceBernstein, a global investment-management and research firm, has created Abbie, a virtual assistant based on artificial intelligence that helps investors make bond investing decisions.\n\nYou can read more about Abbie’s abilities here: alliancebernstein.com/fixed-income/bonds/abbie.htm", "blocks": [{"body": "Using Artificial Intelligence (AI) to Invest in Bonds\n\nIndividuals who invest in bonds often spend considerable time and effort gathering and assessing vast amounts of market information as well as company-specific information.\n\nUsing technology to expand and expedite this process would allow investors to make more informed, quicker decisions.\n\nAllianceBernstein, a global investment-management and research firm, has created Abbie, a virtual assistant based on artificial intelligence that helps investors make bond investing decisions.\n\nYou can read more about Abbie’s abilities here: alliancebernstein.com/fixed-income/bonds/abbie.htm", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 22}], "format": "plain", "slides": [22], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t12", "title": "Bonds Issued at Par", "body": "On January 1, 2022, Mobile Services Corporation (MSC) issued 6% bonds with a par value of $400,000 and received $400,000 in cash.  The bonds mature in 10 years and interest is paid semi-annually.\n\nWhen MSC issued its bonds, it agreed to make two types of payments in the future: a single payment of $400,000 when the bond matures in 10 years and an annuity of $12,000 payable twice each year for 10 years. The bond payments can be shown graphically, as follows:\n\nThe present value of the bond payments can be calculated with the PV tables by using the factor for 20 periods and an interest rate of 3 percent (6 percent ÷ 2):\n\nWhen the effective rate of interest equals the stated rate of interest, the present value of the future cash flows associated with a bond always equals the bond’s par value.\n\nA bond’s selling price is determined by the present value of its future cash flows, not the par value.\n\nOn the date of issue, bond liabilities are recorded at the amount creditors are willing to pay for the bonds:", "blocks": [{"body": "On January 1, 2022, Mobile Services Corporation (MSC) issued 6% bonds with a par value of $400,000 and received $400,000 in cash.  The bonds mature in 10 years and interest is paid semi-annually.\n\nWhen MSC issued its bonds, it agreed to make two types of payments in the future: a single payment of $400,000 when the bond matures in 10 years and an annuity of $12,000 payable twice each year for 10 years. The bond payments can be shown graphically, as follows:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image7.png", "alt": "Bonds Issued at Par — original slide 23"}], "slide": 23}, {"body": "The present value of the bond payments can be calculated with the PV tables by using the factor for 20 periods and an interest rate of 3 percent (6 percent ÷ 2):\n\nWhen the effective rate of interest equals the stated rate of interest, the present value of the future cash flows associated with a bond always equals the bond’s par value.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image8.png", "alt": "Bonds Issued at Par — original slide 24"}], "slide": 24}, {"body": "A bond’s selling price is determined by the present value of its future cash flows, not the par value.\n\nOn the date of issue, bond liabilities are recorded at the amount creditors are willing to pay for the bonds:", "tables": [[["Cash (A)", "", "", "", "", "", "400,000", " "], ["     Bonds payable (L)", "", "", "", "", "", " ", "400,000"], ["Assets", "", "=", "Liabilities", "", "+", "Shareholders’ Equity", ""], ["Cash", "+ 400,000", " ", "Bonds payable", "+ 400,000", " ", " ", " "]]], "images": [], "slide": 25}], "format": "plain", "slides": [23, 24, 25], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t13", "title": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at Par", "body": "MSC will pay interest at 3 percent (i.e., 6 percent per year) on the par value of the bonds each June 30 and December 31 until the bond’s maturity date.\n\nThe amount of interest each period will be $12,000 (3% × $400,000). The entry to record the interest payments follows:", "blocks": [{"body": "MSC will pay interest at 3 percent (i.e., 6 percent per year) on the par value of the bonds each June 30 and December 31 until the bond’s maturity date.\n\nThe amount of interest each period will be $12,000 (3% × $400,000). The entry to record the interest payments follows:", "tables": [[["Bond interest expense (E)", "", "", "", "", "", "12,000", " ", ""], ["     Cash (A)", "", "", "", "", "", " ", "12,000", ""], ["Assets", "", "=", "Liabilities", "", "+", "Shareholders’ Equity", "", ""], ["Cash ", "−12,000", " ", " ", " ", " ", "Bond interest expense", "−12,000", "−12,000"]]], "images": [], "slide": 26}], "format": "plain", "slides": [26], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t14", "title": "Bonds Issued at a Discount", "body": "On January 1, 2022, MSC issued 6% bonds with a par value of $400,000.  The bonds mature in 10 years and interest is paid semi-annually.  The market rate is 8% annually.  Therefore, since the stated rate is less than the market rate, this bond is issued at a discount.\n\nTo calculate the issue price of the bonds, we need to calculate the present value of the future cash flows specified on the bond.\n\nThe number of periods is 20, but we use an interest rate of 4 percent (8 percent ÷ 2), which is the market rate of interest.\n\nThe issue price of MSC’s bonds is calculated as follows:\n\nThe cash price of the bonds issued by MSC is $345,644.\n\nSome people refer to this price as 86.4, which means that the bonds were sold at 86.4 percent of their par value ($345,644 ÷ $400,000).\n\nOn the date of issue, MSC records a bond liability equal to the amount creditors are willing to pay for the bonds:\n\nWhile MSC received only $345,644 when it sold the bonds, it must repay $400,000 when the bonds mature.\n\nThe $54,356 of additional cash that MSC must pay at maturity is incrementally reflected in interest expense and added to the carrying amount of the liability each period over the life of the bond.\n\nThis causes interest expense to reflect the market interest rate while the cash being paid to bondholders each period reflects the coupon rate.\n\nThe accounting method used to record interest expense is referred to as the effective-interest method.\n\nCanadian publicly accountable enterprises are required to use the effective-interest method to amortize bond discount or premium.", "blocks": [{"body": "On January 1, 2022, MSC issued 6% bonds with a par value of $400,000.  The bonds mature in 10 years and interest is paid semi-annually.  The market rate is 8% annually.  Therefore, since the stated rate is less than the market rate, this bond is issued at a discount.\n\nTo calculate the issue price of the bonds, we need to calculate the present value of the future cash flows specified on the bond.\n\nThe number of periods is 20, but we use an interest rate of 4 percent (8 percent ÷ 2), which is the market rate of interest.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 27}, {"body": "The issue price of MSC’s bonds is calculated as follows:\n\nThe cash price of the bonds issued by MSC is $345,644.\n\nSome people refer to this price as 86.4, which means that the bonds were sold at 86.4 percent of their par value ($345,644 ÷ $400,000).", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image9.png", "alt": "Bonds Issued at a Discount — original slide 28"}], "slide": 28}, {"body": "On the date of issue, MSC records a bond liability equal to the amount creditors are willing to pay for the bonds:\n\nWhile MSC received only $345,644 when it sold the bonds, it must repay $400,000 when the bonds mature.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image10.png", "alt": "Bonds Issued at a Discount — original slide 29"}], "slide": 29}, {"body": "The $54,356 of additional cash that MSC must pay at maturity is incrementally reflected in interest expense and added to the carrying amount of the liability each period over the life of the bond.\n\nThis causes interest expense to reflect the market interest rate while the cash being paid to bondholders each period reflects the coupon rate.\n\nThe accounting method used to record interest expense is referred to as the effective-interest method.\n\nCanadian publicly accountable enterprises are required to use the effective-interest method to amortize bond discount or premium.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 30}], "format": "plain", "slides": [27, 28, 29, 30], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t15", "title": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Discount using Effective-Interest Amortization - 1", "body": "Step 1: Compute the interest expense:\n\nInterest expense = Unpaid balance x Market interest rate x n/12\n\nn = number of months in each interest period\n\nStep 2: Compute the interest to be paid:\n\nBond face value x Coupon rate per period\n\nStep 3: Compute the amortization amount:\n\nAmortization of bond discount =    Interest expense\n\n– Interest paid (or accrued)", "blocks": [{"body": "Step 1: Compute the interest expense:\n\nInterest expense = Unpaid balance x Market interest rate x n/12\n\nn = number of months in each interest period\n\nStep 2: Compute the interest to be paid:\n\nBond face value x Coupon rate per period\n\nStep 3: Compute the amortization amount:\n\nAmortization of bond discount =    Interest expense\n\n– Interest paid (or accrued)", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 31}], "format": "plain", "slides": [31], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t16", "title": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Discount using Effective-Interest Amortization - 2", "body": "The first interest payment on MSC’s bonds is on June 30, 2022. Interest expense and the amount of the bond discount amortized are:\n\nStep 1: Interest expense:\n\n$345,644 × (0.08 × 6/12)  = $13,826\n\nStep 2: Interest to be paid:\n\n$400,000 × (0.06 × 6/12) = $12,000\n\nStep 3: Amortized discount:\n\n$13,826 − $12,000 = $1,826", "blocks": [{"body": "The first interest payment on MSC’s bonds is on June 30, 2022. Interest expense and the amount of the bond discount amortized are:\n\nStep 1: Interest expense:\n\n$345,644 × (0.08 × 6/12)  = $13,826\n\nStep 2: Interest to be paid:\n\n$400,000 × (0.06 × 6/12) = $12,000\n\nStep 3: Amortized discount:\n\n$13,826 − $12,000 = $1,826", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 32}], "format": "plain", "slides": [32], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t17", "title": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Discount using Effective-Interest Amortization - 3", "body": "The journal entry to record the periodic interest expense is as follows:", "blocks": [{"body": "The journal entry to record the periodic interest expense is as follows:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image11.png", "alt": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Discount using Effective-Interest Amortization - 3 — original slide 33"}], "slide": 33}], "format": "plain", "slides": [33], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t18", "title": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Discount using Effective-Interest Amortization - 4", "body": "Interest expense for the second half of 2022 is calculated by multiplying the carrying amount on June 30, 2022 (which is $345,644 plus $1,826, or $347,470) by the market interest rate for six months ($347,470 × (0.08 × 6/12) = $13,899).\n\nThe amortization of the bond discount in the second period is $1,899.", "blocks": [{"body": "Interest expense for the second half of 2022 is calculated by multiplying the carrying amount on June 30, 2022 (which is $345,644 plus $1,826, or $347,470) by the market interest rate for six months ($347,470 × (0.08 × 6/12) = $13,899).\n\nThe amortization of the bond discount in the second period is $1,899.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image12.png", "alt": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Discount using Effective-Interest Amortization - 4 — original slide 34"}], "slide": 34}], "format": "plain", "slides": [34], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t19", "title": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Discount using Effective-Interest Amortization - 5", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image13.png", "alt": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Discount using Effective-Interest Amortization - 5 — original slide 35"}], "slide": 35}], "format": "plain", "slides": [35], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t20", "title": "Bonds Issued at a Premium", "body": "When the market interest rate is less than the bond’s coupon rate, the bonds sell at a premium.\n\nWe use the same MSC example as before but assume that the market interest rate is 4 percent while the coupon rate remains the same, at 6 percent. The bonds are issued on January 1, 2022; pay interest semi-annually; and mature in 10 years.\n\nThe present value of the bonds can be calculated from the tables using the factor for 20 periods and an interest rate of 2 percent per period (4% × ½ year):\n\nAccounting for bonds issued at a premium is similar to accounting for bonds issued at a discount.\n\nThe January 1, 2022, issuance of MSC’s bonds at a premium would be recorded as follows:\n\nWhile MSC received  $465,417 when it sold the bonds, it must repay only $400,000 when the bonds mature.\n\nThe $65,417 of additional cash that MSC received is repaid gradually over time as part of the periodic interest payments that exceed the amount of interest that the bondholders demanded for their investment in these bonds.\n\nThe difference between the interest payment and the interest expense reduces the carrying amount of the liability each period over the life of the bond.\n\nThe interest expense reflects the market interest rate while the cash being paid to bondholders each period reflects the coupon rate.", "blocks": [{"body": "When the market interest rate is less than the bond’s coupon rate, the bonds sell at a premium.\n\nWe use the same MSC example as before but assume that the market interest rate is 4 percent while the coupon rate remains the same, at 6 percent. The bonds are issued on January 1, 2022; pay interest semi-annually; and mature in 10 years.\n\nThe present value of the bonds can be calculated from the tables using the factor for 20 periods and an interest rate of 2 percent per period (4% × ½ year):", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image14.png", "alt": "Bonds Issued at a Premium — original slide 36"}], "slide": 36}, {"body": "Accounting for bonds issued at a premium is similar to accounting for bonds issued at a discount.\n\nThe January 1, 2022, issuance of MSC’s bonds at a premium would be recorded as follows:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image15.png", "alt": "Bonds Issued at a Premium — original slide 37"}], "slide": 37}, {"body": "While MSC received  $465,417 when it sold the bonds, it must repay only $400,000 when the bonds mature.\n\nThe $65,417 of additional cash that MSC received is repaid gradually over time as part of the periodic interest payments that exceed the amount of interest that the bondholders demanded for their investment in these bonds.\n\nThe difference between the interest payment and the interest expense reduces the carrying amount of the liability each period over the life of the bond.\n\nThe interest expense reflects the market interest rate while the cash being paid to bondholders each period reflects the coupon rate.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 38}], "format": "plain", "slides": [36, 37, 38], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t21", "title": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Premium using Effective-Interest Amortization - 1", "body": "Step 1: Compute the interest expense:\n\nInterest expense = Unpaid balance x Effective interest rate x n/12\n\nn = number of months in each interest period\n\nStep 2: Compute the interest to be paid:\n\nBond face value x Coupon rate per period\n\nStep 3: Compute the amortization amount:\n\nAmortization of bond premium =   Interest paid (or accrued)\n\n– Interest expense", "blocks": [{"body": "Step 1: Compute the interest expense:\n\nInterest expense = Unpaid balance x Effective interest rate x n/12\n\nn = number of months in each interest period\n\nStep 2: Compute the interest to be paid:\n\nBond face value x Coupon rate per period\n\nStep 3: Compute the amortization amount:\n\nAmortization of bond premium =   Interest paid (or accrued)\n\n– Interest expense", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 39}], "format": "plain", "slides": [39], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t22", "title": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Premium using Effective-Interest Amortization - 2", "body": "Step 1: Interest expense:\n\n$465,417 × (0.04 × 6/12)  = $9,308\n\nStep 2: Interest to be paid:\n\n$400,000 × (0.06 × 6/12) = $12,000\n\nStep 3: Amortized premium:\n\n$12,000 − $9,308 = $2,692", "blocks": [{"body": "Step 1: Interest expense:\n\n$465,417 × (0.04 × 6/12)  = $9,308\n\nStep 2: Interest to be paid:\n\n$400,000 × (0.06 × 6/12) = $12,000\n\nStep 3: Amortized premium:\n\n$12,000 − $9,308 = $2,692", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 40}], "format": "plain", "slides": [40], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t23", "title": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Premium using Effective-Interest Amortization - 3", "body": "The first payment of interest on the bonds is recorded, as follows:", "blocks": [{"body": "The first payment of interest on the bonds is recorded, as follows:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image16.png", "alt": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Premium using Effective-Interest Amortization - 3 — original slide 41"}], "slide": 41}], "format": "plain", "slides": [41], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t24", "title": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Premium using Effective-Interest Amortization - 4", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image17.png", "alt": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Premium using Effective-Interest Amortization - 4 — original slide 42"}], "slide": 42}], "format": "plain", "slides": [42], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t25", "title": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Premium using Effective-Interest Amortization - 5", "body": "The basic difference between effective-interest amortization of a bond discount and a bond premium is that the amortization of a discount increases the carrying amount of the liability and the amortization of a premium reduces it.\n\nBoth serve the same purpose: to bring the liability to the bond’s face value at the maturity date.\n\nThis is illustrated on the next slide, which shows how the carrying amount of a bond changes over its life when the bond is issued at par, at a discount, or at a premium.", "blocks": [{"body": "The basic difference between effective-interest amortization of a bond discount and a bond premium is that the amortization of a discount increases the carrying amount of the liability and the amortization of a premium reduces it.\n\nBoth serve the same purpose: to bring the liability to the bond’s face value at the maturity date.\n\nThis is illustrated on the next slide, which shows how the carrying amount of a bond changes over its life when the bond is issued at par, at a discount, or at a premium.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 43}], "format": "plain", "slides": [43], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t26", "title": "The Change in the Carrying Amount of a Bond over Time, Exhibit 9.6", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image18.png", "alt": "The Change in the Carrying Amount of a Bond over Time, Exhibit 9.6 — original slide 44"}], "slide": 44}], "format": "plain", "slides": [44], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t27", "title": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Premium using Effective-Interest Amortization - 6", "body": "Regardless of whether a company issues bonds at par, at a discount, or at a premium, the company will record the same journal entry when it retires the bonds at maturity.\n\nFor our MSC example, the journal entry would be:", "blocks": [{"body": "Regardless of whether a company issues bonds at par, at a discount, or at a premium, the company will record the same journal entry when it retires the bonds at maturity.\n\nFor our MSC example, the journal entry would be:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch9-image19.png", "alt": "Reporting Interest Expense on Bonds Issued at a Premium using Effective-Interest Amortization - 6 — original slide 45"}], "slide": 45}], "format": "plain", "slides": [45], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t28", "title": "Accounting for Issuance Costs", "body": "When companies issue bonds, they almost always hire underwriters to help them with the offering for a fee.\n\nThe amount of the fee is deducted from the proceeds of the bond issuance.\n\nIf after deducting fees the total amount received is less than the face value of the bonds, the bonds are issued at a discount.\n\nIf after deducting fees the amount received is greater than the face value of the bonds, the bonds are issued at a premium.\n\nThe examples in this chapter assume that the company issuing bonds does not incur any issuance costs.", "blocks": [{"body": "When companies issue bonds, they almost always hire underwriters to help them with the offering for a fee.\n\nThe amount of the fee is deducted from the proceeds of the bond issuance.\n\nIf after deducting fees the total amount received is less than the face value of the bonds, the bonds are issued at a discount.\n\nIf after deducting fees the amount received is greater than the face value of the bonds, the bonds are issued at a premium.\n\nThe examples in this chapter assume that the company issuing bonds does not incur any issuance costs.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 46}], "format": "plain", "slides": [46], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t29", "title": "Reporting Interest Expense Using Straight-Line Amortization", "body": "While the effective-interest method is the preferred method to amortize bond discount or premium, Canadian private enterprises may use straight-line amortization, which allocates an equal amount to each interest period, because it is relatively easy to compute the required numbers.\n\nPrivate enterprises are permitted to use the straight-line method because the amortized discount or premium that result from the two methods are normally not materially different.", "blocks": [{"body": "While the effective-interest method is the preferred method to amortize bond discount or premium, Canadian private enterprises may use straight-line amortization, which allocates an equal amount to each interest period, because it is relatively easy to compute the required numbers.\n\nPrivate enterprises are permitted to use the straight-line method because the amortized discount or premium that result from the two methods are normally not materially different.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 47}], "format": "plain", "slides": [47], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t30", "title": "Key Ratio Analysis – Times Interest Earned Ratio", "body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → Is the company generating sufficient resources (added value) from its profit-making activities to meet its current obligations associated with debt?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The times interest earned ratio is helpful in answering this question. It is calculated as follows:\n\nThe ratio shows the amount of resources generated for each dollar of interest expense.  In general, a high ratio is viewed more favourable than a low ratio.\n\nTimes InterestEarned Ratio\n\n=\n\nNet earnings + Interest expense + Income tax expense\n\nInterest expense", "blocks": [{"body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → Is the company generating sufficient resources (added value) from its profit-making activities to meet its current obligations associated with debt?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The times interest earned ratio is helpful in answering this question. It is calculated as follows:\n\nThe ratio shows the amount of resources generated for each dollar of interest expense.  In general, a high ratio is viewed more favourable than a low ratio.\n\nTimes InterestEarned Ratio\n\n=\n\nNet earnings + Interest expense + Income tax expense\n\nInterest expense", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 48}], "format": "plain", "slides": [48], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t31", "title": "Early Retirement of Debt", "body": "Bonds are normally issued for long periods, such as 20 or 30 years.\n\nBondholders who need cash prior to the maturity date can simply sell the bonds to another investor. This transaction does not affect the accounts of the company that issued the bonds.\n\nEach bond issue has characteristics specified in the bond indenture.\n\nThe issuing company often adds special characteristics to a bond to make it more attractive to investors that normally have a large number of investment alternatives from which to select.\n\nSome bonds have a call feature that allows the issuing company to call (retire) the bonds early.\n\nA call feature most often requires the issuing company to pay investors an amount greater than the bond’s face value to retire the bonds before their maturity date.\n\nThe amount often is stated as a percentage of the bond’s face value and is described in the bond indenture or bond prospectus.\n\nA company may elect to retire debt early by purchasing it on the open market, just as an investor would.\n\nThis approach is necessary when the bonds do not have a call feature.", "blocks": [{"body": "Bonds are normally issued for long periods, such as 20 or 30 years.\n\nBondholders who need cash prior to the maturity date can simply sell the bonds to another investor. This transaction does not affect the accounts of the company that issued the bonds.\n\nEach bond issue has characteristics specified in the bond indenture.\n\nThe issuing company often adds special characteristics to a bond to make it more attractive to investors that normally have a large number of investment alternatives from which to select.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 49}, {"body": "Some bonds have a call feature that allows the issuing company to call (retire) the bonds early.\n\nA call feature most often requires the issuing company to pay investors an amount greater than the bond’s face value to retire the bonds before their maturity date.\n\nThe amount often is stated as a percentage of the bond’s face value and is described in the bond indenture or bond prospectus.\n\nA company may elect to retire debt early by purchasing it on the open market, just as an investor would.\n\nThis approach is necessary when the bonds do not have a call feature.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 50}], "format": "plain", "slides": [49, 50], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t32", "title": "International Perspective – Borrowing in Foreign Currencies", "body": "Companies may elect to borrow in foreign markets\n\nTo reduce exchange rate risk.\n\nBecause interest rates often are low in other countries.\n\nFor reporting purposes, accountants must convert, or translate, foreign debt into Canadian dollars.\n\nAssume that Amazon borrowed 1 million euros. For Amazon’s annual report, the accountant must use the conversion rate as of the statement of financial position date, which we assume was €1.00 to US$1.1371.\n\n€1,000,000 × US$1.1371 = US$1,137,100\n\nThe debt will be reported at $1,137,100 on Amazon’s financial statements.", "blocks": [{"body": "Companies may elect to borrow in foreign markets\n\nTo reduce exchange rate risk.\n\nBecause interest rates often are low in other countries.\n\nFor reporting purposes, accountants must convert, or translate, foreign debt into Canadian dollars.\n\nAssume that Amazon borrowed 1 million euros. For Amazon’s annual report, the accountant must use the conversion rate as of the statement of financial position date, which we assume was €1.00 to US$1.1371.\n\n€1,000,000 × US$1.1371 = US$1,137,100\n\nThe debt will be reported at $1,137,100 on Amazon’s financial statements.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 51}], "format": "plain", "slides": [51], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t33", "title": "Lease Liabilities", "body": "Companies often lease rather than purchase assets.\n\nWhen a company leases an asset, it enters into a contractual agreement with the owner of the asset.\n\nThe party that owns the asset is referred to as the lessor.\n\nThe party that pays for the right to use the asset is referred to as the lessee.\n\nThis textbook focuses on accounting for leases from the lessee’s perspective.\n\nFor accounting purposes, a lessee can lease an asset by signing either a short-term lease (12 months or less) or a long-term lease.\n\nThe lease contract for a short-term lease does not transfer control of the asset to the lessee.\n\nFor long-term leases, which are more common, International Financial Reporting Standard (IFRS) 16 requires the lessee to recognize a right-of-use asset and a lease liability, measured at the present value of future lease payments.", "blocks": [{"body": "Companies often lease rather than purchase assets.\n\nWhen a company leases an asset, it enters into a contractual agreement with the owner of the asset.\n\nThe party that owns the asset is referred to as the lessor.\n\nThe party that pays for the right to use the asset is referred to as the lessee.\n\nThis textbook focuses on accounting for leases from the lessee’s perspective.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 52}, {"body": "For accounting purposes, a lessee can lease an asset by signing either a short-term lease (12 months or less) or a long-term lease.\n\nThe lease contract for a short-term lease does not transfer control of the asset to the lessee.\n\nFor long-term leases, which are more common, International Financial Reporting Standard (IFRS) 16 requires the lessee to recognize a right-of-use asset and a lease liability, measured at the present value of future lease payments.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 53}], "format": "plain", "slides": [52, 53], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t34", "title": "Key Ratio Analysis – Debt-To-Equity Ratio", "body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → As an investor who must decide whether or not to buy shares, it is important to know how much of the company’s assets is financed by creditors and how much is financed by owners. The debt-to-equity ratio is used to assess the debt capacity of a business. It is computed as follows:\n\nThe ratio shows the relationship between the amount of capital provided by owners and the amount provided by creditors.  In general, a high ratio suggest that a company relies heavily on funds provided by creditors.\n\nDebt-to-equityRatio\n\n=\n\nTotal Liabilities\n\nShareholders’ equity", "blocks": [{"body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → As an investor who must decide whether or not to buy shares, it is important to know how much of the company’s assets is financed by creditors and how much is financed by owners. The debt-to-equity ratio is used to assess the debt capacity of a business. It is computed as follows:\n\nThe ratio shows the relationship between the amount of capital provided by owners and the amount provided by creditors.  In general, a high ratio suggest that a company relies heavily on funds provided by creditors.\n\nDebt-to-equityRatio\n\n=\n\nTotal Liabilities\n\nShareholders’ equity", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 54}], "format": "plain", "slides": [54], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}, {"id": "c9-t35", "title": "Focus on Cash Flows", "body": "The cash received when a company issues long-term debt is reported as a cash inflow from financing activities.\n\nThe repayment of principal is reported as an outflow from financing activities.\n\nYou will see in Chapter 11 that interest paid may be reported in either the operating or the financing section of the statement of cash flows.\n\nInterest expense is reported on the statement of earnings and is a component of net earnings. As a result, most companies report interest payments as operating activities.", "blocks": [{"body": "The cash received when a company issues long-term debt is reported as a cash inflow from financing activities.\n\nThe repayment of principal is reported as an outflow from financing activities.\n\nYou will see in Chapter 11 that interest paid may be reported in either the operating or the financing section of the statement of cash flows.\n\nInterest expense is reported on the statement of earnings and is a component of net earnings. As a result, most companies report interest payments as operating activities.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 55}], "format": "plain", "slides": [55], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 9 slides"}], "deck": "sources/chapter-9.pptx"}, {"id": 10, "title": "Reporting and Interpreting Shareholders’ Equity", "topics": [{"id": "c10-t1", "title": "Understanding The Business", "body": "A critical advantage that corporations have over sole proprietorships and partnerships is that they can raise large amounts of capital because both large and small investors can easily participate, not in management but in their ownership.\n\nThis ease is related to three important factors:\n\nShares can be purchased in small amounts.\n\nOwnership interest can easily be transferred through the sale of shares on established markets, such as the Toronto Stock Exchange (TSX).\n\nStock ownership provides investors with limited liability.\n\nMany Canadians own shares, either directly or indirectly through a mutual fund or pension program.\n\nOwning shares offers the opportunity to earn higher returns than those possible on deposits to bank accounts or investments in corporate bonds.\n\nShare ownership also involves a higher risk of loss of the investment in shares because the company has no obligation either to repay investors the purchase price of the shares or pay dividends on common shares.\n\nThe proper balance between risk and the expected return on an investment depends on individual investors’ preferences.", "blocks": [{"body": "A critical advantage that corporations have over sole proprietorships and partnerships is that they can raise large amounts of capital because both large and small investors can easily participate, not in management but in their ownership.\n\nThis ease is related to three important factors:\n\nShares can be purchased in small amounts.\n\nOwnership interest can easily be transferred through the sale of shares on established markets, such as the Toronto Stock Exchange (TSX).\n\nStock ownership provides investors with limited liability.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 4}, {"body": "Many Canadians own shares, either directly or indirectly through a mutual fund or pension program.\n\nOwning shares offers the opportunity to earn higher returns than those possible on deposits to bank accounts or investments in corporate bonds.\n\nShare ownership also involves a higher risk of loss of the investment in shares because the company has no obligation either to repay investors the purchase price of the shares or pay dividends on common shares.\n\nThe proper balance between risk and the expected return on an investment depends on individual investors’ preferences.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 5}], "format": "plain", "slides": [4, 5], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t2", "title": "Shareholders’ Equity", "body": "For most corporations, shareholders’ equity includes two primary sources of equity capital:\n\nContributed capital states the amount invested by shareholders, and two distinct components:\n\nAmounts initially received from the sale of shares and\n\nContributed surplus that reflects contributions made by shareholders in excess of the amounts credited to share capital accounts.\n\nRetained earnings are generated by the profit-making activities of the company. This is the cumulative amount of net earnings since the corporation’s inception, less the cumulative amount of dividends declared by the corporation since its formation as a corporation.", "blocks": [{"body": "For most corporations, shareholders’ equity includes two primary sources of equity capital:\n\nContributed capital states the amount invested by shareholders, and two distinct components:\n\nAmounts initially received from the sale of shares and\n\nContributed surplus that reflects contributions made by shareholders in excess of the amounts credited to share capital accounts.\n\nRetained earnings are generated by the profit-making activities of the company. This is the cumulative amount of net earnings since the corporation’s inception, less the cumulative amount of dividends declared by the corporation since its formation as a corporation.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 6}], "format": "plain", "slides": [6], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t3", "title": "Ownership of a Corporation", "body": "The corporation is the only business form that the law recognizes as a separate entity.\n\nThe entity itself may own assets, incur liabilities, expand and contract in size, sue others, be sued, and managers may enter into contracts on behalf of the corporation, independent of the equity owners.\n\nThe law establishes the corporation as a distinct entity, existing separately from its equity owners and its managers.", "blocks": [{"body": "The corporation is the only business form that the law recognizes as a separate entity.\n\nThe entity itself may own assets, incur liabilities, expand and contract in size, sue others, be sued, and managers may enter into contracts on behalf of the corporation, independent of the equity owners.\n\nThe law establishes the corporation as a distinct entity, existing separately from its equity owners and its managers.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 7}], "format": "plain", "slides": [7], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t4", "title": "Benefits of Share Ownership", "body": "Owners of common shares receive the following benefits:\n\nA voice in management. They may vote at the shareholders’ meeting (or by proxy) on major issues concerning management of the corporation.\n\nDividends. They receive a proportionate share of the distribution of the corporation’s net earnings.\n\nResidual claim. They may receive a proportionate share of the distribution of remaining assets upon the liquidation of the company.", "blocks": [{"body": "Owners of common shares receive the following benefits:\n\nA voice in management. They may vote at the shareholders’ meeting (or by proxy) on major issues concerning management of the corporation.\n\nDividends. They receive a proportionate share of the distribution of the corporation’s net earnings.\n\nResidual claim. They may receive a proportionate share of the distribution of remaining assets upon the liquidation of the company.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 8}], "format": "plain", "slides": [8], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t5", "title": "Typical Organizational Structure of a Corporation", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch10-image2.png", "alt": "Typical Organizational Structure of a Corporation — original slide 9"}], "slide": 9}], "format": "plain", "slides": [9], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t6", "title": "Authorized, Issued, and Outstanding Shares", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["Definitions", " ", "Illustrations", "", " "], ["Authorized shares: The maximum number of shares that can be issued, as specified in the charter of the corporation.", " ", "The charter specifies “an unlimited number of common shares.”", "", ""], ["Issued shares: The total number of shares that the corporation has issued to date.", " ", "To date, XYZ Corporation has sold and issued 30,000 common shares.", "", ""], ["Un-issued shares: The number of authorized shares that have never been issued to date.", " ", "Authorized shares Issued shares Un-issued shares", "100,000    (30,000 )  70,000", ""], ["Treasury shares: Shares that have been issued to investors and then reacquired by the issuing corporation.", " ", "To date, XYZ Corporation has repurchased 1,000 previously issued shares.", "", ""], ["Outstanding number of shares: The number of shares currently owned by shareholders—that is, the number of shares authorized, minus the total number of un-issued shares, minus the number of treasury shares.", " ", "Authorized shares Treasury shares Un-issued shares Outstanding shares ", "100,000     (1,000)   (70,000 )   29,000", ""]]], "images": [], "slide": 10}], "format": "plain", "slides": [10], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t7", "title": "Key Ratio Analysis – Earnings per Share Ratio", "body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How profitable is a company?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The earnings per share (EPS) is a measure of the return on investment that is based on the number of shares outstanding instead of the dollar amounts reported on the statement of financial position. In simple situations, it is computed as follows:\n\nEarnings per share is probably the single most widely watched financial ratio.\n\nEarnings per share\n\n=\n\nNet earnings available to common shareholders\n\nAverage number of common shares outstanding", "blocks": [{"body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How profitable is a company?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The earnings per share (EPS) is a measure of the return on investment that is based on the number of shares outstanding instead of the dollar amounts reported on the statement of financial position. In simple situations, it is computed as follows:\n\nEarnings per share is probably the single most widely watched financial ratio.\n\nEarnings per share\n\n=\n\nNet earnings available to common shareholders\n\nAverage number of common shares outstanding", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 11}], "format": "plain", "slides": [11], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t8", "title": "Common Share Transactions", "body": "Corporations issue two types of shares: common shares and preferred shares.\n\nAll corporations issue common shares, while only some issue preferred shares, which grant preferences that the common shares do not have.\n\nCommon shares are the basic voting shares issued by a corporation. They are often called the residual equity because they rank after the preferred shares for dividend and asset distribution upon liquidation of the corporation.", "blocks": [{"body": "Corporations issue two types of shares: common shares and preferred shares.\n\nAll corporations issue common shares, while only some issue preferred shares, which grant preferences that the common shares do not have.\n\nCommon shares are the basic voting shares issued by a corporation. They are often called the residual equity because they rank after the preferred shares for dividend and asset distribution upon liquidation of the corporation.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 12}], "format": "plain", "slides": [12], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t9", "title": "No Par Value and Par Value Shares", "body": "Par value is the nominal value per share established in the charter of a corporation. It has no relationship to the price at which shares are actually sold to investors.\n\nThe Canada Business Corporations Act and most provincial corporation acts prohibit the issuance of par value shares. The few Canadian companies that still have par value shares outstanding issued them before the CBCA was amended in 1985.\n\nThe remainder of this chapter focuses on no par value shares, which do not have an amount per share specified in the corporate charter. In contrast, most U.S. corporations issue par value shares.", "blocks": [{"body": "Par value is the nominal value per share established in the charter of a corporation. It has no relationship to the price at which shares are actually sold to investors.\n\nThe Canada Business Corporations Act and most provincial corporation acts prohibit the issuance of par value shares. The few Canadian companies that still have par value shares outstanding issued them before the CBCA was amended in 1985.\n\nThe remainder of this chapter focuses on no par value shares, which do not have an amount per share specified in the corporate charter. In contrast, most U.S. corporations issue par value shares.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 13}], "format": "plain", "slides": [13], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t10", "title": "Initial Sale of Shares", "body": "Two names are applied to transactions involving the initial sale of a company’s shares to the public. An initial public offering, or IPO, involves the very first sale of a company’s shares to the public (i.e., when the company first “goes public”).\n\nMost sales of shares to the public are cash transactions.\n\nFor example; assume that BCE sold 100,000 common shares for $60 per share, as an initial sale of shares. The company records the following journal entry:", "blocks": [{"body": "Two names are applied to transactions involving the initial sale of a company’s shares to the public. An initial public offering, or IPO, involves the very first sale of a company’s shares to the public (i.e., when the company first “goes public”).\n\nMost sales of shares to the public are cash transactions.\n\nFor example; assume that BCE sold 100,000 common shares for $60 per share, as an initial sale of shares. The company records the following journal entry:", "tables": [[["Cash (A) (100,000 × $60)", "", "", "", "", "", "6,000,000", " ", ""], ["     Common shares (SE)", "", "", "", "", " ", " ", "6,000,000", ""], ["Assets", "", "=", "Liabilities", "", "+", "Shareholders’ Equity", "", ""], ["Cash", "+6,000,000", " ", " ", " ", " ", "Common shares", "", "+6,000,000"]]], "images": [], "slide": 14}], "format": "plain", "slides": [14], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t11", "title": "Sale of Shares in Secondary Markets", "body": "Subsequent to the initial sale, investors can sell shares to other investors without directly affecting the corporation.\n\nFor example, if investor Eric Mortisson sold 1,000 of BCE’s common shares to Jennifer Hussein, BCE does not record a journal entry on its books.\n\nEric received cash for the shares he sold, and Jennifer received shares for the cash she paid.\n\nBCE itself did not receive or pay anything because of this transaction.", "blocks": [{"body": "Subsequent to the initial sale, investors can sell shares to other investors without directly affecting the corporation.\n\nFor example, if investor Eric Mortisson sold 1,000 of BCE’s common shares to Jennifer Hussein, BCE does not record a journal entry on its books.\n\nEric received cash for the shares he sold, and Jennifer received shares for the cash she paid.\n\nBCE itself did not receive or pay anything because of this transaction.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 15}], "format": "plain", "slides": [15], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t12", "title": "Shares Issued for Non-cash Assets or Services", "body": "Many start-up companies have a shortage of cash. These companies often cannot afford to pay cash for needed assets and services, they sometimes issue shares to people who can supply these assets and services.\n\nMany executives, for instance, will join start-up companies for very low salaries because they also earn compensation in the form of common shares.\n\nWhen a company issues shares to acquire assets or services, the acquired items are recorded at the fair value of the assets or services received.\n\nIf the company cannot reliably estimate the fair value of the assets or services received, then the fair value of the shares would be used.", "blocks": [{"body": "Many start-up companies have a shortage of cash. These companies often cannot afford to pay cash for needed assets and services, they sometimes issue shares to people who can supply these assets and services.\n\nMany executives, for instance, will join start-up companies for very low salaries because they also earn compensation in the form of common shares.\n\nWhen a company issues shares to acquire assets or services, the acquired items are recorded at the fair value of the assets or services received.\n\nIf the company cannot reliably estimate the fair value of the assets or services received, then the fair value of the shares would be used.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 16}], "format": "plain", "slides": [16], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t13", "title": "Shares Issued for Employee Compensation", "body": "Stock options allow employees to purchase shares from the corporation at a predetermined, fixed price, which is generally at a fraction of the share price in the secondary market.\n\nExecutive and employee stock option plans have become an increasingly common form of non-cash compensation that should provide an incentive for executives and employees to act in the best interests of the corporation.\n\nCanadian companies must estimate and report compensation expense associated with stock options on the statement of earnings.", "blocks": [{"body": "Stock options allow employees to purchase shares from the corporation at a predetermined, fixed price, which is generally at a fraction of the share price in the secondary market.\n\nExecutive and employee stock option plans have become an increasingly common form of non-cash compensation that should provide an incentive for executives and employees to act in the best interests of the corporation.\n\nCanadian companies must estimate and report compensation expense associated with stock options on the statement of earnings.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 17}], "format": "plain", "slides": [17], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t14", "title": "Repurchase of Shares", "body": "Most Canadian companies cancel their shares when they buy them back from shareholders.\n\nWhen shares are cancelled, the appropriate share capital account is reduced by an amount that reflects the average issuance price per share.\n\nIf the purchase price is less than the average issuance price, the difference is credited to contributed surplus.\n\nFor example, if BCE purchases 50,000 common shares in the open market at $50 per share and the average price of the previously issued common shares is $54, the journal entry and the transaction effects will be as follows:\n\nRepurchases of shares at prices lower than the average issue price do not result in profit for the issuing company because they are capital transactions, not operating transactions.\n\nCompanies profit from buying and selling shares issued by other companies, but not their own shares.\n\nAssume, that BCE subsequently purchased 40,000 of its own common shares when the price per share was $60.\n\nIn this case, the excess of the purchase price over the average issuance price is $6 per share for a total of $240,000. This difference is debited first to contributed surplus to the extent of $200,000 (the account balance), and the remaining amount of $40,000 is debited to retained earnings.\n\nThe retained earnings account is reduced, because the excess of the purchase price over the contribution made previously by shareholders reflects the company’s profitable operations, which resulted in net earnings and caused an increase in share price.\n\nTherefor, the $40,000 is viewed as a distribution of accumulated net earnings, or retained earnings, to shareholders who sold their shares to the company. The journal entry and the transaction effects follow:", "blocks": [{"body": "Most Canadian companies cancel their shares when they buy them back from shareholders.\n\nWhen shares are cancelled, the appropriate share capital account is reduced by an amount that reflects the average issuance price per share.\n\nIf the purchase price is less than the average issuance price, the difference is credited to contributed surplus.\n\nFor example, if BCE purchases 50,000 common shares in the open market at $50 per share and the average price of the previously issued common shares is $54, the journal entry and the transaction effects will be as follows:", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 18}, {"body": "Repurchases of shares at prices lower than the average issue price do not result in profit for the issuing company because they are capital transactions, not operating transactions.\n\nCompanies profit from buying and selling shares issued by other companies, but not their own shares.", "tables": [[["Common shares (SE) (50,000 × $54)", "", "", "", "", "", "2,700,000", " "], ["     Cash (A)", "", "", "", "", "", " ", "2,500,000"], ["     Contributed surplus (SE)", "", "", "", "", " ", " ", "200,000"], ["Assets", "", "=", "Liabilities", "", "+", "Shareholders’ Equity", ""], ["Cash", "−2,500,000", " ", " ", " ", " ", "Common shares", "−2,700,000"], [" ", " ", " ", " ", " ", " ", "Contributed surplus", "+200,000"]]], "images": [], "slide": 19}, {"body": "Assume, that BCE subsequently purchased 40,000 of its own common shares when the price per share was $60.\n\nIn this case, the excess of the purchase price over the average issuance price is $6 per share for a total of $240,000. This difference is debited first to contributed surplus to the extent of $200,000 (the account balance), and the remaining amount of $40,000 is debited to retained earnings.\n\nThe retained earnings account is reduced, because the excess of the purchase price over the contribution made previously by shareholders reflects the company’s profitable operations, which resulted in net earnings and caused an increase in share price.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 20}, {"body": "Therefor, the $40,000 is viewed as a distribution of accumulated net earnings, or retained earnings, to shareholders who sold their shares to the company. The journal entry and the transaction effects follow:", "tables": [[["Common shares (SE) (40,000 × $54)", "2,160,000", " "], ["Contributed surplus (SE)", "200,000", " "], ["Retained earnings (SE)", "40,000", " "], ["     Cash (A)", " ", "2,400,000"]], [["Assets", "", "=", "Liabilities", "", "+", "Shareholders’ Equity", ""], ["Cash", "−2,400,000", " ", " ", " ", " ", "Common shares", "−2,160,000"], [" ", " ", " ", " ", " ", " ", "Contributed surplus", "−200,000"], [" ", " ", " ", " ", " ", " ", "Retained earnings", "−40,000"]]], "images": [], "slide": 21}], "format": "plain", "slides": [18, 19, 20, 21], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t15", "title": "Dividends on Common Shares", "body": "The declaration and payment of dividends involves three key dates:\n\nThe declaration date: is the date on which the board of directors officially approved the dividend. As soon as it makes the declaration, it creates a dividend liability.\n\nThe date of record: is the date on which the corporation prepares the list of current shareholders, based on its shareholder records. The dividend is payable only to those names listed on the record date. No journal entry is made on this date.\n\nThe payment date: is the date on which the cash is disbursed to pay the dividend liability.\n\n\n\nThere are two fundamental requirements for the payment of a cash dividend:\n\nSufficient retained earnings.\n\nSufficient cash.\n\nIn Canada, regardless of how profitable a company is, there is no legal obligation for companies to declare dividends.\n\nWhile Canadian companies are not legally obligated to declare dividends, once the board of directors declares a dividend (i.e., creates a dividend payable), there is a legal obligation to pay that dividend.", "blocks": [{"body": "The declaration and payment of dividends involves three key dates:\n\nThe declaration date: is the date on which the board of directors officially approved the dividend. As soon as it makes the declaration, it creates a dividend liability.\n\nThe date of record: is the date on which the corporation prepares the list of current shareholders, based on its shareholder records. The dividend is payable only to those names listed on the record date. No journal entry is made on this date.\n\nThe payment date: is the date on which the cash is disbursed to pay the dividend liability.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 22}, {"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch10-image3.png", "alt": "Dividends on Common Shares — original slide 23"}], "slide": 23}, {"body": "There are two fundamental requirements for the payment of a cash dividend:\n\nSufficient retained earnings.\n\nSufficient cash.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 24}, {"body": "In Canada, regardless of how profitable a company is, there is no legal obligation for companies to declare dividends.\n\nWhile Canadian companies are not legally obligated to declare dividends, once the board of directors declares a dividend (i.e., creates a dividend payable), there is a legal obligation to pay that dividend.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 25}], "format": "plain", "slides": [22, 23, 24, 25], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t16", "title": "Key Ratio Analysis – Dividend Yield Ratio", "body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → Investors in common shares expect to earn a return on their investment. A portion of this return comes in the form of dividends. How much do investors earn on their investment based on dividends?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The dividend yield ratio is a measure of the percentage return that shareholders earn from the dividends they receive. Potential investors often use this ratio to help select among alternative investment opportunities.\n\nThe\n\nDividend Yield Ratio\n\n=\n\nDividends per share\n\nMarket price per share", "blocks": [{"body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → Investors in common shares expect to earn a return on their investment. A portion of this return comes in the form of dividends. How much do investors earn on their investment based on dividends?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The dividend yield ratio is a measure of the percentage return that shareholders earn from the dividends they receive. Potential investors often use this ratio to help select among alternative investment opportunities.\n\nThe\n\nDividend Yield Ratio\n\n=\n\nDividends per share\n\nMarket price per share", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 26}], "format": "plain", "slides": [26], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t17", "title": "Stock Dividends", "body": "A stock dividend is a distribution of additional shares of a corporation’s own share capital to its shareholders on a pro rata basis, at no cost to the shareholder.\n\nThe term “pro rata basis” means that each shareholder receives additional shares equal to the percentage of shares already held.\n\nStock dividends usually consist of additional common shares issued to the holders of common shares.\n\nAn investor owning 10 percent of the outstanding shares receives 10 percent of any additional shares issued as a stock dividend.\n\nIf a company declared on July 25, 2023 a 10 percent stock dividend on common shares to be issued on August 25, 2023 to shareholders of record on August 10, 2023. The company had 100,000 shares outstanding, and the market price per share was $20 on the date of declaration.\n\nThe value of the stock dividend is $200,000 (100,000 x 10% x $20) and the declaration of the stock dividend requires the following journal entry on July 25, 2023:\n\nThe equity account “Stock dividend to be issued” is credited, instead of the “Common shares” account, until the shares are issued. The issuance and distribution of the additional shares on August 25, 2023, is recorded as follows:", "blocks": [{"body": "A stock dividend is a distribution of additional shares of a corporation’s own share capital to its shareholders on a pro rata basis, at no cost to the shareholder.\n\nThe term “pro rata basis” means that each shareholder receives additional shares equal to the percentage of shares already held.\n\nStock dividends usually consist of additional common shares issued to the holders of common shares.\n\nAn investor owning 10 percent of the outstanding shares receives 10 percent of any additional shares issued as a stock dividend.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 27}, {"body": "If a company declared on July 25, 2023 a 10 percent stock dividend on common shares to be issued on August 25, 2023 to shareholders of record on August 10, 2023. The company had 100,000 shares outstanding, and the market price per share was $20 on the date of declaration.\n\nThe value of the stock dividend is $200,000 (100,000 x 10% x $20) and the declaration of the stock dividend requires the following journal entry on July 25, 2023:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch10-image4.png", "alt": "Stock Dividends — original slide 28"}], "slide": 28}, {"body": "The equity account “Stock dividend to be issued” is credited, instead of the “Common shares” account, until the shares are issued. The issuance and distribution of the additional shares on August 25, 2023, is recorded as follows:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch10-image5.png", "alt": "Stock Dividends — original slide 29"}], "slide": 29}], "format": "plain", "slides": [27, 28, 29], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t18", "title": "Stock Splits", "body": "Stock splits are not dividends. They are similar to a stock dividend but are quite different in terms of their impact on the shareholders’ equity accounts.\n\nIn a stock split, the total number of authorized, issued, and outstanding shares is increased by a specified number, such as a two-for-one split.\n\nIn both a stock dividend and a stock split, the shareholder receives more shares but does not pay to acquire the additional shares.\n\nA stock dividend requires a journal entry; a stock split does not require one but is disclosed in the notes to the financial statements.\n\nThe effects of a 2-for-1 stock split may be summarized as follows:", "blocks": [{"body": "Stock splits are not dividends. They are similar to a stock dividend but are quite different in terms of their impact on the shareholders’ equity accounts.\n\nIn a stock split, the total number of authorized, issued, and outstanding shares is increased by a specified number, such as a two-for-one split.\n\nIn both a stock dividend and a stock split, the shareholder receives more shares but does not pay to acquire the additional shares.\n\nA stock dividend requires a journal entry; a stock split does not require one but is disclosed in the notes to the financial statements.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 30}, {"body": "The effects of a 2-for-1 stock split may be summarized as follows:", "tables": [[["Shareholders’ Equity", "Before the Stock Split", "After the Stock Split"], ["Contributed capital", "", ""], ["     Number of shares outstanding", "30,000", "60,000"], ["     Average issue price per share", "$10", "$5"], ["     Common shares", "300,000", "300,000"], ["Retained earnings", "650,000", "650,000"], ["Total shareholders’ equity", "$950,000", "$950,000"]]], "images": [], "slide": 31}], "format": "plain", "slides": [30, 31], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t19", "title": "Preferred Shares", "body": "In addition to common shares, some corporations issue preferred shares.\n\nPreferred shares differ from common shares in a number of ways. The most significant differences are:\n\nPreferred shares typically do not grant voting rights.\n\nPreferred shares typically have a fixed dividend rate.\n\nPreferred shares are less risky than common shares.", "blocks": [{"body": "In addition to common shares, some corporations issue preferred shares.\n\nPreferred shares differ from common shares in a number of ways. The most significant differences are:\n\nPreferred shares typically do not grant voting rights.\n\nPreferred shares typically have a fixed dividend rate.\n\nPreferred shares are less risky than common shares.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 32}], "format": "plain", "slides": [32], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t20", "title": "Special Features of Preferred Shares", "body": "Convertible preferred shares may be exchanged for a different series of preferred shares or for common shares of the corporation.\n\nRedeemable or Callable preferred shares may be repurchased by the corporation at a predetermined price.\n\nRetractable preferred shares may be redeemed at the option of the shareholder for the redemption price.", "blocks": [{"body": "Convertible preferred shares may be exchanged for a different series of preferred shares or for common shares of the corporation.\n\nRedeemable or Callable preferred shares may be repurchased by the corporation at a predetermined price.\n\nRetractable preferred shares may be redeemed at the option of the shareholder for the redemption price.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 33}], "format": "plain", "slides": [33], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t21", "title": "Dividends on Preferred Shares", "body": "Generally, preferred shareholders do not have the right to vote, nor do they share in increased earnings if the company becomes more profitable.\n\nTo compensate these investors, preferred shares offer a dividend preference. The two most common dividend preferences are:\n\nCurrent Dividend Preference:  The current preferred dividends must be paid before paying any dividends to common shares.\n\nCumulative Dividend Preference:  Any unpaid dividends from previous years (dividends in arrears) must be paid before common dividends are paid.\n\nIf the preferred shares are non-cumulative, any dividends not declared in previous years are lost permanently.", "blocks": [{"body": "Generally, preferred shareholders do not have the right to vote, nor do they share in increased earnings if the company becomes more profitable.\n\nTo compensate these investors, preferred shares offer a dividend preference. The two most common dividend preferences are:\n\nCurrent Dividend Preference:  The current preferred dividends must be paid before paying any dividends to common shares.\n\nCumulative Dividend Preference:  Any unpaid dividends from previous years (dividends in arrears) must be paid before common dividends are paid.\n\nIf the preferred shares are non-cumulative, any dividends not declared in previous years are lost permanently.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 34}], "format": "plain", "slides": [34], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t22", "title": "Dividends on Preferred Shares: Current Dividend Preference Only", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["Preferred shares outstanding, $1.20; 2,000 shares.", "", "", ""], ["Common shares outstanding; 5,000 shares.", "", "", ""], ["Allocation of dividends between preferred and common shares assuming current dividend preference only:", "", "", ""], ["Assumptions", "Total Dividends Paid", "$1.20 Preferred Shares (2,000 shares)", "Common Shares (5,000 shares)"], ["No. 1", "$  2,400", "  $2,400  ", "        0"], ["No. 2", "    3,000", "  2,400", "$     600   "], ["No. 3", "  18,000", "  2,400", "15,600 "]]], "images": [], "slide": 35}], "format": "plain", "slides": [35], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t23", "title": "Dividends on Preferred Shares: Cumulative Dividend Preference", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["Preferred shares outstanding, $1.20; 2,000 shares.", "", "", ""], ["Common shares outstanding; 5,000 shares.", "", "", ""], ["Dividends are in arrears for the two preceding years.", "", "", ""], ["Allocation of dividends between preferred and common shares, assuming cumulative preference:", "", "", ""], ["Assumptions (dividends in arrears, 2 years)", "Total Dividends Paid", "$1.20 Preferred Shares (2,000 shares)", "Common Shares (5,000 shares)"], ["No. 1", "$  2,400 ", "$ 2,400 ", "         0"], ["No. 2", "   7,200", "  7,200", "         0"], ["No. 3", "   8,000", "  7,200", "$     800  "], ["No. 4", " 30,000", "  7,200", "22,800"], ["", "", "", ""]]], "images": [], "slide": 36}], "format": "plain", "slides": [36], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t24", "title": "Measuring and Reporting Changes in Shareholders’ Equity", "body": "Two additional components of shareholders’ equity are:\n\nRetained Earnings\n\nAccumulated Other Comprehensive Income (Loss) (AOCI)", "blocks": [{"body": "Two additional components of shareholders’ equity are:\n\nRetained Earnings\n\nAccumulated Other Comprehensive Income (Loss) (AOCI)", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 37}], "format": "plain", "slides": [37], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t25", "title": "Retained Earnings", "body": "Retained earnings represent net earnings that has been earned less dividends that have been declared since the first day of the company’s operations.\n\nUnder rare circumstances, an adjustment to the beginning balance of retained earnings may result from either (1) a correction of a material accounting error that occurred in a prior period, or (2) a change in accounting policy.", "blocks": [{"body": "Retained earnings represent net earnings that has been earned less dividends that have been declared since the first day of the company’s operations.\n\nUnder rare circumstances, an adjustment to the beginning balance of retained earnings may result from either (1) a correction of a material accounting error that occurred in a prior period, or (2) a change in accounting policy.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 38}], "format": "plain", "slides": [38], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t26", "title": "Accumulated Other Comprehensive Income (Loss)", "body": "IFRS require Canadian publicly accountable enterprises to report accumulated other comprehensive income (loss).\n\nThis equity item reflects the financial effect of events that cause changes in shareholders’ equity, other than investments by shareholders or distributions to shareholders.\n\nSuch changes in equity result from unrealized gains or losses related to the valuation of specific assets and liabilities at fair value.\n\nThe unrealized gains and losses resulting from these changes are not reported on the statement of earnings because they have not been realized yet.\n\nMeasurement of the unrealized gains and losses related to these and similar items is fairly complex and is covered in intermediate and advanced accounting courses.", "blocks": [{"body": "IFRS require Canadian publicly accountable enterprises to report accumulated other comprehensive income (loss).\n\nThis equity item reflects the financial effect of events that cause changes in shareholders’ equity, other than investments by shareholders or distributions to shareholders.\n\nSuch changes in equity result from unrealized gains or losses related to the valuation of specific assets and liabilities at fair value.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 39}, {"body": "The unrealized gains and losses resulting from these changes are not reported on the statement of earnings because they have not been realized yet.\n\nMeasurement of the unrealized gains and losses related to these and similar items is fairly complex and is covered in intermediate and advanced accounting courses.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 40}], "format": "plain", "slides": [39, 40], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t27", "title": "Accounting and Reporting for Unincorporated Businesses", "body": "There are three forms of business organizations: corporations, sole proprietorships, and partnerships.\n\nA corporation is a legal entity, separate and distinct from its owners. It can enter into contracts in its own name, it can be sued, and it is taxed as a separate entity.\n\nA sole proprietorship is an unincorporated business owned by one individual. It is not necessary to file any legal papers to create a proprietorship.\n\nA partnership is an unincorporated business owned by two or more people. Again, it is not necessary to file legal papers to create a partnership, but it is certainly a good idea to have a lawyer draw up a contract among all the partners.\n\nNeither partnerships nor proprietorships are separate legal entities. As a result, owners may be directly sued and are individually taxed on the earnings of the business.\n\nThe fundamentals of accounting and reporting for unincorporated businesses are the same as for a corporation, except for shareholders’ equity.\n\nTypical account structures for the three forms of business organizations are outlined in the following slide.", "blocks": [{"body": "There are three forms of business organizations: corporations, sole proprietorships, and partnerships.\n\nA corporation is a legal entity, separate and distinct from its owners. It can enter into contracts in its own name, it can be sued, and it is taxed as a separate entity.\n\nA sole proprietorship is an unincorporated business owned by one individual. It is not necessary to file any legal papers to create a proprietorship.\n\nA partnership is an unincorporated business owned by two or more people. Again, it is not necessary to file legal papers to create a partnership, but it is certainly a good idea to have a lawyer draw up a contract among all the partners.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 41}, {"body": "Neither partnerships nor proprietorships are separate legal entities. As a result, owners may be directly sued and are individually taxed on the earnings of the business.\n\nThe fundamentals of accounting and reporting for unincorporated businesses are the same as for a corporation, except for shareholders’ equity.\n\nTypical account structures for the three forms of business organizations are outlined in the following slide.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 42}], "format": "plain", "slides": [41, 42], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t28", "title": "Comparative Account Structure among Types of Business Entities", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["Typical Account Structure", "", "", "", ""], ["Corporation (Shareholders’ Equity)", "", "Sole Proprietorship (Owner’s Equity)", "", "Partnership (Partners’ Equity)"], ["Share capital, contributed surplus", "", "Doe, capital", "", "Able, capital; Baker, capital"], ["Retained earnings", "", "Not used", "", "Not used"], ["Dividends paid", "", "Doe, drawings", "", "Able, drawings; Baker, drawings"], ["Revenues, expenses, gains, and losses", "", "Same", "", "Same"], ["Assets and liabilities", "", "Same", "", "Same"]]], "images": [], "slide": 43}], "format": "plain", "slides": [43], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t29", "title": "Appendix 10A: Accounting for Owners’ Equity for Sole Proprietorships and Partnerships", "body": "Proprietorships and partnerships are private enterprises that do not issue shares for sale to the public.\n\nThey normally report their financial information in accordance with the accounting standards for private enterprises.\n\nThese standards are less complicated than the IFRS that publicly accountable enterprises must use.\n\nThe use of accounting standards for private enterprises does not mean, however, that the standards are less rigorous than those used by publicly accountable enterprises.\n\nThis simplified set of standards makes the preparation and audit of financial statements affordable for smaller business entities.\n\nAll of the principles, such as economic substance over form and the excess of benefit over cost, guided the production of these standards.", "blocks": [{"body": "Proprietorships and partnerships are private enterprises that do not issue shares for sale to the public.\n\nThey normally report their financial information in accordance with the accounting standards for private enterprises.\n\nThese standards are less complicated than the IFRS that publicly accountable enterprises must use.\n\nThe use of accounting standards for private enterprises does not mean, however, that the standards are less rigorous than those used by publicly accountable enterprises.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 44}, {"body": "This simplified set of standards makes the preparation and audit of financial statements affordable for smaller business entities.\n\nAll of the principles, such as economic substance over form and the excess of benefit over cost, guided the production of these standards.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 45}], "format": "plain", "slides": [44, 45], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t30", "title": "Appendix 10A: Owners’ Equity for Sole Proprietorships", "body": "A sole proprietorship is an unincorporated business owned by one person.\n\nOnly two owner’s equity accounts are needed: a capital account for the proprietor (J. Doe, capital) and a drawings (or withdrawal) account for the proprietor (J. Doe, drawings).\n\nThe capital account of a sole proprietorship serves two purposes: to record investments by the owner and to accumulate periodic net earnings or loss.\n\nThe drawings account is used to record the owner’s withdrawals of cash or other assets from the business.\n\nThe drawings account is closed to the capital account at the end of each accounting period.\n\nThe capital account reflects the cumulative total of all investments by the owner, plus all earnings of the entity, less all withdrawals of resources from the entity by the owner.", "blocks": [{"body": "A sole proprietorship is an unincorporated business owned by one person.\n\nOnly two owner’s equity accounts are needed: a capital account for the proprietor (J. Doe, capital) and a drawings (or withdrawal) account for the proprietor (J. Doe, drawings).\n\nThe capital account of a sole proprietorship serves two purposes: to record investments by the owner and to accumulate periodic net earnings or loss.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 46}, {"body": "The drawings account is used to record the owner’s withdrawals of cash or other assets from the business.\n\nThe drawings account is closed to the capital account at the end of each accounting period.\n\nThe capital account reflects the cumulative total of all investments by the owner, plus all earnings of the entity, less all withdrawals of resources from the entity by the owner.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 47}], "format": "plain", "slides": [46, 47], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}, {"id": "c10-t31", "title": "Appendix 10A: Owners’ Equity for a Partnerships", "body": "A partnership is formed by two or more persons reaching mutual agreement about the terms of the partnership.\n\nThe law does not require an application for a charter as it does in the case of a corporation. Instead, the agreement between the partners constitutes a legally enforceable partnership contract.\n\nThe agreement should specify matters such as division of net earnings, management responsibilities, transfer or sale of partnership interests, disposition of assets upon liquidation, and procedures to be followed in case of the death of a partner.\n\nThe primary advantages of a partnership are:\n\nEase of formation,\n\nComplete control by the partners, and\n\nLack of income taxes on the business itself.\n\nThe primary disadvantage is the unlimited liability of each partner for the partnership’s liabilities.\n\nIf the partnership does not have sufficient assets to satisfy outstanding debt, its creditors can seize the partners’ personal assets.\n\nAccounting for partners’ equity follows the same pattern as illustrated earlier for a sole proprietorship, except that separate partner capital and drawings accounts must be established for each partner.\n\nInvestments by each partner are credited to the partner’s capital account, and withdrawals from the partnership by each partner are debited to the respective drawings accounts.\n\nThe net earnings for a partnership are divided between the partners in the profit ratio specified in the partnership agreement, and credited to each partner’s account.\n\nThe respective drawings accounts are closed to the partner capital accounts.\n\nAfter the closing process, each partner’s capital account reflects the cumulative total of all investments of that individual partner, plus the partner’s share of all partnership earnings, less all the partner’s withdrawals.\n\nThe financial statements of a partnership follow the same format as those for a corporation, except that:\n\nThe statement of earnings includes an additional section titled distribution of net earnings,\n\nThe partners’ equity section of the statement of financial position is detailed for each partner,\n\nA partnership has no income tax expense because partnerships do not pay income tax (each partner must report their share of the partnership net earnings on their individual tax return), and\n\nSalaries paid to partners are not recorded as expense but treated as a distribution of net earnings (withdrawals).", "blocks": [{"body": "A partnership is formed by two or more persons reaching mutual agreement about the terms of the partnership.\n\nThe law does not require an application for a charter as it does in the case of a corporation. Instead, the agreement between the partners constitutes a legally enforceable partnership contract.\n\nThe agreement should specify matters such as division of net earnings, management responsibilities, transfer or sale of partnership interests, disposition of assets upon liquidation, and procedures to be followed in case of the death of a partner.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 48}, {"body": "The primary advantages of a partnership are:\n\nEase of formation,\n\nComplete control by the partners, and\n\nLack of income taxes on the business itself.\n\nThe primary disadvantage is the unlimited liability of each partner for the partnership’s liabilities.\n\nIf the partnership does not have sufficient assets to satisfy outstanding debt, its creditors can seize the partners’ personal assets.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 49}, {"body": "Accounting for partners’ equity follows the same pattern as illustrated earlier for a sole proprietorship, except that separate partner capital and drawings accounts must be established for each partner.\n\nInvestments by each partner are credited to the partner’s capital account, and withdrawals from the partnership by each partner are debited to the respective drawings accounts.\n\nThe net earnings for a partnership are divided between the partners in the profit ratio specified in the partnership agreement, and credited to each partner’s account.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 50}, {"body": "The respective drawings accounts are closed to the partner capital accounts.\n\nAfter the closing process, each partner’s capital account reflects the cumulative total of all investments of that individual partner, plus the partner’s share of all partnership earnings, less all the partner’s withdrawals.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 51}, {"body": "The financial statements of a partnership follow the same format as those for a corporation, except that:\n\nThe statement of earnings includes an additional section titled distribution of net earnings,\n\nThe partners’ equity section of the statement of financial position is detailed for each partner,\n\nA partnership has no income tax expense because partnerships do not pay income tax (each partner must report their share of the partnership net earnings on their individual tax return), and\n\nSalaries paid to partners are not recorded as expense but treated as a distribution of net earnings (withdrawals).", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 52}], "format": "plain", "slides": [48, 49, 50, 51, 52], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 10 slides"}], "deck": "sources/chapter-10.pptx"}, {"id": 11, "title": "Statement of Cash Flows", "topics": [{"id": "c11-t1", "title": "Classifications of Cash Flows", "body": "The statement of cash flows explains how the cash balance at the beginning of the period changed to another cash balance at the end of the period.\n\nThe definition of cash for the purpose of the cash flow statement includes cash and cash equivalents.\n\nCash equivalents are short-term, highly liquid investments that are both:\n\nReadily convertible to known amounts of cash, and\n\nThree months or less to their maturity and subject to an insignificant risk of changes in value.\n\nThe statement of cash flows reports cash inflows and outflows based on three broad categories: (1) operating activities, (2) investing activities, and (3) financing activities.\n\nTogether, these three cash flow categories explain the change in cash from the beginning balance to the ending balance on the statement of financial position.", "blocks": [{"body": "The statement of cash flows explains how the cash balance at the beginning of the period changed to another cash balance at the end of the period.\n\nThe definition of cash for the purpose of the cash flow statement includes cash and cash equivalents.\n\nCash equivalents are short-term, highly liquid investments that are both:\n\nReadily convertible to known amounts of cash, and\n\nThree months or less to their maturity and subject to an insignificant risk of changes in value.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 4}, {"body": "The statement of cash flows reports cash inflows and outflows based on three broad categories: (1) operating activities, (2) investing activities, and (3) financing activities.\n\nTogether, these three cash flow categories explain the change in cash from the beginning balance to the ending balance on the statement of financial position.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 5}], "format": "plain", "slides": [4, 5], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t2", "title": "Cash flows from Operating Activities", "body": "Cash flows from operating activities are the cash inflows and cash outflows that directly relate to revenues and expenses reported on the statement of earnings.\n\nThere are two alternative approaches for presenting the operating activities section of the statement: the direct method and the indirect method.\n\nIt is important to remember that the direct and indirect methods are simply alternative ways to calculate the same amount. The net cash provided by (used in) operating activities is always the same, whether it is calculated by using the direct or the indirect method.", "blocks": [{"body": "Cash flows from operating activities are the cash inflows and cash outflows that directly relate to revenues and expenses reported on the statement of earnings.\n\nThere are two alternative approaches for presenting the operating activities section of the statement: the direct method and the indirect method.\n\nIt is important to remember that the direct and indirect methods are simply alternative ways to calculate the same amount. The net cash provided by (used in) operating activities is always the same, whether it is calculated by using the direct or the indirect method.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 6}], "format": "plain", "slides": [6], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t3", "title": "The Direct Method", "body": "The direct method reports the components of cash flows from operating activities listed as gross receipts and gross payments:\n\nThe difference between the inflows and outflows is called the net cash provided by (used in) operating activities.", "blocks": [{"body": "The direct method reports the components of cash flows from operating activities listed as gross receipts and gross payments:\n\nThe difference between the inflows and outflows is called the net cash provided by (used in) operating activities.", "tables": [[["Inflows", "Outflows"], ["Cash received from", "Cash paid for"], ["Customers", "Purchase of services (electricity, etc.) and goods for resale"], ["Dividends and interest on investments", "Salaries and wages Income taxes Interest on borrowings"]]], "images": [], "slide": 7}], "format": "plain", "slides": [7], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t4", "title": "The Indirect Method", "body": "The indirect method starts with net earnings for the period and then eliminates non-cash items to arrive at net cash inflow (outflow) from operating activities:", "blocks": [{"body": "The indirect method starts with net earnings for the period and then eliminates non-cash items to arrive at net cash inflow (outflow) from operating activities:", "tables": [[["       Net earnings"], ["+/− Adjustments for non-cash items"], ["       Net cash provided by (used in) operating activities"]]], "images": [], "slide": 8}], "format": "plain", "slides": [8], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t5", "title": "Cash Flows from Investing Activities", "body": "Cash flows from investing activities are cash inflows and outflows related to the purchase and sale of long-lived productive assets and investments in the securities of other companies. Typical cash flows from investing activities include the following:\n\nThe difference between these cash inflows and outflows is called net cash provided by (used in) investing activities.", "blocks": [{"body": "Cash flows from investing activities are cash inflows and outflows related to the purchase and sale of long-lived productive assets and investments in the securities of other companies. Typical cash flows from investing activities include the following:\n\nThe difference between these cash inflows and outflows is called net cash provided by (used in) investing activities.", "tables": [[["Inflows", "Outflows"], ["Cash received from", "Cash paid for"], ["Sale or disposal of property, plant, and equipment", "Purchase of property, plant, and equipment"], ["Sale or maturity of investments in securities", "Purchase of investments in securities"]]], "images": [], "slide": 9}], "format": "plain", "slides": [9], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t6", "title": "Cash Flows from Financing Activities", "body": "Cash flows from financing activities are cash inflows and outflows related to external sources (owners and creditors)  to finance the enterprise and its operations. Usual cash flows from financing activities include:\n\nThe difference between these cash inflows and outflows is called net cash provided by (used in) financing activities.", "blocks": [{"body": "Cash flows from financing activities are cash inflows and outflows related to external sources (owners and creditors)  to finance the enterprise and its operations. Usual cash flows from financing activities include:\n\nThe difference between these cash inflows and outflows is called net cash provided by (used in) financing activities.", "tables": [[["Inflows", "Outflows"], ["Cash received from", "Cash paid for"], ["Borrowing on notes, mortgages, bonds, etc. from creditors", "Repayment of principal to creditors"], ["Issuing shares to shareholders", "Interest on borrowings if it is classified as a financing activity"], ["", "Repurchasing shares from shareholders"], ["", "Dividends to shareholders"]]], "images": [], "slide": 10}], "format": "plain", "slides": [10], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t7", "title": "Net Increase (Decrease) in Cash", "body": "The combination of the net cash flows provided by (used in) operating, investing, and financing activities must equal the net increase (decrease) in cash for the reporting period.", "blocks": [{"body": "The combination of the net cash flows provided by (used in) operating, investing, and financing activities must equal the net increase (decrease) in cash for the reporting period.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 11}], "format": "plain", "slides": [11], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t8", "title": "Relationships to the Statement of Financial Position and the Statement of Earnings", "body": "To prepare the statement of cash flows, the following data is needed:\n\nComparative statements of financial position, which are used to compute the cash flows from all activities (operating, investing, and financing).\n\nA complete statement of earnings, which is used primarily to identify cash flows from operating activities.\n\nAdditional details concerning selected accounts because often the net change in an account balance during the year does not reveal the underlying nature of the cash flows.\n\nThe approach to preparing and understanding the statement of cash flows focuses on the changes in the accounts that are reported on the statement of financial position.\n\nIt relies on a simple algebraic manipulation of the accounting equation as follows:\n\nAssets = Liabilities + Shareholders’ equity\n\nCash + Non-cash assets = Liabilities + Shareholders’ equity\n\nCash = Liabilities + Shareholders’ equity – Non-cash assets\n\nTherefore...\n\nIn general, increases in cash are associated with decreases in non-cash asset accounts or increases in liability and shareholders’ equity accounts.\n\nIn contrast, cash decreases when non-cash assets increase, and when liabilities or shareholders’ equity decrease:", "blocks": [{"body": "To prepare the statement of cash flows, the following data is needed:\n\nComparative statements of financial position, which are used to compute the cash flows from all activities (operating, investing, and financing).\n\nA complete statement of earnings, which is used primarily to identify cash flows from operating activities.\n\nAdditional details concerning selected accounts because often the net change in an account balance during the year does not reveal the underlying nature of the cash flows.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 12}, {"body": "The approach to preparing and understanding the statement of cash flows focuses on the changes in the accounts that are reported on the statement of financial position.\n\nIt relies on a simple algebraic manipulation of the accounting equation as follows:\n\nAssets = Liabilities + Shareholders’ equity\n\nCash + Non-cash assets = Liabilities + Shareholders’ equity\n\nCash = Liabilities + Shareholders’ equity – Non-cash assets\n\nTherefore...", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch11-image2.jpeg", "alt": "Relationships to the Statement of Financial Position and the Statement of Earnings — original slide 13"}], "slide": 13}, {"body": "In general, increases in cash are associated with decreases in non-cash asset accounts or increases in liability and shareholders’ equity accounts.\n\nIn contrast, cash decreases when non-cash assets increase, and when liabilities or shareholders’ equity decrease:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch11-image3.jpeg", "alt": "Relationships to the Statement of Financial Position and the Statement of Earnings — original slide 14"}], "slide": 14}], "format": "plain", "slides": [12, 13, 14], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t9", "title": "Cash Transactions and Their Effects on Other Statement of Financial Position Accounts", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["Category", "Transaction", "Effect on Cash   ", "Other Account Affected"], ["Operating", "Collect accounts receivable", "+Cash", "−Accounts receivable (A)"], [" ", "Pay accounts payable", "−Cash", "−Accounts payable (L)"], [" ", "Pay rent in advance", "−Cash", "+Prepaid rent (A)"], [" ", "Pay interest", "−Cash", "−Retained earnings (SE)"], [" ", "Sell goods and services for cash", "+Cash", "+Retained earnings (SE)"], ["Investing", "Purchase equipment for cash", "−Cash", "+Equipment (A)"], [" ", "Sell investment securities for cash  ", "+Cash", "−Investments"], ["Financing", "Pay back debt to bank", "−Cash", "−Notes payable—Bank (L)"], [" ", "Issue shares for cash", "+Cash", "+Share capital (SE)"]]], "images": [], "slide": 15}], "format": "plain", "slides": [15], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t10", "title": "Preliminary Steps in Preparing the Statement of Cash Flows", "body": "We use the following preliminary steps to prepare the statement of cash flows:\n\nDetermine the change in each account on the statement of financial position by subtracting this year’s ending balance from this year’s beginning balance (which is last year’s ending balance).\n\nClassify each change as relating to operating (O), investing (I), or financing (F) activities.", "blocks": [{"body": "We use the following preliminary steps to prepare the statement of cash flows:\n\nDetermine the change in each account on the statement of financial position by subtracting this year’s ending balance from this year’s beginning balance (which is last year’s ending balance).\n\nClassify each change as relating to operating (O), investing (I), or financing (F) activities.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 16}], "format": "plain", "slides": [16], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t11", "title": "Reporting Cash Flows from Operating Activities – Indirect Method", "body": "The indirect method of reporting cash flows from operating activities starts with net earnings and adjusts for the non-cash components of revenues and expenses.\n\n\n\nCompleting the operating section by using the indirect method involves two steps:\n\nStep 1: Adjust net earnings for the effect of depreciation and amortization expenses and gains and losses on sale of investing assets such as property, plant, and equipment, and investments.\n\nStep 2: Adjust net earnings for changes in assets and liabilities marked as operating (O).\n\nWhen converting net earnings to cash flows from operating activities, apply the following general rules:\n\nAdd the change when an operating asset decreases or an operating liability increases.\n\nSubtract the change when an operating asset increases or an operating liability decreases.\n\nUnderstanding what makes these assets and liabilities increase and decrease is the key to understanding the logic of these additions and subtractions.", "blocks": [{"body": "The indirect method of reporting cash flows from operating activities starts with net earnings and adjusts for the non-cash components of revenues and expenses.", "tables": [[["Operating Activities"], ["Net earnings"], ["Add/subtract items not affecting cash:"], ["+ Depreciation and amortization expense"], ["− Gain on sale of investing activities"], ["+ Loss on sale of investing activities"], ["+ Decreases in operating assets"], ["− Increases in operating assets"], ["+ Increases in operating liabilities"], ["− Decreases in operating liabilities"], ["Net Cash Flows from Operating Activities"]]], "images": [], "slide": 17}, {"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch11-image4.png", "alt": "Reporting Cash Flows from Operating Activities – Indirect Method — original slide 18"}], "slide": 18}, {"body": "Completing the operating section by using the indirect method involves two steps:\n\nStep 1: Adjust net earnings for the effect of depreciation and amortization expenses and gains and losses on sale of investing assets such as property, plant, and equipment, and investments.\n\nStep 2: Adjust net earnings for changes in assets and liabilities marked as operating (O).", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 19}, {"body": "When converting net earnings to cash flows from operating activities, apply the following general rules:\n\nAdd the change when an operating asset decreases or an operating liability increases.\n\nSubtract the change when an operating asset increases or an operating liability decreases.\n\nUnderstanding what makes these assets and liabilities increase and decrease is the key to understanding the logic of these additions and subtractions.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 20}], "format": "plain", "slides": [17, 18, 19, 20], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t12", "title": "Change in Accounts Receivable", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch11-image5.png", "alt": "Change in Accounts Receivable — original slide 21"}], "slide": 21}], "format": "plain", "slides": [21], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t13", "title": "Change in Inventory", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch11-image6.png", "alt": "Change in Inventory — original slide 22"}], "slide": 22}], "format": "plain", "slides": [22], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t14", "title": "Change in Prepaid Expenses", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch11-image7.png", "alt": "Change in Prepaid Expenses — original slide 23"}], "slide": 23}], "format": "plain", "slides": [23], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t15", "title": "Change in Accounts Payable", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch11-image8.png", "alt": "Change in Accounts Payable — original slide 24"}], "slide": 24}], "format": "plain", "slides": [24], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t16", "title": "Change in Accrued Liabilities", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch11-image9.png", "alt": "Change in Accrued Liabilities — original slide 25"}], "slide": 25}], "format": "plain", "slides": [25], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t17", "title": "Change in Income Tax Payable", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch11-image10.png", "alt": "Change in Income Tax Payable — original slide 26"}], "slide": 26}], "format": "plain", "slides": [26], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t18", "title": "Summary of Additions and Subtractions", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["", "Additions and Subtractions to Reconcile Net Earnings to Cash Flows from Operating Activities", ""], ["Item", "When Item Increases", "When Item  Decreases"], ["Depreciation and amortization", "+", "NA"], ["Accounts receivable", "−", "+"], ["Inventories", "−", "+"], ["Prepaid expenses", "−", "+"], ["Accounts payable", "+", "−"], ["Accrued liabilities", "+", "−"], ["Income tax payable", "+", "−"]]], "images": [], "slide": 27}], "format": "plain", "slides": [27], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t19", "title": "Interpreting Cash Flows from Operating Activities", "body": "Investors will not invest in a company if they do not believe that cash generated from operations will be available to pay them dividends or expand the company.\n\nCreditors will not lend money if they do not believe that cash generated from operations will be available to pay back the loan.\n\nA common guideline followed by financial and credit analysts is to avoid firms with rising net earnings but falling cash flow from operations.", "blocks": [{"body": "Investors will not invest in a company if they do not believe that cash generated from operations will be available to pay them dividends or expand the company.\n\nCreditors will not lend money if they do not believe that cash generated from operations will be available to pay back the loan.\n\nA common guideline followed by financial and credit analysts is to avoid firms with rising net earnings but falling cash flow from operations.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 28}], "format": "plain", "slides": [28], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t20", "title": "Key Ratio Analysis – Quality of Earnings Ratio", "body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How much cash does each dollar of net earnings generate?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The quality of earnings ratio is useful in answering this question. It is computed as follows:\n\nIn general, this ratio measures the portion of net earnings that was generated in cash.  All other things equal, a higher quality of earnings ratio indicates greater ability to finance operating and other cash needs from operating cash inflows.\n\nCash Flow from Operating Activities\n\nNet Earnings\n\nQuality of Earnings Ratio\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → How much cash does each dollar of net earnings generate?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → The quality of earnings ratio is useful in answering this question. It is computed as follows:\n\nIn general, this ratio measures the portion of net earnings that was generated in cash.  All other things equal, a higher quality of earnings ratio indicates greater ability to finance operating and other cash needs from operating cash inflows.\n\nCash Flow from Operating Activities\n\nNet Earnings\n\nQuality of Earnings Ratio\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 29}], "format": "plain", "slides": [29], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t21", "title": "Reporting Cash Flows from Investing Activities", "body": "Preparing this section of the statement of cash flows requires analyzing the accounts related to property, plant, and equipment; intangible assets; and investments in the securities of other companies.\n\nNormally, the relevant statement of financial position accounts include short-term investments and long-lived asset accounts, such as long-term investments and property, plant, and equipment.\n\nThe following relationships are the ones that you will encounter most frequently:\n\nTypical investing activities include the following:\n\nCash expenditures that include the acquisition of tangible productive assets, such as buildings and equipment; intangible assets, such as trademarks and patents; and short- or long-term investments, such as shares or bonds issued by other companies, guaranteed investment certificates, or government securities with maturities of more than three months. Only purchases paid for with cash or cash equivalents are included.\n\nCash proceeds from the sale of productive assets; intangible assets; and maturity of sale of short- or long-term investments. This is the amount of cash that was received from the sale of assets, regardless of whether the assets were sold at a gain or a loss.", "blocks": [{"body": "Preparing this section of the statement of cash flows requires analyzing the accounts related to property, plant, and equipment; intangible assets; and investments in the securities of other companies.\n\nNormally, the relevant statement of financial position accounts include short-term investments and long-lived asset accounts, such as long-term investments and property, plant, and equipment.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 30}, {"body": "The following relationships are the ones that you will encounter most frequently:", "tables": [[["Related Statement of Financial Position Account(s)", "Investing Activity", "Cash Flow Effect"], ["Property, plant, and equipment and intangible assets (e.g., patents)", "Purchase of property, plant, and equipment  or intangible assets for cash", "Outflow"], ["", "Sale of property, plant, and equipment  or intangible assets for cash", "Inflow"], ["Short- or long-term investments in shares and bonds issued by other companies", "Purchase of investment securities for cash", "Outflow"], ["", "Sale (maturity) of investment securities for cash", "  Inflow"]]], "images": [], "slide": 31}, {"body": "Typical investing activities include the following:\n\nCash expenditures that include the acquisition of tangible productive assets, such as buildings and equipment; intangible assets, such as trademarks and patents; and short- or long-term investments, such as shares or bonds issued by other companies, guaranteed investment certificates, or government securities with maturities of more than three months. Only purchases paid for with cash or cash equivalents are included.\n\nCash proceeds from the sale of productive assets; intangible assets; and maturity of sale of short- or long-term investments. This is the amount of cash that was received from the sale of assets, regardless of whether the assets were sold at a gain or a loss.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 32}], "format": "plain", "slides": [30, 31, 32], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t22", "title": "Key Ratio Analysis – Capital Expenditures Ratio", "body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → To what degree was the company able to finance purchases of property, plant, and equipment with cash provided by operating activities?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → This ratio is calculated as:\n\nIn general, this ratio reflects the portion of purchases of property, plant, and equipment financed from operating activities without the need for outside debt or equity financing or the sale of long-lived assets or other investments. A high ratio indicates less need for outside financing for current and future expansion.\n\nCash Flow from Operating Activities\n\nCash Paid for Capital Expenditures\n\nCapital Expenditures Ratio\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "ANALYTICAL QUESTION → To what degree was the company able to finance purchases of property, plant, and equipment with cash provided by operating activities?\n\nRATIO AND COMPARISONS → This ratio is calculated as:\n\nIn general, this ratio reflects the portion of purchases of property, plant, and equipment financed from operating activities without the need for outside debt or equity financing or the sale of long-lived assets or other investments. A high ratio indicates less need for outside financing for current and future expansion.\n\nCash Flow from Operating Activities\n\nCash Paid for Capital Expenditures\n\nCapital Expenditures Ratio\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 33}], "format": "plain", "slides": [33], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t23", "title": "Free Cash Flow", "body": "Managers and analysts also often calculate free cash flow as a measure of the firm’s ability to pursue long-term investment opportunities. It is normally calculated as follows:\n\nCash Flow from Operating Activities\n\n– Dividends – Capital Expenditures\n\nFree\n\nCash Flow\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "Managers and analysts also often calculate free cash flow as a measure of the firm’s ability to pursue long-term investment opportunities. It is normally calculated as follows:\n\nCash Flow from Operating Activities\n\n– Dividends – Capital Expenditures\n\nFree\n\nCash Flow\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 34}], "format": "plain", "slides": [34], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t24", "title": "Reporting Cash Flows from Financing Activities", "body": "Financing activities are associated with generating capital from creditors and owners.\n\nThis section reflects changes in two current liabilities, notes payable to financial institutions (often called short-term debt) and current portion of long-term debt, as well as changes in non-current liabilities and shareholders’ equity accounts.\n\nThese statement of financial position accounts relate to the issuance and retirement of debt, repurchase of shares, and payment of dividends.", "blocks": [{"body": "Financing activities are associated with generating capital from creditors and owners.\n\nThis section reflects changes in two current liabilities, notes payable to financial institutions (often called short-term debt) and current portion of long-term debt, as well as changes in non-current liabilities and shareholders’ equity accounts.\n\nThese statement of financial position accounts relate to the issuance and retirement of debt, repurchase of shares, and payment of dividends.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 35}], "format": "plain", "slides": [35], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t25", "title": "Preparing, Reporting, and Explaining Cash Flows from Financing Activities", "body": "The following relationships are the ones that you will encounter most frequently:\n\nTypical financing activities include the following:\n\nProceeds from issuance of short- and long-term debt.\n\nPrincipal payments on short- and long-term debt.\n\nProceeds from the issuance of shares.\n\nPurchase of shares for retirement.\n\nInterest and dividends.", "blocks": [{"body": "The following relationships are the ones that you will encounter most frequently:", "tables": [[["Related Statement of Financial Position Account(s)", " Financing Activity", "Cash Flow Effect"], ["Short-term debt", "Borrowing cash from bank or other financial institution", "Inflow"], ["", "Repayment of loan principal", "Outflow"], ["Long-term debt", "Long-term borrowings for cash", "Inflow"], ["", "Repayment of principal on long-term borrowings", "Outflow"], ["Share capital", "Issuance of shares for cash", "Inflow"], ["", "Repurchase (retirement) of shares for cash", "Outflow"], ["Retained earnings", "Payment of cash dividends", "Outflow"]]], "images": [], "slide": 36}, {"body": "Typical financing activities include the following:\n\nProceeds from issuance of short- and long-term debt.\n\nPrincipal payments on short- and long-term debt.\n\nProceeds from the issuance of shares.\n\nPurchase of shares for retirement.\n\nInterest and dividends.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 37}], "format": "plain", "slides": [36, 37], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t26", "title": "Structure of the Statement of Cash Flows (Indirect Method)", "body": "\n\n", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["Statement of Cash Flows (Indirect Method)"], ["Operating Activities:"], ["Net earnings"], ["+ Depreciation and amortization expense"], ["− Gain on sale of long-lived asset"], ["+ Loss on sale of long-lived asset"], ["+ Decreases in operating assets"], ["+ Increases in operating liabilities"], ["− Increases in operating assets"], ["− Decreases in operating liabilities"], ["Net Cash Flow from Operating Activities"]]], "images": [], "slide": 38}, {"body": "", "tables": [[["Investing Activities:"], ["− Purchase of property, plant, and equipment or intangible assets"], ["+ Sale of property, plant, and equipment or intangible assets"], ["− Purchase of investment securities"], ["+ Sale (maturity) of investment securities"], ["Net Cash Flow from Investing Activities"], ["Financing Activities:"], ["+ Borrowing from bank or other financial institution"], ["− Repayment of loan principal"], ["+ Issuance of long-term debt for cash"], ["− Repayment of principal on long-term debt"], ["+ Issuance of shares"], ["− Repurchase (retirement) of shares"], ["− Payment of (cash) dividends"], ["Net Cash Flow from Financing Activities"], ["Net Increase or Decrease in Cash and Cash Equivalents"], ["Cash and cash equivalents at beginning of period"], ["Cash and cash equivalents at end of period"]]], "images": [], "slide": 39}], "format": "plain", "slides": [38, 39], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t27", "title": "Non-cash Investing and Financing Activities", "body": "Certain transactions are important investing and financing activities but have no cash flow effects.\n\nThese are called non-cash investing and financing activities; they are reported as a supplement to the statement of cash flows in narrative or schedule form.", "blocks": [{"body": "Certain transactions are important investing and financing activities but have no cash flow effects.\n\nThese are called non-cash investing and financing activities; they are reported as a supplement to the statement of cash flows in narrative or schedule form.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 40}], "format": "plain", "slides": [40], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t28", "title": "Supplemental Cash Flow Information", "body": "Companies that use the indirect method of presenting cash flows from operations must also disclose the amounts of interest and dividends received and paid during the period, as well as cash paid for income taxes.\n\nThese are normally listed in the operating activities section of the statement or in the notes.", "blocks": [{"body": "Companies that use the indirect method of presenting cash flows from operations must also disclose the amounts of interest and dividends received and paid during the period, as well as cash paid for income taxes.\n\nThese are normally listed in the operating activities section of the statement or in the notes.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 41}], "format": "plain", "slides": [41], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t29", "title": "Appendix 11A: Adjustment for Gains and Losses on the Sale of Long-Lived Assets — Indirect Method", "body": "Transactions that cause gains and losses should be classified on the statement of cash flows as operating, investing, or financing activities, depending on their dominant characteristics.  For example, if the sale of equipment produced a gain, it would be classified as an investing activity.\n\nGains → Gains must be subtracted from profit to avoid double counting the gain.\n\nLosses → Losses must be added to profit to avoid double counting the loss.", "blocks": [{"body": "Transactions that cause gains and losses should be classified on the statement of cash flows as operating, investing, or financing activities, depending on their dominant characteristics.  For example, if the sale of equipment produced a gain, it would be classified as an investing activity.\n\nGains → Gains must be subtracted from profit to avoid double counting the gain.\n\nLosses → Losses must be added to profit to avoid double counting the loss.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 42}], "format": "plain", "slides": [42], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t30", "title": "Appendix 11B: Reporting Cash Flows from Operating Activities – Direct Method", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch11-image11.png", "alt": "Appendix 11B: Reporting Cash Flows from Operating Activities – Direct Method — original slide 43"}], "slide": 43}], "format": "plain", "slides": [43], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t31", "title": "Converting Revenue and Expense Items from an Accrual Basis to a Cash Basis", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["Statement of Earnings Account", "+/− Change in Statement of Financial Position Account(s)", "= Operating Cash Flow"], ["Sales revenue", "+ Beginning accounts receivable − Ending accounts receivable", "= Collections from customers"], ["Cost of sales", "− Beginning inventory + Ending inventory + Beginning accounts payable − Ending accounts payable", "= Payments to suppliers of     inventory"], ["Selling, general, and administrative expenses", "− Beginning prepaid expenses + Ending prepaid expenses + Beginning accrued liabilities − Ending accrued liabilities", "= Payments to suppliers of services    (e.g., rent, utilities, wages)"], ["Interest expense", "+ Beginning interest payable − Ending interest payable", "= Payments for interest"], ["Income tax expense", "+ Beginning income taxes payable − Ending income taxes payable", "= Payments for income taxes"]]], "images": [], "slide": 44}], "format": "plain", "slides": [44], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t32", "title": "Appendix 5C: T-Account Approach – Indirect Method", "body": "The following three steps are used to prepare the statement of cash flows:\n\nDetermine the change in each statement of financial position account. From this year’s ending balance, subtract this year’s beginning balance\n\nIdentify the cash flow category or categories to which each account relates. Remember that changes in the balances of some accounts result from two types of activities.\n\nCreate schedules that summarize cash flows from operating, investing, and financing activities.\n\nInstead of creating separate schedules for each section of the statement, many accountants prefer to prepare a single large T-account to represent the changes that have taken place in cash, subdivided into the three sections of the statement of cash flows.", "blocks": [{"body": "The following three steps are used to prepare the statement of cash flows:\n\nDetermine the change in each statement of financial position account. From this year’s ending balance, subtract this year’s beginning balance\n\nIdentify the cash flow category or categories to which each account relates. Remember that changes in the balances of some accounts result from two types of activities.\n\nCreate schedules that summarize cash flows from operating, investing, and financing activities.\n\nInstead of creating separate schedules for each section of the statement, many accountants prefer to prepare a single large T-account to represent the changes that have taken place in cash, subdivided into the three sections of the statement of cash flows.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 45}], "format": "plain", "slides": [45], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}, {"id": "c11-t33", "title": "Appendix 5D: Spreadsheet Approach – Statement of Cash Flows, Indirect Method", "body": "The spreadsheet approach offers a systematic way to keep track of data.  A spreadsheet is organized as follows:\n\nFour columns to record dollar amounts are established (beginning balance, debit changes, credit changes, and ending balance).\n\nOn the left of the top half of the spreadsheet, each account name from the statement of financial position is entered.\n\nOn the left of the bottom half of the spreadsheet, the name of each item that will be reported on the statement of cash flows is entered.\n\nChanges in the various statement of financial position accounts are analyzed in terms of debits and credits in the top half of the spreadsheet, with the offsetting debits and credits being recorded in the bottom half of the spreadsheet in terms of their impact on cash flows. Each change in the non-cash statement of financial position accounts explains part of the change in the cash account.", "blocks": [{"body": "The spreadsheet approach offers a systematic way to keep track of data.  A spreadsheet is organized as follows:\n\nFour columns to record dollar amounts are established (beginning balance, debit changes, credit changes, and ending balance).\n\nOn the left of the top half of the spreadsheet, each account name from the statement of financial position is entered.\n\nOn the left of the bottom half of the spreadsheet, the name of each item that will be reported on the statement of cash flows is entered.\n\nChanges in the various statement of financial position accounts are analyzed in terms of debits and credits in the top half of the spreadsheet, with the offsetting debits and credits being recorded in the bottom half of the spreadsheet in terms of their impact on cash flows. Each change in the non-cash statement of financial position accounts explains part of the change in the cash account.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 46}], "format": "plain", "slides": [46], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 11 slides"}], "deck": "sources/chapter-11.pptx"}, {"id": 12, "title": "Communicating Accounting Information and Analyzing Financial Statements", "topics": [{"id": "c12-t1", "title": "Ensuring the Integrity of Financial Information", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch12-image2.png", "alt": "Ensuring the Integrity of Financial Information — original slide 4"}], "slide": 4}], "format": "plain", "slides": [4], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t2", "title": "Regulators", "body": "The financial information reported by Canadian companies is subject to strict regulations and standards issued by government regulators and private standard-setting organizations.\n\nCanadian publicly traded corporations must comply with provincial securities regulations that are coordinated by the Canadian Securities Administrators (CSA).\n\nThe CSA is a forum for the 13 securities regulators of Canada’s provinces and territories that was established to harmonize regulation of the Canadian capital markets where financial securities are offered and traded.\n\nSecurities regulators work closely with the Accounting Standards Board (AcSB), which is responsible for establishing standards of accounting and reporting by Canadian companies.\n\nExternal auditors ensure that companies prepare their financial reports in accordance with these standards, and their audit work is guided by International Standards on Auditing, which have been adopted by the Canadian Auditing and Assurance Standards Board (AASB) as Canadian Auditing Standards.\n\nStock exchanges also provide an essential quality assurance service to listed companies by undertaking ongoing surveillance of their reporting and trading activities.\n\nWhen they suspect non-compliance with accounting standards, the stock exchanges undertake independent investigations and share information with law enforcement agencies such as securities commissions, the Canada Revenue Agency (CRA), which collects income taxes from corporations, and the Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP).", "blocks": [{"body": "The financial information reported by Canadian companies is subject to strict regulations and standards issued by government regulators and private standard-setting organizations.\n\nCanadian publicly traded corporations must comply with provincial securities regulations that are coordinated by the Canadian Securities Administrators (CSA).\n\nThe CSA is a forum for the 13 securities regulators of Canada’s provinces and territories that was established to harmonize regulation of the Canadian capital markets where financial securities are offered and traded.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 5}, {"body": "Securities regulators work closely with the Accounting Standards Board (AcSB), which is responsible for establishing standards of accounting and reporting by Canadian companies.\n\nExternal auditors ensure that companies prepare their financial reports in accordance with these standards, and their audit work is guided by International Standards on Auditing, which have been adopted by the Canadian Auditing and Assurance Standards Board (AASB) as Canadian Auditing Standards.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 6}, {"body": "Stock exchanges also provide an essential quality assurance service to listed companies by undertaking ongoing surveillance of their reporting and trading activities.\n\nWhen they suspect non-compliance with accounting standards, the stock exchanges undertake independent investigations and share information with law enforcement agencies such as securities commissions, the Canada Revenue Agency (CRA), which collects income taxes from corporations, and the Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP).", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 7}], "format": "plain", "slides": [5, 6, 7], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t3", "title": "Managers", "body": "The primary responsibility is for the information in the financial statements and disclosures lies with both:\n\nChief Executive Officer (CEO): highest officer of the company.\n\nChief Financial Officer (CFO): highest officer associated with the financial and accounting side of the business.\n\nAccounting Staff:  prepare the details of the reports and bear professional responsibility for the accuracy of the information.\n\nAll Canadian publicly accountable enterprises, the CEO and CFO must personally certify the following:\n\nEach report filed with the regulatory authorities does not contain any untrue statement of a material fact or omit stating a material fact, and fairly presents, in all material respects, the financial condition, financial performance, and cash flows of the company.\n\nThere are no significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in the design or operation of internal controls over financial reporting that are reasonably likely to adversely affect the company’s ability to record, process, summarize, and report financial information.\n\nThey have disclosed to the auditors and the audit committee of the board any weaknesses in internal controls or any fraud involving management or other employees who have a significant role in financial reporting.", "blocks": [{"body": "The primary responsibility is for the information in the financial statements and disclosures lies with both:\n\nChief Executive Officer (CEO): highest officer of the company.\n\nChief Financial Officer (CFO): highest officer associated with the financial and accounting side of the business.\n\nAccounting Staff:  prepare the details of the reports and bear professional responsibility for the accuracy of the information.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 8}, {"body": "All Canadian publicly accountable enterprises, the CEO and CFO must personally certify the following:\n\nEach report filed with the regulatory authorities does not contain any untrue statement of a material fact or omit stating a material fact, and fairly presents, in all material respects, the financial condition, financial performance, and cash flows of the company.\n\nThere are no significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in the design or operation of internal controls over financial reporting that are reasonably likely to adversely affect the company’s ability to record, process, summarize, and report financial information.\n\nThey have disclosed to the auditors and the audit committee of the board any weaknesses in internal controls or any fraud involving management or other employees who have a significant role in financial reporting.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 9}], "format": "plain", "slides": [8, 9], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t4", "title": "Board of Directors (Audit Committee)", "body": "The board of directors, elected by the shareholders to represent their interests, is responsible for maintaining the integrity of the company’s strategy, internal control system, and presentation of financial reports.\n\nThe audit committee of the board, which must be composed of independent, financially literate directors, oversees management in carrying out its responsibilities for financial reporting systems and internal control.\n\nThe audit committee reports its findings to the board of directors for them to consider and approve the annual financial statements to be issued to shareholders.\n\nRecent changes to securities regulations have increased the burden of management’s responsibility for accurate financial disclosure.\n\nIt is the responsibility of management and those charged with governance, including the board of directors, to develop and oversee systems to prevent and detect fraud.\n\nIf any company listed on a stock exchange is found guilty of knowingly violating any disclosure regulation, not only the company but also individual senior officers, such as the CEO and CFO, and members of its board of directors can be sued.\n\nIf experts, such as accountants or financial analysts, who relied on the company’s financial reports also issued disclosure that misrepresented the company, they too may be individually sued by users who seek to recover some or all of their financial losses, which may have resulted from reliance on misleading information.", "blocks": [{"body": "The board of directors, elected by the shareholders to represent their interests, is responsible for maintaining the integrity of the company’s strategy, internal control system, and presentation of financial reports.\n\nThe audit committee of the board, which must be composed of independent, financially literate directors, oversees management in carrying out its responsibilities for financial reporting systems and internal control.\n\nThe audit committee reports its findings to the board of directors for them to consider and approve the annual financial statements to be issued to shareholders.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 10}, {"body": "Recent changes to securities regulations have increased the burden of management’s responsibility for accurate financial disclosure.\n\nIt is the responsibility of management and those charged with governance, including the board of directors, to develop and oversee systems to prevent and detect fraud.\n\nIf any company listed on a stock exchange is found guilty of knowingly violating any disclosure regulation, not only the company but also individual senior officers, such as the CEO and CFO, and members of its board of directors can be sued.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 11}, {"body": "If experts, such as accountants or financial analysts, who relied on the company’s financial reports also issued disclosure that misrepresented the company, they too may be individually sued by users who seek to recover some or all of their financial losses, which may have resulted from reliance on misleading information.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 12}], "format": "plain", "slides": [10, 11, 12], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t5", "title": "Auditors", "body": "The provincial securities commissions require that publicly traded companies have their statements and their control systems over the financial reporting process audited by professional independent accountants in accordance with International Standards on Auditing (ISA).\n\nIn Canada, the Canadian Auditing Standards (CAS) converge with the ISA. Many privately owned companies also have their statements audited.\n\nAn unqualified (clean) audit opinion is the auditors’ declaration that the financial statements are fair presentations in all material respects in conformity with IFRS.\n\nBy signing an unqualified audit opinion, the audit firm assumes full responsibility for conducting the audit in compliance with all authoritative accounting standards and guidance, as well as the jurisdiction’s laws and regulations.\n\nThis opinion, which adds credibility to the statements, is also often required by agreements with lenders and private investors.", "blocks": [{"body": "The provincial securities commissions require that publicly traded companies have their statements and their control systems over the financial reporting process audited by professional independent accountants in accordance with International Standards on Auditing (ISA).\n\nIn Canada, the Canadian Auditing Standards (CAS) converge with the ISA. Many privately owned companies also have their statements audited.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 13}, {"body": "An unqualified (clean) audit opinion is the auditors’ declaration that the financial statements are fair presentations in all material respects in conformity with IFRS.\n\nBy signing an unqualified audit opinion, the audit firm assumes full responsibility for conducting the audit in compliance with all authoritative accounting standards and guidance, as well as the jurisdiction’s laws and regulations.\n\nThis opinion, which adds credibility to the statements, is also often required by agreements with lenders and private investors.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 14}], "format": "plain", "slides": [13, 14], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t6", "title": "How Data Analytics is Affecting What Auditors Do", "body": "Modern data analytics is changing the world of auditing.\n\nThe availability of more data from clients’ enterprise-wide accounting systems, has allowed access now to entire populations of transactions instead of smaller statistical samples as in the past.\n\nThis eases identification of trends, patterns, and outliers, which become the focus for further investigation.\n\nEven for a large multinational client, auditors can now examine the whole population of manual entries to determine particular locations and types of transactions generating unexpected numbers of entries, which the auditor then examines further.\n\nData analytics also makes it easier to correlate information from various parts of the client’s accounting systems such as sales invoices, shipping records, and cash receipts to identify areas where undetected material misstatements may exist (audit risks).\n\nThe ability to understand client accounting systems and to apply predictive models and data visualization tools to data extracted from client systems is fast becoming a key part of the auditor’s skill set.\n\nThe availability of data analytics also decreases the amount of repetitive manual work in the audit and increases the value of audit services to clients and securities markets.", "blocks": [{"body": "Modern data analytics is changing the world of auditing.\n\nThe availability of more data from clients’ enterprise-wide accounting systems, has allowed access now to entire populations of transactions instead of smaller statistical samples as in the past.\n\nThis eases identification of trends, patterns, and outliers, which become the focus for further investigation.\n\nEven for a large multinational client, auditors can now examine the whole population of manual entries to determine particular locations and types of transactions generating unexpected numbers of entries, which the auditor then examines further.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 15}, {"body": "Data analytics also makes it easier to correlate information from various parts of the client’s accounting systems such as sales invoices, shipping records, and cash receipts to identify areas where undetected material misstatements may exist (audit risks).\n\nThe ability to understand client accounting systems and to apply predictive models and data visualization tools to data extracted from client systems is fast becoming a key part of the auditor’s skill set.\n\nThe availability of data analytics also decreases the amount of repetitive manual work in the audit and increases the value of audit services to clients and securities markets.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 16}], "format": "plain", "slides": [15, 16], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t7", "title": "Information Intermediaries: Analysts and Information Services", "body": "", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch12-image3.png", "alt": "Information Intermediaries: Analysts and Information Services — original slide 17"}], "slide": 17}], "format": "plain", "slides": [17], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t8", "title": "Financial Analysts", "body": "Financial analysts receive accounting reports and other information about the company from electronic information services. They also gather information through conversations with company executives and visits to company facilities and competitors. The results of their analyses are combined into analysts’ reports.\n\nAnalysts’ reports normally include forecasts of share price and future quarterly and annual earnings per share; a buy, sell, or hold recommendation for the company shares; and explanations for these judgments.\n\nIn making these earnings forecasts or predictions of earnings for future accounting periods, the analysts rely heavily on their knowledge of how the accounting system translates business events into the numbers on a company’s financial statements.\n\nIndividual analysts often specialize in particular industries (such as sporting goods or energy companies). Analysts are regularly evaluated based on the accuracy of their forecasts, as well as the profitability of their stock picks.", "blocks": [{"body": "Financial analysts receive accounting reports and other information about the company from electronic information services. They also gather information through conversations with company executives and visits to company facilities and competitors. The results of their analyses are combined into analysts’ reports.\n\nAnalysts’ reports normally include forecasts of share price and future quarterly and annual earnings per share; a buy, sell, or hold recommendation for the company shares; and explanations for these judgments.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 18}, {"body": "In making these earnings forecasts or predictions of earnings for future accounting periods, the analysts rely heavily on their knowledge of how the accounting system translates business events into the numbers on a company’s financial statements.\n\nIndividual analysts often specialize in particular industries (such as sporting goods or energy companies). Analysts are regularly evaluated based on the accuracy of their forecasts, as well as the profitability of their stock picks.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 19}], "format": "plain", "slides": [18, 19], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t9", "title": "Information Services", "body": "Canadian companies file financial statements and other securities-related forms electronically with SEDAR (System for Electronic Document Analysis and Retrieval), which is the official site for the filing of documents by public companies as required by securities laws in Canada.\n\nSEDAR is currently a free service available on the Internet at www.sedar.com.\n\nMany companies also provide access to their financial statements and other information over the Internet.", "blocks": [{"body": "Canadian companies file financial statements and other securities-related forms electronically with SEDAR (System for Electronic Document Analysis and Retrieval), which is the official site for the filing of documents by public companies as required by securities laws in Canada.\n\nSEDAR is currently a free service available on the Internet at www.sedar.com.\n\nMany companies also provide access to their financial statements and other information over the Internet.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 20}], "format": "plain", "slides": [20], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t10", "title": "Users: Institutional and Private Investors, Creditors, and Other", "body": "Institutional Investors:\n\nIncludes private pension funds, public pension funds, mutual funds, endowments and trust funds, and charitable foundations.\n\nPrivate Investors:\n\nIndividuals who purchase shares in companies.\n\nLenders (creditors):\n\nSuppliers, banks, commercial credit companies, and other financial institutions that lend money to companies.", "blocks": [{"body": "Institutional Investors:\n\nIncludes private pension funds, public pension funds, mutual funds, endowments and trust funds, and charitable foundations.\n\nPrivate Investors:\n\nIndividuals who purchase shares in companies.\n\nLenders (creditors):\n\nSuppliers, banks, commercial credit companies, and other financial institutions that lend money to companies.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 21}], "format": "plain", "slides": [21], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t11", "title": "ESG – Investor Demand for Relevant Information", "body": "In 2021, the CPA profession responded to intensifying demand by global institutional investors for relevant, faithfully representative, standardized, and publicly available ESG measures.\n\nThe IASB established the International Sustainability Standards Board (ISSB).\n\nIFRS published drafts of general ESG and specific environmental standards, with the expectation that final standards will follow in due course.\n\nCPA Canada can choose to adopt the standards as they are or adapt them to better reflect unique factors in Canada’s business and economic contexts.", "blocks": [{"body": "In 2021, the CPA profession responded to intensifying demand by global institutional investors for relevant, faithfully representative, standardized, and publicly available ESG measures.\n\nThe IASB established the International Sustainability Standards Board (ISSB).\n\nIFRS published drafts of general ESG and specific environmental standards, with the expectation that final standards will follow in due course.\n\nCPA Canada can choose to adopt the standards as they are or adapt them to better reflect unique factors in Canada’s business and economic contexts.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 22}], "format": "plain", "slides": [22], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t12", "title": "The Disclosure Process – Press Releases", "body": "To provide timely information to external users and to limit the possibility of selective leakage of information, most public companies announce quarterly and annual earnings through a press release: a written public news announcement normally distributed to major news services as soon as the verified figures (audited for annual and reviewed for quarterly earnings) are available.\n\nThe announcements are sent electronically to the major print and electronic news services, which make them immediately available to subscribers.", "blocks": [{"body": "To provide timely information to external users and to limit the possibility of selective leakage of information, most public companies announce quarterly and annual earnings through a press release: a written public news announcement normally distributed to major news services as soon as the verified figures (audited for annual and reviewed for quarterly earnings) are available.\n\nThe announcements are sent electronically to the major print and electronic news services, which make them immediately available to subscribers.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 23}], "format": "plain", "slides": [23], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t13", "title": "Annual Reports", "body": "For privately held companies, annual reports are relatively simple documents.\n\nThey normally include the following:\n\nBasic financial statements: statement of earnings, statement of financial position, statement of changes in equity, and statement of cash flows.\n\nRelated notes.\n\nReport of independent accountants (auditor’s opinion) if the statements are audited.\n\nThe annual reports of public companies are significantly more elaborate, both because of additional reporting requirements imposed on these companies by securities commissions and because many companies use their annual reports as public relations tools to communicate non-accounting information to shareholders, customers, the press, and others.\n\nThe annual reports of public companies are normally split into two sections.\n\nNon-financial:\n\nIncludes a letter to shareholders from the chairperson and CEO, along with descriptions of the company’s management philosophy, products, successes (and occasionally failures), and exciting prospects and challenges for the future.\n\nFinancial:\n\nIncludes the core of the report. Securities regulators set minimum disclosure standards for the financial section of the annual reports of public companies.\n\nThe principal components of the financial section follow:\n\nSummarized financial data.\n\nManagement’s discussion and analysis (MD&A) of the company's financial condition and results of operations.\n\nReport of the independent accountants (Auditor’s Opinion) and the management report.\n\nThe basic financial statements.\n\nNotes to the financial statements.\n\nThe order of these components varies.", "blocks": [{"body": "For privately held companies, annual reports are relatively simple documents.\n\nThey normally include the following:\n\nBasic financial statements: statement of earnings, statement of financial position, statement of changes in equity, and statement of cash flows.\n\nRelated notes.\n\nReport of independent accountants (auditor’s opinion) if the statements are audited.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 24}, {"body": "The annual reports of public companies are significantly more elaborate, both because of additional reporting requirements imposed on these companies by securities commissions and because many companies use their annual reports as public relations tools to communicate non-accounting information to shareholders, customers, the press, and others.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 25}, {"body": "The annual reports of public companies are normally split into two sections.\n\nNon-financial:\n\nIncludes a letter to shareholders from the chairperson and CEO, along with descriptions of the company’s management philosophy, products, successes (and occasionally failures), and exciting prospects and challenges for the future.\n\nFinancial:\n\nIncludes the core of the report. Securities regulators set minimum disclosure standards for the financial section of the annual reports of public companies.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 26}, {"body": "The principal components of the financial section follow:\n\nSummarized financial data.\n\nManagement’s discussion and analysis (MD&A) of the company's financial condition and results of operations.\n\nReport of the independent accountants (Auditor’s Opinion) and the management report.\n\nThe basic financial statements.\n\nNotes to the financial statements.\n\nThe order of these components varies.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 27}], "format": "plain", "slides": [24, 25, 26, 27], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t14", "title": "Quarterly Reports", "body": "Quarterly reports normally begin with a short letter to shareholders.\n\nA condensed statement of earnings for the quarter, which often shows less detail than the annual statement of earnings, and a condensed statement of financial position dated at the end of the quarter. These condensed financial statements are not audited and so are marked “unaudited.”\n\nOften, the statement of cash flows, the statement of changes in equity, and some notes to the financial statements are omitted.", "blocks": [{"body": "Quarterly reports normally begin with a short letter to shareholders.\n\nA condensed statement of earnings for the quarter, which often shows less detail than the annual statement of earnings, and a condensed statement of financial position dated at the end of the quarter. These condensed financial statements are not audited and so are marked “unaudited.”\n\nOften, the statement of cash flows, the statement of changes in equity, and some notes to the financial statements are omitted.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 28}], "format": "plain", "slides": [28], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t15", "title": "Reports to Securities Commissions", "body": "Public companies must also file periodic reports with the OSC and other provincial securities commissions.\n\nThese reports include the annual report, quarterly reports, an annual information form, and an information circular.\n\nThe annual information form provides a more detailed description of the business.\n\nThe information circular is a legal document that is forwarded to the company’s shareholders prior to the annual general or special meeting of shareholders. The circular also provides compensation details for the CEO, CFO, and other key executives as well as information about the company’s corporate governance practices.", "blocks": [{"body": "Public companies must also file periodic reports with the OSC and other provincial securities commissions.\n\nThese reports include the annual report, quarterly reports, an annual information form, and an information circular.\n\nThe annual information form provides a more detailed description of the business.\n\nThe information circular is a legal document that is forwarded to the company’s shareholders prior to the annual general or special meeting of shareholders. The circular also provides compensation details for the CEO, CFO, and other key executives as well as information about the company’s corporate governance practices.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 29}], "format": "plain", "slides": [29], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t16", "title": "The Investment Decision", "body": "When considering an investment in shares, the investor should evaluate the future net earnings and growth potential of the business on the basis of three factors:\n\nEconomy-wide factors: The overall health of the economy has a direct impact on the performance of an individual business. Prudent investors must consider data such as the unemployment rate, the general inflation rate, and changes in interest rates.\n\nIndustry factors: Certain events have a major impact on each company within an industry but have only a minor impact on other companies outside the industry.\n\nIndividual company factors: To properly analyze a company, you should learn as much as you can about it. Good analysts do not rely solely on the information contained in the financial statements. They visit the company, buy its products, and read about it in the business press.", "blocks": [{"body": "When considering an investment in shares, the investor should evaluate the future net earnings and growth potential of the business on the basis of three factors:", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch12-image4.png", "alt": "The Investment Decision — original slide 30"}], "slide": 30}, {"body": "Economy-wide factors: The overall health of the economy has a direct impact on the performance of an individual business. Prudent investors must consider data such as the unemployment rate, the general inflation rate, and changes in interest rates.\n\nIndustry factors: Certain events have a major impact on each company within an industry but have only a minor impact on other companies outside the industry.\n\nIndividual company factors: To properly analyze a company, you should learn as much as you can about it. Good analysts do not rely solely on the information contained in the financial statements. They visit the company, buy its products, and read about it in the business press.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 31}], "format": "plain", "slides": [30, 31], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t17", "title": "Understanding A Company’s Strategy", "body": "Financial statement analysis involves more than just “crunching numbers.”\n\nBefore you start looking at numbers, you should know what you are looking for.\n\nWhile financial statements report on transactions, each of these transactions is the result of a company’s operating decisions as it implements its business strategy.\n\nBusinesses can earn a high rate of return by following different strategies. There are two fundamental strategies:\n\nProduct differentiation (or high-value): Under this strategy, companies offer products with unique benefits, such as high quality or unusual features or style.\n\nCost differentiation: Under this strategy, companies selling similar products attempt to operate more efficiently than their competitors, allowing them to offer lower prices to attract customers.", "blocks": [{"body": "Financial statement analysis involves more than just “crunching numbers.”\n\nBefore you start looking at numbers, you should know what you are looking for.\n\nWhile financial statements report on transactions, each of these transactions is the result of a company’s operating decisions as it implements its business strategy.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch12-image5.png", "alt": "Understanding A Company’s Strategy — original slide 32"}], "slide": 32}, {"body": "Businesses can earn a high rate of return by following different strategies. There are two fundamental strategies:\n\nProduct differentiation (or high-value): Under this strategy, companies offer products with unique benefits, such as high quality or unusual features or style.\n\nCost differentiation: Under this strategy, companies selling similar products attempt to operate more efficiently than their competitors, allowing them to offer lower prices to attract customers.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 33}], "format": "plain", "slides": [32, 33], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t18", "title": "Financial Statement Analysis", "body": "Financial results cannot be evaluated in isolation.\n\nTo properly analyze the information reported in financial statements, you must develop appropriate comparisons.\n\nThe task of finding appropriate benchmarks requires judgment, and it is not always an easy task.\n\nFor this reason, financial analysis is a sophisticated skill, not a mechanical process.\n\nThere are two types of benchmarks for making financial comparisons: time series and comparisons with other companies.\n\nComparing across time. In this type of analysis, which often is referred to as “time-series analysis,” information on a single company is compared over time.\n\nComparing across companies. In this type of analysis, which often is referred to as “cross-sectional analysis,” information for multiple companies is compared at a point in time or across time. By comparing a company with another one in the same line of business, an analyst can obtain better insight into its performance.", "blocks": [{"body": "Financial results cannot be evaluated in isolation.\n\nTo properly analyze the information reported in financial statements, you must develop appropriate comparisons.\n\nThe task of finding appropriate benchmarks requires judgment, and it is not always an easy task.\n\nFor this reason, financial analysis is a sophisticated skill, not a mechanical process.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 34}, {"body": "There are two types of benchmarks for making financial comparisons: time series and comparisons with other companies.\n\nComparing across time. In this type of analysis, which often is referred to as “time-series analysis,” information on a single company is compared over time.\n\nComparing across companies. In this type of analysis, which often is referred to as “cross-sectional analysis,” information for multiple companies is compared at a point in time or across time. By comparing a company with another one in the same line of business, an analyst can obtain better insight into its performance.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 35}], "format": "plain", "slides": [34, 35], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t19", "title": "Component Percentages, Trend Analysis, and Ratio Analysis", "body": "All financial analysts use various tools to analyze a company’s financial statements.\n\nThese tools include:\n\nComponent percentages,\n\nTrend analysis, and\n\nRatio analysis", "blocks": [{"body": "All financial analysts use various tools to analyze a company’s financial statements.\n\nThese tools include:\n\nComponent percentages,\n\nTrend analysis, and\n\nRatio analysis", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 36}], "format": "plain", "slides": [36], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t20", "title": "Component Percentages", "body": "A component percentage expresses each item on a financial statement as a percentage of a single base amount, the denominator of the ratio. The base amount on the statement of earnings is net sales.\n\nTo calculate component percentages on the statement of earnings, each amount reported is divided by net sales. The base amount on the statement of financial position is total assets.\n\nTo calculate component percentages on the statement of financial position, each amount is divided by total assets. This is also known as vertical analysis or creating a common-size financial statement.", "blocks": [{"body": "A component percentage expresses each item on a financial statement as a percentage of a single base amount, the denominator of the ratio. The base amount on the statement of earnings is net sales.\n\nTo calculate component percentages on the statement of earnings, each amount reported is divided by net sales. The base amount on the statement of financial position is total assets.\n\nTo calculate component percentages on the statement of financial position, each amount is divided by total assets. This is also known as vertical analysis or creating a common-size financial statement.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 37}], "format": "plain", "slides": [37], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t21", "title": "Trend Analysis and Ratio Analysis", "body": "Trend analysis (or horizontal analysis) examines the dollar amounts or changes in the reported values of specific financial statement items over multiple periods to identify patterns that may be useful in predicting future values.\n\nRatios may be computed by using amounts in one statement, such as the statement of earnings, or in two different statements, such as the statement of earnings and the statement of financial position.", "blocks": [{"body": "Trend analysis (or horizontal analysis) examines the dollar amounts or changes in the reported values of specific financial statement items over multiple periods to identify patterns that may be useful in predicting future values.\n\nRatios may be computed by using amounts in one statement, such as the statement of earnings, or in two different statements, such as the statement of earnings and the statement of financial position.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 38}], "format": "plain", "slides": [38], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t22", "title": "Trend Analysis", "body": "Another type of analysis tracks the changes to specific line items in financial statements over time for the purpose of identifying significant changes that may be used in predicting future values.\n\nTrend analysis helps in identifying significant changes over time and in raising questions about the underlying reasons for such changes. It is important to remember, however, that changes that appear to be significant in percentage terms may not be significant in dollar amount.", "blocks": [{"body": "Another type of analysis tracks the changes to specific line items in financial statements over time for the purpose of identifying significant changes that may be used in predicting future values.\n\nTrend analysis helps in identifying significant changes over time and in raising questions about the underlying reasons for such changes. It is important to remember, however, that changes that appear to be significant in percentage terms may not be significant in dollar amount.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch12-image6.png", "alt": "Trend Analysis — original slide 39"}], "slide": 39}], "format": "plain", "slides": [39], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t23", "title": "Ratio Analysis", "body": "Component percentages are a type of ratio.\n\nRatios simply express one number relative to another number.\n\nWith component percentages, both the numerator and the denominator of the ratio come from the same financial statement. This does not have to be the case.\n\nRatio analysis helps decision makers identify significant relationships and make meaningful comparisons.\n\nA basic fact about financial statements: The statement of financial position amounts are as at a specific point in time while statement of earnings amounts relate to a period of time.\n\nTo adjust for this difference, analysts often use the average statement of financial position amount when comparing a statement of financial position amount to a statement of earnings amount.\n\nMaking this adjustment allows you to compare the average statement of financial position amount that corresponds to the period for which the statement of earnings amount was generated.", "blocks": [{"body": "Component percentages are a type of ratio.\n\nRatios simply express one number relative to another number.\n\nWith component percentages, both the numerator and the denominator of the ratio come from the same financial statement. This does not have to be the case.\n\nRatio analysis helps decision makers identify significant relationships and make meaningful comparisons.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 40}, {"body": "A basic fact about financial statements: The statement of financial position amounts are as at a specific point in time while statement of earnings amounts relate to a period of time.\n\nTo adjust for this difference, analysts often use the average statement of financial position amount when comparing a statement of financial position amount to a statement of earnings amount.\n\nMaking this adjustment allows you to compare the average statement of financial position amount that corresponds to the period for which the statement of earnings amount was generated.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 41}], "format": "plain", "slides": [40, 41], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t24", "title": "Widely Used Accounting Ratios", "body": "\n\n\n\n\n\n", "blocks": [{"body": "", "tables": [[["", "", ""], ["Profitability Ratios", "", "BASIC COMPUTATION"], ["1. Return on equity (ROE)", "Chapter 4", "Net earnings "], ["", "", "Net earnings Average shareholders ’ equity"], ["2. Return on assets (ROA)", "Chapter 3", "Net earnings"], ["", "", "Average total assets"], ["3. Gross profit margin ratio", "Chapter 5", "Gross profit"], ["", "", "Net sales"], ["4. Net profit margin ratio", "Chapter 4", "Net earnings "], ["", "", "Net sales"], ["5. Earnings per share (EPS) ratio", "Chapter 10", "Net earnings available to common shareholders"], ["", "", "Average number of common shares outstanding"], ["6. Quality of earnings ratio", "Chapter 11", "Cash flows from operating activities "], ["", "", "Net earnings"]]], "images": [], "slide": 42}, {"body": "", "tables": [[["Asset Turnover Ratios", "", "BASIC COMPUTATION"], ["7. Total asset turnover ratio", "Chapter 3", "Net sales"], ["", "", "Average total assets"], ["8. Fixed asset turnover ratio", "Chapter 7", "Net sales "], ["", "", "Average net fixed assets"], ["9. Receivables turnover ratio", "Chapter 5", "Net credit sales"], ["", "", "Average net accounts receivable"], ["10. Inventory turnover ratio", "Chapter 6", "Cost of sales"], ["", "", "Average inventory"]]], "images": [], "slide": 43}, {"body": "", "tables": [[["Liquidity Ratios", "", "BASIC COMPUTATION"], ["11. Current ratio", "Chapter 2", "Current assets"], ["", "", "Current liabilities"], ["12. Quick ratio", "Chapter 8", "Quick assets "], ["", "", "Current liabilities"], ["13. Cash ratio", "Chapter 12", "Cash + Cash equivalents "], ["", "", "Current liabilities"], ["", "", ""], ["Solvency Ratios", "", "BASIC COMPUTATION"], ["14. Times interest earned ratio", "Chapter 9", "Net earnings + Interest expense + Income tax expense"], ["", "", " Interest expense"], ["15. Cash coverage ratio", "Chapter 12", "Cash flows from operating activities (before interest and taxes) "], ["", "", "Interest paid"], ["16. Debt-to-equity ratio", "Chapter 9", "Total liabilities "], ["", "", "Shareholders ’ equity"]]], "images": [], "slide": 44}, {"body": "", "tables": [[["Market Ratios", "", "BASIC COMPUTATION"], ["17. Price/earnings ratio", "Chapter 12", "Current market price per share"], ["", "", " Earnings per share"], ["18. Dividend yield ratio", "Chapter 10", "Dividends per share"], ["", "", "Market price per share"]]], "images": [], "slide": 45}], "format": "plain", "slides": [42, 43, 44, 45], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t25", "title": "Profitability Ratios", "body": "Profitability is a primary measure of the overall success of a company. It is necessary for a company’s survival.\n\nInvestors and creditors prefer a single measure of profitability that is meaningful in all situations. However, there is no single measure that can meet this need.\n\nSeveral profitability ratios focus on measuring the adequacy of net earnings by comparing it to other items reported on the financial statements.", "blocks": [{"body": "Profitability is a primary measure of the overall success of a company. It is necessary for a company’s survival.\n\nInvestors and creditors prefer a single measure of profitability that is meaningful in all situations. However, there is no single measure that can meet this need.\n\nSeveral profitability ratios focus on measuring the adequacy of net earnings by comparing it to other items reported on the financial statements.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 46}], "format": "plain", "slides": [46], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t26", "title": "1. Return on Equity (ROE)", "body": "Return on equity (also called return on owners’ investment) relates net earnings to the investment made by the owners.\n\nIt reflects the simple fact that investors expect to earn a return on the money they invest in a company.\n\nNet Earnings\n\nAverage Shareholders’ Equity\n\nReturn on Equity\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "Return on equity (also called return on owners’ investment) relates net earnings to the investment made by the owners.\n\nIt reflects the simple fact that investors expect to earn a return on the money they invest in a company.\n\nNet Earnings\n\nAverage Shareholders’ Equity\n\nReturn on Equity\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 47}], "format": "plain", "slides": [47], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t27", "title": "2. Return on Assets (ROA)", "body": "Another test of profitability compares net earnings to the total assets (i.e., total investment) used to generate the net earnings. The ROA is computed as follows:\n\nNet Earnings\n\nAverage Total Assets\n\nReturn on Assets\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "Another test of profitability compares net earnings to the total assets (i.e., total investment) used to generate the net earnings. The ROA is computed as follows:\n\nNet Earnings\n\nAverage Total Assets\n\nReturn on Assets\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 48}], "format": "plain", "slides": [48], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t28", "title": "3. Gross Profit Margin Ratio", "body": "The gross profit margin ratio (also referred to as the gross profit percentage) reflects gross profit as a percent of sales.\n\nIf not shown separately on a company’s statement of earnings, gross profit is computed by subtracting cost of sales from net sales.\n\nThe gross profit margin ratio is computed as follows:\n\nGross Profit\n\nNet Sales\n\nGross Profit Margin Ratio\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "The gross profit margin ratio (also referred to as the gross profit percentage) reflects gross profit as a percent of sales.\n\nIf not shown separately on a company’s statement of earnings, gross profit is computed by subtracting cost of sales from net sales.\n\nThe gross profit margin ratio is computed as follows:\n\nGross Profit\n\nNet Sales\n\nGross Profit Margin Ratio\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 49}], "format": "plain", "slides": [49], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t29", "title": "4. Net Profit Margin Ratio", "body": "The net profit margin ratio measures the percentage of each sales dollar, on average, that represents net earnings. It is computed as follows:\n\nNet Earnings\n\nNet Sales\n\nNet Profit Margin Ratio\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "The net profit margin ratio measures the percentage of each sales dollar, on average, that represents net earnings. It is computed as follows:\n\nNet Earnings\n\nNet Sales\n\nNet Profit Margin Ratio\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 50}], "format": "plain", "slides": [50], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t30", "title": "5. Earnings per Share (EPS)", "body": "Earnings per share (EPS) is a measure of the return on investment that is based on the number of shares outstanding instead of the dollar amounts reported on the statement of financial position. In simple situations, EPS is computed as follows:\n\nThis calculation of EPS is based on information reported on the statement of earnings. The additional complexities in the calculation of EPS are discussed in advanced accounting courses.\n\nNet earnings available to common shareholders\n\nAverage number of common shares outstanding\n\nEPS =", "blocks": [{"body": "Earnings per share (EPS) is a measure of the return on investment that is based on the number of shares outstanding instead of the dollar amounts reported on the statement of financial position. In simple situations, EPS is computed as follows:\n\nThis calculation of EPS is based on information reported on the statement of earnings. The additional complexities in the calculation of EPS are discussed in advanced accounting courses.\n\nNet earnings available to common shareholders\n\nAverage number of common shares outstanding\n\nEPS =", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 51}], "format": "plain", "slides": [51], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t31", "title": "6. Quality of Earnings Ratio", "body": "Most financial analysts are concerned about the quality of a company’s earnings because the use of some accounting procedures can result in higher earnings numbers. A ratio higher than 1 indicates high-quality earnings.\n\nCash flows from operating activities\n\nQuality of earnings\n\nratio\n\nNet earnings\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "Most financial analysts are concerned about the quality of a company’s earnings because the use of some accounting procedures can result in higher earnings numbers. A ratio higher than 1 indicates high-quality earnings.\n\nCash flows from operating activities\n\nQuality of earnings\n\nratio\n\nNet earnings\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 52}], "format": "plain", "slides": [52], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t32", "title": "Asset Turnover Ratios", "body": "Asset turnover ratios focus on capturing how efficiently a company uses its assets.\n\nFor example, acquiring and selling inventory is a key activity for many companies. The inventory turnover ratio helps analysts evaluate a company’s ability to sell its inventory.\n\nAnother example is the receivables turnover ratio, which helps analysts evaluate a company’s ability to collect its receivables.\n\nThese and other asset turnover ratios are discussed below.", "blocks": [{"body": "Asset turnover ratios focus on capturing how efficiently a company uses its assets.\n\nFor example, acquiring and selling inventory is a key activity for many companies. The inventory turnover ratio helps analysts evaluate a company’s ability to sell its inventory.\n\nAnother example is the receivables turnover ratio, which helps analysts evaluate a company’s ability to collect its receivables.\n\nThese and other asset turnover ratios are discussed below.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 53}], "format": "plain", "slides": [53], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t33", "title": "7. Total Asset Turnover Ratio", "body": "The total asset turnover ratio captures how well a company uses its assets to generate revenue. The ratio is computed as follows:\n\nTotal AssetTurnover\n\nNet SalesAverage Total Assets\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "The total asset turnover ratio captures how well a company uses its assets to generate revenue. The ratio is computed as follows:\n\nTotal AssetTurnover\n\nNet SalesAverage Total Assets\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 54}], "format": "plain", "slides": [54], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t34", "title": "8. Fixed Asset Turnover Ratio", "body": "The fixed asset turnover ratio focuses more narrowly on how well a company uses its fixed assets to generate revenues.\n\nThe term fixed assets is synonymous with property, plant, and equipment.\n\nThe ratio is computed as follows:\n\nFixed AssetTurnover Ratio\n\nNet SalesAverage Net Fixed Assets\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "The fixed asset turnover ratio focuses more narrowly on how well a company uses its fixed assets to generate revenues.\n\nThe term fixed assets is synonymous with property, plant, and equipment.\n\nThe ratio is computed as follows:\n\nFixed AssetTurnover Ratio\n\nNet SalesAverage Net Fixed Assets\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 55}], "format": "plain", "slides": [55], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t35", "title": "9. Receivables Turnover Ratio", "body": "This ratio measures how quickly a company collects its trade receivables.\n\nThe receivables turnover ratio is often converted to a time basis known as the average days to collect receivables or average collection period.\n\nNet Credit Sales\n\nAverage Net Accounts Receivable\n\nReceivables Turnover Ratio\n\n=\n\nDays in a year\n\nReceivables turnover ratio\n\nAverage days to collect Receivables\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "This ratio measures how quickly a company collects its trade receivables.\n\nThe receivables turnover ratio is often converted to a time basis known as the average days to collect receivables or average collection period.\n\nNet Credit Sales\n\nAverage Net Accounts Receivable\n\nReceivables Turnover Ratio\n\n=\n\nDays in a year\n\nReceivables turnover ratio\n\nAverage days to collect Receivables\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 56}], "format": "plain", "slides": [56], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t36", "title": "10. Inventory Turnover Ratio", "body": "The inventory turnover ratio is a measure of operating efficiency.\n\nIt reflects the relationship of inventory to the amount of sales during the period.\n\nIt is computed as follows:\n\nCost of Sales\n\nAverage Inventory\n\nInventory Turnover Ratio\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "The inventory turnover ratio is a measure of operating efficiency.\n\nIt reflects the relationship of inventory to the amount of sales during the period.\n\nIt is computed as follows:\n\nCost of Sales\n\nAverage Inventory\n\nInventory Turnover Ratio\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 57}], "format": "plain", "slides": [57], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t37", "title": "Using Ratios to Analyze the Operating Cycle", "body": "The operating cycle is the time it takes for a company to pay cash to its suppliers, sell goods to its customers, and collect cash from its customers.\n\nAnalysts are interested in the operating cycle because it helps them evaluate a company’s cash needs and is a good indicator of operating efficiency.\n\nThe operating cycle for most companies involves four distinct phases: acquisition of inventory, payment of cash to suppliers, sale of inventory, and collection of cash from customers.\n\nWe have discussed three ratios that are helpful in evaluating a company’s operating cycle. They are the accounts payable turnover ratio, the inventory turnover ratio, and the receivables turnover ratio.\n\n", "blocks": [{"body": "The operating cycle is the time it takes for a company to pay cash to its suppliers, sell goods to its customers, and collect cash from its customers.\n\nAnalysts are interested in the operating cycle because it helps them evaluate a company’s cash needs and is a good indicator of operating efficiency.\n\nThe operating cycle for most companies involves four distinct phases: acquisition of inventory, payment of cash to suppliers, sale of inventory, and collection of cash from customers.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 58}, {"body": "We have discussed three ratios that are helpful in evaluating a company’s operating cycle. They are the accounts payable turnover ratio, the inventory turnover ratio, and the receivables turnover ratio.", "tables": [[["Ratio", "Operating Activity"], ["Accounts payable turnover ratio", "Purchase of inventory"], ["Inventory turnover ratio", "Sale of inventory"], ["Receivables turnover ratio", "Collection of cash from customers"]]], "images": [], "slide": 59}, {"body": "", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch12-image7.png", "alt": "Using Ratios to Analyze the Operating Cycle — original slide 60"}], "slide": 60}], "format": "plain", "slides": [58, 59, 60], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t38", "title": "ROA Profit Driver Analysis and the Dupont Model", "body": "The ROA profit driver analysis is a useful framework for evaluating a company’s profit drivers.\n\nBreaking down ROA into its two components provides a better understanding of what underlies a company’s performance. With this knowledge, one can better assess whether a company is effectively implementing its business strategy.\n\nSome analysts include a third component in their analysis: financial leverage.\n\nWhen financial leverage is added to the model, it is commonly referred to as the DuPont model.\n\nA key insight provided by the DuPont model is that companies can be profitable by achieving high profit margins; by achieving a rapid turnover of assets; by increasing reliance on debt relative to equity in their capital structure; or by some combination of these strategies.", "blocks": [{"body": "The ROA profit driver analysis is a useful framework for evaluating a company’s profit drivers.\n\nBreaking down ROA into its two components provides a better understanding of what underlies a company’s performance. With this knowledge, one can better assess whether a company is effectively implementing its business strategy.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch12-image8.png", "alt": "ROA Profit Driver Analysis and the Dupont Model — original slide 61"}], "slide": 61}, {"body": "Some analysts include a third component in their analysis: financial leverage.\n\nWhen financial leverage is added to the model, it is commonly referred to as the DuPont model.", "tables": [], "images": [{"src": "sources/images/ch12-image9.png", "alt": "ROA Profit Driver Analysis and the Dupont Model — original slide 62"}], "slide": 62}, {"body": "A key insight provided by the DuPont model is that companies can be profitable by achieving high profit margins; by achieving a rapid turnover of assets; by increasing reliance on debt relative to equity in their capital structure; or by some combination of these strategies.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 63}], "format": "plain", "slides": [61, 62, 63], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t39", "title": "Liquidity Ratios", "body": "Liquidity refers to a company’s ability to meet its short-term obligations.\n\nBecause most short-term obligations are paid with current assets, liquidity ratios focus on the relationship between current assets and current liabilities.\n\nA company’s ability to pay its current liabilities is an important factor in evaluating its short-term financial strength.\n\nThree ratios are used to measure liquidity:\n\nThe current ratio\n\nThe quick ratio\n\nThe cash ratio", "blocks": [{"body": "Liquidity refers to a company’s ability to meet its short-term obligations.\n\nBecause most short-term obligations are paid with current assets, liquidity ratios focus on the relationship between current assets and current liabilities.\n\nA company’s ability to pay its current liabilities is an important factor in evaluating its short-term financial strength.\n\nThree ratios are used to measure liquidity:\n\nThe current ratio\n\nThe quick ratio\n\nThe cash ratio", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 64}], "format": "plain", "slides": [64], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t40", "title": "11. Current Ratio", "body": "The current ratio measures the relationship between current assets and current liabilities at a specific date.\n\nIt measures the ability of the company to pay current debts as they become due.\n\nCurrent\n\nRatio\n\nCurrent Assets\n\nCurrent Liabilities\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "The current ratio measures the relationship between current assets and current liabilities at a specific date.\n\nIt measures the ability of the company to pay current debts as they become due.\n\nCurrent\n\nRatio\n\nCurrent Assets\n\nCurrent Liabilities\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 65}], "format": "plain", "slides": [65], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t41", "title": "12. Quick Ratio", "body": "The quick ratio, sometimes referred to as the acid test, is a more stringent test of short-term liquidity than the current ratio.\n\nThe quick ratio compares quick assets, defined as cash and near-cash assets, to current liabilities.\n\nQuick Assets\n\nCurrent Liabilities\n\n=\n\nQuick\n\nRatio", "blocks": [{"body": "The quick ratio, sometimes referred to as the acid test, is a more stringent test of short-term liquidity than the current ratio.\n\nThe quick ratio compares quick assets, defined as cash and near-cash assets, to current liabilities.\n\nQuick Assets\n\nCurrent Liabilities\n\n=\n\nQuick\n\nRatio", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 66}], "format": "plain", "slides": [66], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t42", "title": "13. Cash Ratio", "body": "This ratio measures the adequacy of available cash.\n\nCash Ratio\n\nCash + Cash Equivalents\n\nCurrent Liabilities\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "This ratio measures the adequacy of available cash.\n\nCash Ratio\n\nCash + Cash Equivalents\n\nCurrent Liabilities\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 67}], "format": "plain", "slides": [67], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t43", "title": "Solvency Ratios", "body": "Solvency refers to a company’s ability to meet its long-term obligations.\n\nSolvency ratios measure this ability; examples are:\n\nTimes interest earned\n\nCash coverage\n\nDebt-to-equity ratios", "blocks": [{"body": "Solvency refers to a company’s ability to meet its long-term obligations.\n\nSolvency ratios measure this ability; examples are:\n\nTimes interest earned\n\nCash coverage\n\nDebt-to-equity ratios", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 68}], "format": "plain", "slides": [68], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t44", "title": "14. Times Interest Earned", "body": "This ratio indicates a margin of protection for creditors.\n\nNet Earnings     Interest       Income Tax                            Expense        Expense                   Interest Expense\n\nTimes Interest Earned Ratio\n\n=\n\n+\n\n+", "blocks": [{"body": "This ratio indicates a margin of protection for creditors.\n\nNet Earnings     Interest       Income Tax                            Expense        Expense                   Interest Expense\n\nTimes Interest Earned Ratio\n\n=\n\n+\n\n+", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 69}], "format": "plain", "slides": [69], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t45", "title": "15. Cash Coverage Ratio", "body": "This ratio compares the cash generated with the cash obligations of the period.\n\nCashCoverage\n\nRatio\n\nCash Flow from Operating ActivitiesBefore Interest and TaxesInterest Paid (from statement of cash flows)\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "This ratio compares the cash generated with the cash obligations of the period.\n\nCashCoverage\n\nRatio\n\nCash Flow from Operating ActivitiesBefore Interest and TaxesInterest Paid (from statement of cash flows)\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 70}], "format": "plain", "slides": [70], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t46", "title": "16. Debt-to-Equity Ratio", "body": "This ratio expresses a company’s debt as a proportion of its shareholders’ equity.\n\nIt is computed as follows:\n\nTotal Liabilities\n\nShareholders’ Equity\n\nDebt-to-Equity Ratio\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "This ratio expresses a company’s debt as a proportion of its shareholders’ equity.\n\nIt is computed as follows:\n\nTotal Liabilities\n\nShareholders’ Equity\n\nDebt-to-Equity Ratio\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 71}], "format": "plain", "slides": [71], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t47", "title": "Market Ratios", "body": "Some ratios, often called market ratios, relate the current market price per share to the return that accrues to shareholders.\n\nMany analysts prefer these ratios because they are based on the current value of an owner’s investment in a company.", "blocks": [{"body": "Some ratios, often called market ratios, relate the current market price per share to the return that accrues to shareholders.\n\nMany analysts prefer these ratios because they are based on the current value of an owner’s investment in a company.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 72}], "format": "plain", "slides": [72], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t48", "title": "17. Price/Earnings (P/E) Ratio", "body": "This ratio measures the relationship between the current market price of the share and its earnings per share.\n\nPrice/Earnings\n\nRatio\n\nCurrent Market Price Per ShareEarnings Per Share\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "This ratio measures the relationship between the current market price of the share and its earnings per share.\n\nPrice/Earnings\n\nRatio\n\nCurrent Market Price Per ShareEarnings Per Share\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 73}], "format": "plain", "slides": [73], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t49", "title": "18. Dividend Yield Ratio", "body": "When investors buy shares, they expect returns from two sources: dividend income and price appreciation.\n\nThe dividend yield ratio measures the relationship between the dividend per share paid to shareholders and the current market price per share.\n\nDividend\n\nYield Ratio\n\nDividends Per Share\n\nMarket Price Per Share\n\n=", "blocks": [{"body": "When investors buy shares, they expect returns from two sources: dividend income and price appreciation.\n\nThe dividend yield ratio measures the relationship between the dividend per share paid to shareholders and the current market price per share.\n\nDividend\n\nYield Ratio\n\nDividends Per Share\n\nMarket Price Per Share\n\n=", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 74}], "format": "plain", "slides": [74], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t50", "title": "Interpreting Ratios and Other Analytical Considerations", "body": "Ratios may be interpreted by comparison with ratios of other companies or with industry average ratios.\n\nRatios may vary because of the company’s industry characteristics, nature of operations, size, and accounting policies.", "blocks": [{"body": "Ratios may be interpreted by comparison with ratios of other companies or with industry average ratios.\n\nRatios may vary because of the company’s industry characteristics, nature of operations, size, and accounting policies.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 75}], "format": "plain", "slides": [75], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}, {"id": "c12-t51", "title": "Other Financial Information", "body": "In addition to financial ratios, special factors might affect company analysis:\n\nRapid growth.\n\nUneconomical expansion.\n\nNon-financial information.\n\nNo single approach can be used to analyze all companies.\n\nAn effective analyst will look beyond the information contained in an annual report.", "blocks": [{"body": "In addition to financial ratios, special factors might affect company analysis:\n\nRapid growth.\n\nUneconomical expansion.\n\nNon-financial information.\n\nNo single approach can be used to analyze all companies.\n\nAn effective analyst will look beyond the information contained in an annual report.", "tables": [], "images": [], "slide": 76}], "format": "plain", "slides": [76], "source": "Libby · Financial Accounting, Eighth Canadian Edition · Chapter 12 slides"}], "deck": "sources/chapter-12.pptx"}];
